B.Ww=s(["Disease-modifying therapies","High-dose IV methylprednisolone (3-5 days)","Permanent escalation of disease-modifying therapy","Observation only"],t.s)
B.aaW=new A.e("Acute MS relapses are treated with:",B.Ww,1,"Acute MS relapses: high-dose IV methylprednisolone (typically 1g/day for 3-5 days) with or without oral taper. This shortens relapse duration but does not change long-term outcome. Plasma exchange (plasmapheresis) is second-line for severe relapses unresponsive to steroids.")
B.VO=s(["Bed rest only","Energy conservation strategies, exercise, and amantadine or modafinil","High-dose caffeine","Opioid medications"],t.s)
B.a6v=new A.e("Fatigue in MS is best managed with:",B.VO,1,"MS fatigue management: energy conservation (prioritize tasks, take scheduled rest breaks), regular exercise (shown to reduce fatigue), cooling strategies, and medications (amantadine, modafinil/armodafinil, or methylphenidate). Treating contributing factors (depression, sleep disorders, medications) is also important.")
B.PM=s(["Surgical intervention","Baclofen or tizanidine combined with stretching","High-dose steroids","Chemotherapy"],t.s)
B.a5C=new A.e("Spasticity in MS is initially treated with:",B.PM,1,"MS spasticity: stretching program (most important), oral baclofen (GABA-B agonist, first-line) or tizanidine (alpha-2 agonist). Focal spasticity: botulinum toxin. Severe generalized: intrathecal baclofen pump. Cannabis-based medications (nabiximols) are approved in some countries.")
B.Xq=s(["Myasthenia gravis","Multiple sclerosis","ALS","Parkinson disease"],t.s)
B.a8n=new A.e("Internuclear ophthalmoplegia (INO) in a young patient is highly suggestive of:",B.Xq,1,"INO (impaired adduction of the ipsilateral eye with nystagmus of the abducting eye on lateral gaze) results from a lesion in the medial longitudinal fasciculus. In a young patient, bilateral INO is virtually pathognomonic for MS. In elderly patients, it is more commonly due to vascular disease.")
B.VZ=s(["Stress incontinence","Detrusor hyperreflexia (overactive bladder with urgency and frequency)","Complete urinary retention","Normal bladder function throughout disease"],t.s)
B.a3T=new A.e("Bladder dysfunction in MS most commonly presents as:",B.VZ,1,"Detrusor hyperreflexia (UMN bladder pattern) is the most common bladder dysfunction in MS, causing urgency, frequency, and urge incontinence. Detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia can also occur. Urodynamic testing guides management. First-line: anticholinergics (oxybutynin) for urgency; intermittent catheterization if retention or high residuals.")
B.Zb=s([B.aaW,B.a6v,B.a5C,B.a8n,B.a3T],t.b)
B.Ik=new A.a6("Multiple Sclerosis 2",B.Zb)
B.Pw=s(["Beta-amyloid","Alpha-synuclein","Hyperphosphorylated tau","Prion protein"],t.s)
B.a5l=new A.e("Chronic traumatic encephalopathy (CTE) is associated with accumulation of which protein?",B.Pw,2,"CTE is associated with accumulation of hyperphosphorylated tau protein in a pattern distinct from Alzheimer's disease. It can only be definitively diagnosed post-mortem. Associated with repetitive head trauma (contact sports, military blast exposure). Symptoms include behavioral changes, cognitive decline, and depression.")
B.Wq=s(["1 to 5","1 to 8","0 to 15","0 to 100"],t.s)
B.abx=new A.e("The Glasgow Outcome Scale-Extended (GOS-E) ranges from:",B.Wq,1,"GOS-E: 1 = death, 2 = vegetative state, 3-4 = severe disability (lower/upper), 5-6 = moderate disability (lower/upper), 7-8 = good recovery (lower/upper). It is the most commonly used outcome measure in TBI clinical trials. The original GOS had 5 categories.")
B.RR=s(["24 hours","48 hours","At least 1 week (completing all 6 graduated steps)","1 month"],t.s)
B.a3L=new A.e("Return-to-play after concussion follows a stepwise protocol. What is the minimum time before return to full contact?",B.RR,2,"The graduated return-to-play protocol has 6 steps, each requiring at least 24 hours: (1) symptom-limited activity, (2) light aerobic exercise, (3) sport-specific exercise, (4) non-contact drills, (5) full-contact practice, (6) game play. Minimum 1 week if all steps completed without symptom recurrence.")
B.NX=s(["Areflexic (flaccid) bladder","Uninhibited (hyperreflexic) neurogenic bladder","Detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia","Normal bladder function"],t.s)
B.a6N=new A.e("Neurogenic bladder after TBI most commonly presents as:",B.NX,1,"TBI most commonly causes an uninhibited neurogenic bladder \u2014 the frontal lobe inhibition of the micturition reflex is lost, resulting in urinary urgency, frequency, and incontinence. This is an upper motor neuron pattern. Timed voiding and anticholinergic medications are first-line treatments.")
B.LY=s(["Surgical and pharmacological","Restorative (retraining) and compensatory (adaptive strategies)","Physical and occupational therapy only","Medication and restraint"],t.s)
B.a73=new A.e("Cognitive rehabilitation after TBI focuses on which two main approaches?",B.LY,1,"Cognitive rehab uses restorative approaches (repetitive practice to improve the impaired function) and compensatory strategies (external aids \u2014 memory books, alarms, checklists \u2014 to work around persistent deficits). Evidence supports both, with compensatory strategies showing the strongest evidence for functional improvement.")
B.SX=s([B.a5l,B.abx,B.a3L,B.a6N,B.a73],t.b)
B.Il=new A.a6("Traumatic Brain Injury 6",B.SX)
B.Ox=s(["5","8","10","12"],t.s)
B.a3Z=new A.e("The Rancho Los Amigos Cognitive Scale has how many levels?",B.Ox,2,"The Rancho Los Amigos Scale has 10 levels (originally 8, revised to 10). Level I = no response, Level IV = confused-agitated (most challenging for rehab staff), Level VIII = purposeful-appropriate, Levels IX-X = added in revised version for community reintegration.")
B.We=s(["No response to stimuli","Confused and agitated","Purposeful and appropriate","Localized response to stimuli"],t.s)
B.a5P=new A.e("A patient at Rancho Level IV is described as:",B.We,1,"Rancho IV = confused-agitated. The patient is alert but confused, combative, may exhibit bizarre behavior, requires maximal assistance. Management focuses on safety, environmental modification (low stimulation), and redirecting rather than restraining. Agitation is self-limiting.")
B.QD=s(["Knee","Shoulder","Hip","Elbow"],t.s)
B.a4V=new A.e("Heterotopic ossification (HO) after TBI most commonly occurs around which joint?",B.QD,2,"The hip is the most common site for HO after TBI, followed by the elbow, shoulder, and knee. Risk factors include prolonged coma, spasticity, and prolonged immobilization. Early signs: decreased ROM, swelling, warmth. Triple-phase bone scan is the most sensitive early diagnostic test.")
B.TY=s(["0 (no disability) to 29 (extreme vegetative state/death)","1 to 7","0 to 100","3 to 15"],t.s)
B.a7H=new A.e("The Disability Rating Scale (DRS) for TBI ranges from:",B.TY,0,"The DRS ranges from 0 (no disability) to 29 (extreme vegetative state) to 30 (death). It assesses arousability, awareness, cognitive ability for self-care, dependence on others, and employability. It is more sensitive to change than the GCS for rehabilitation outcomes.")
B.ZU=s(["Late post-traumatic seizures","Early post-traumatic seizures","Status epilepticus","Non-epileptic events"],t.s)
B.aa5=new A.e("Post-traumatic seizures occurring within the first 7 days are classified as:",B.ZU,1,"Early seizures occur within 7 days of injury; late seizures occur after 7 days. Prophylactic antiepileptics (levetiracetam or phenytoin) are recommended for the first 7 days after severe TBI to prevent early seizures. They do NOT prevent late (post-traumatic) epilepsy.")
B.Vj=s([B.a3Z,B.a5P,B.a4V,B.a7H,B.aa5],t.b)
B.Im=new A.a6("Traumatic Brain Injury 2",B.Vj)
B.Xx=s(["0 to 15","3 to 15","1 to 10","0 to 12"],t.s)
B.aaK=new A.e("The Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) scores range from:",B.Xx,1,"GCS scores range from 3 (worst) to 15 (normal). Components: Eye opening (1-4), Verbal response (1-5), Motor response (1-6). Severe TBI = GCS 3-8, Moderate = 9-12, Mild = 13-15.")
B.NV=s(["Mild TBI","Moderate TBI","Severe TBI","Normal recovery expected"],t.s)
B.a6A=new A.e("Post-traumatic amnesia (PTA) duration is the best predictor of TBI outcome. PTA >24 hours indicates:",B.NV,2,"PTA duration predicts outcome: <1 hour = mild, 1-24 hours = moderate, >24 hours = severe, >7 days = very severe. The Galveston Orientation and Amnesia Test (GOAT) is used to track PTA resolution. PTA includes both retrograde and anterograde amnesia.")
B.Sp=s(["Bridging veins","Middle meningeal artery","Anterior cerebral artery","Superior sagittal sinus"],t.s)
B.a76=new A.e("An epidural hematoma is most commonly caused by rupture of which vessel?",B.Sp,1,"Epidural hematomas result from middle meningeal artery rupture, typically after temporal bone fracture. Classic presentation: brief loss of consciousness, lucid interval, then rapid deterioration. CT shows biconvex (lens-shaped) hyperdensity. This is a neurosurgical emergency.")
B.TG=s(["Middle meningeal artery","Bridging veins","Circle of Willis aneurysm","Cavernous sinus"],t.s)
B.a8X=new A.e("A subdural hematoma is most commonly caused by rupture of:",B.TG,1,"Subdural hematomas result from torn bridging veins between the cortex and dural sinuses. They are crescent-shaped on CT and follow the brain contour. Risk factors include advanced age, anticoagulation, and brain atrophy. Chronic subdurals can present weeks after minor trauma.")
B.Q4=s(["CT scan without contrast","Skull X-ray","MRI with diffusion-weighted imaging","Lumbar puncture"],t.s)
B.a4y=new A.e("Diffuse axonal injury (DAI) is best detected by:",B.Q4,2,"DAI results from rotational/shearing forces causing widespread axonal disruption. CT is often initially normal despite severe neurological deficits. MRI (especially DWI and SWI sequences) is far more sensitive, showing punctate hemorrhages at gray-white junctions, corpus callosum, and brainstem.")
B.WN=s([B.aaK,B.a6A,B.a76,B.a8X,B.a4y],t.b)
B.In=new A.a6("Traumatic Brain Injury 1",B.WN)
B.Vo=s(["Active runners","Low-activity level amputees who need a stable, durable foot","Bilateral transfemoral amputees","Pediatric patients who need adjustability"],t.s)
B.a7k=new A.e("A SACH (Solid Ankle Cushion Heel) foot is best for:",B.Vo,1,"SACH foot: simple, durable, low-maintenance, no moving parts. The compressible heel cushion simulates plantarflexion at heel strike. Best for K1-K2 level ambulators (household/limited community). Limitations: no energy return, no multi-axial motion, poor on uneven terrain.")
B.Z1=s(["K0 to K4","K1 to K5","K0 to K3","Level 1 to Level 10"],t.s)
B.a4F=new A.e("The Medicare Functional Classification Levels (K-levels) range from:",B.Z1,0,"K-levels: K0 = non-ambulatory (no prosthetic potential). K1 = household ambulator (limited). K2 = limited community ambulator (low-level variable cadence). K3 = community ambulator (variable cadence, most environmental barriers). K4 = exceeds basic ambulation (high-impact activities, sports). K-level determines which prosthetic components are covered.")
B.Ut=s(["Lower cost","Real-time adjustment of swing and stance resistance based on walking speed and terrain","Lighter weight","Simpler maintenance"],t.s)
B.a8M=new A.e("A microprocessor knee (e.g., C-Leg) provides which advantage over mechanical knees?",B.Ut,1,"Microprocessor knees use sensors and algorithms to continuously adjust hydraulic resistance during both stance (stumble recovery, stair descent) and swing (speed-responsive cadence). Benefits: reduced falls, improved confidence, better stair/ramp navigation, variable walking speed. Covered for K3-K4 level ambulators. More expensive and heavier than mechanical options.")
B.SN=s(["A socket that is too tight","Weak hip abductors on the amputated side (Trendelenburg)","An excessively long prosthesis","A knee that flexes too easily"],t.s)
B.abw=new A.e("A transfemoral amputee exhibiting lateral trunk lean toward the prosthetic side during stance likely has:",B.SN,1,"Lateral trunk lean (abductor lurch): compensatory lean toward the prosthetic side to place the center of gravity over the support base. Causes: weak hip abductors (most common), prosthesis too short, or lateral wall of socket too high/uncomfortable. If the prosthesis is too long, the patient vaults on the sound side instead.")
B.UN=s(["Suction (liner with valve)","Pin/shuttle lock liner","Supracondylar cuff","Harness with figure-8 strap"],t.s)
B.a7j=new A.e("Suspension methods for transtibial prostheses include all EXCEPT:",B.UN,3,"Transtibial suspension: suction (seal-in liner, most popular), pin/shuttle lock (pin on distal liner clicks into socket), supracondylar cuff/strap, sleeve suspension, and anatomic (supracondylar/suprapatellar). The figure-8 harness is used for transhumeral (above-elbow) UPPER extremity prostheses, not transtibial.")
B.W2=s([B.a7k,B.a4F,B.a8M,B.abw,B.a7j],t.b)
B.Io=new A.a6("Prosthetics & Orthotics 3",B.W2)
B.SY=s(["Internal intercostals","External intercostals","Diaphragm","Sternocleidomastoid"],t.s)
B.a8x=new A.e("The primary muscle of respiration is:",B.SY,2,"The diaphragm (innervated by the phrenic nerve, C3-C5) is responsible for ~70% of quiet breathing. When it contracts, it descends, creating negative intrathoracic pressure. Accessory muscles (SCM, scalenes, external intercostals) assist during exercise or respiratory distress.")
B.X1=s(["Reduced total lung capacity","Increased residual volume, decreased FEV1/FVC ratio, air trapping","Normal spirometry","Restrictive pattern on PFTs"],t.s)
B.abO=new A.e("Obstructive lung disease (COPD) is characterized by:",B.X1,1,"Obstructive disease (COPD, asthma, emphysema): airflow limitation during expiration. FEV1/FVC ratio <70% (hallmark). Air trapping increases residual volume and total lung capacity. The FEV1 is reduced disproportionately to FVC. Restrictive disease shows reduced TLC with preserved or increased FEV1/FVC ratio.")
B.SB=s(["Increasing oxygen intake","Creating back-pressure that stents open collapsed airways and prolongs expiration","Strengthening the diaphragm","Reducing heart rate"],t.s)
B.a6U=new A.e("Pursed-lip breathing in COPD patients helps by:",B.SB,1,"Pursed-lip breathing: expiring against partially closed lips creates positive back-pressure in the airways, preventing premature small airway collapse (which causes air trapping in emphysema). This prolongs expiration, improves CO2 elimination, reduces respiratory rate, and decreases dyspnea. One of the most effective self-management strategies.")
B.PY=s(["Maximum sprint speed","Submaximal functional exercise capacity (distance walked in 6 minutes)","Lung volumes","Maximum oxygen consumption"],t.s)
B.a6C=new A.e("The 6-minute walk test (6MWT) measures:",B.PY,1,"6MWT: practical, reliable measure of functional exercise capacity. The patient walks as far as possible in 6 minutes on a flat surface. Normal: ~400-700m depending on age/sex. A minimal clinically important difference (MCID) is ~30 meters. It correlates with VO2max, predicts mortality in COPD, and is used to monitor pulmonary rehab outcomes.")
B.Nm=s(["<80 mmHg","<55 mmHg (or <60 mmHg with cor pulmonale/polycythemia)",">90 mmHg","<70 mmHg"],t.s)
B.a3D=new A.e("Supplemental oxygen is indicated for COPD patients with resting PaO2:",B.Nm,1,"Long-term oxygen therapy (LTOT) criteria: PaO2 \u226455 mmHg or SaO2 \u226488% at rest, OR PaO2 56-59 mmHg with evidence of cor pulmonale, right heart failure, or polycythemia (Hct >55%). LTOT \u226515 hours/day has been shown to improve survival. Target SpO2 88-92% (avoid hyperoxia which can suppress respiratory drive).")
B.ZM=s([B.a8x,B.abO,B.a6U,B.a6C,B.a3D],t.b)
B.Ip=new A.a6("Pulmonary Rehab 1",B.ZM)
B.Qk=s(["Lateral curvature of the spine","Anterior wedging of \u22653 consecutive vertebral bodies by \u22655\xb0 each","Posterior element fractures","Disc herniation at multiple levels"],t.s)
B.a4R=new A.e("Scheuermann's disease is characterized by:",B.Qk,1,"Scheuermann's disease is a structural thoracic kyphosis caused by anterior vertebral body wedging (\u22655\xb0 in \u22653 consecutive vertebrae). It presents in adolescence and is distinguished from postural kyphosis by its rigidity \u2014 it does not correct with extension.")
B.SR=s(["L2-L3","L3-L4","L4-L5","L5-S1"],t.s)
B.a5D=new A.e("The most common location for a herniated nucleus pulposus in the lumbar spine is:",B.SR,3,"L5-S1 is the most common level for lumbar disc herniation, followed by L4-L5. Together these two levels account for ~95% of lumbar disc herniations. The L5-S1 disc compresses the S1 nerve root in posterolateral herniations.")
B.VI=s(["Age >50 with new onset back pain","Unexplained weight loss","Pain that worsens at night and is unrelieved by rest","Pain that improves with Valsalva maneuver"],t.s)
B.a3t=new A.e("Red flags in back pain that suggest malignancy include all EXCEPT:",B.VI,3,"Pain that WORSENS with Valsalva is concerning for disc herniation (increased intrathecal pressure). Red flags for malignancy: age >50, history of cancer, unexplained weight loss, night pain unrelieved by position change, and pain lasting >6 weeks despite conservative treatment.")
B.ZE=s(["Within the first 2 weeks of symptoms","After failed surgery","For subacute radicular symptoms (4-12 weeks)","For chronic low back pain without radiculopathy"],t.s)
B.a9C=new A.e("Epidural steroid injections for radiculopathy are most effective when performed:",B.ZE,2,"Epidural steroid injections are most effective for subacute radicular pain. They target the inflammatory component of nerve root compression. They are less effective for chronic pain, axial back pain without radiculopathy, or spinal stenosis.")
B.QL=s(["Annulus fibrosus","Nucleus pulposus","Vertebral end plate","Posterior longitudinal ligament"],t.s)
B.a5h=new A.e("Degenerative disc disease most commonly begins with dehydration of which structure?",B.QL,1,"Degenerative disc disease begins with dehydration of the nucleus pulposus (loss of proteoglycans and water content). This reduces disc height, alters load distribution, and leads to annular tears, facet joint overload, and segmental instability.")
B.S2=s([B.a4R,B.a5D,B.a3t,B.a9C,B.a5h],t.b)
B.Iq=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Spine 4",B.S2)
B.Ta=s(["It determines the speed of nerve conduction","High skin impedance degrades the recorded signal quality","It calculates the distance between electrodes","It measures the safety factor at the NMJ"],t.s)
B.abj=new A.e("In nerve conduction studies, Ohm's law (I = V/R) is important because:",B.Ta,1,"Ohm's law explains that high skin resistance (impedance) reduces the recorded signal. Proper skin preparation reduces impedance, improving signal-to-noise ratio. This is why skin abrasion is critical before electrode placement.")
B.Nr=s(["On the contralateral limb","Between the stimulating and recording electrodes","Directly over the motor point","As far from the nerve as possible"],t.s)
B.a3O=new A.e("The ground electrode in nerve conduction studies should be placed:",B.Nr,1,"The ground electrode must be placed between the stimulating and recording electrodes to intercept stimulus artifact. Incorrect placement allows artifact to contaminate the recorded signal.")
B.WS=s(["Adding G1 and G2 signals together","Subtracting G2 from G1 to cancel common noise","Amplifying only the G1 signal","Filtering out all signals below 100 Hz"],t.s)
B.aaS=new A.e("A differential amplifier in the EMG machine works by:",B.WS,1,"The differential amplifier subtracts G2 from G1 (G1 - G2), canceling common-mode noise (60 Hz interference, etc.) while preserving the biological signal. The common mode rejection ratio (CMRR) must be >90 dB.")
B.Zh=s(["20 Hz to 2 kHz","2 Hz to 10 kHz","20 Hz to 10 kHz","1 Hz to 5 kHz"],t.s)
B.ab_=new A.e("The standard low-frequency filter (LFF) and high-frequency filter (HFF) settings for motor NCS are:",B.Zh,1,"Motor NCS standard filters: LFF = 2 Hz, HFF = 10 kHz. Sensory NCS uses LFF = 20 Hz, HFF = 10 kHz. Raising the LFF artificially reduces CMAP amplitude; lowering the HFF artificially prolongs latency.")
B.M9=s(["5% above","10% above","20% above","50% above"],t.s)
B.a9p=new A.e("Supramaximal stimulation in NCS means stimulating at what intensity above the level that produces maximal response?",B.M9,2,"Supramaximal stimulation is 20% above the intensity that produces the maximal CMAP. This ensures all axons are activated and the response is reproducible. Submaximal stimulation underestimates amplitude.")
B.Pc=s([B.abj,B.a3O,B.aaS,B.ab_,B.a9p],t.b)
B.Ir=new A.a6("Electrodiagnostic 3",B.Pc)
B.Zp=s(["Young adults; lower extremities > upper","Elderly with cervical spondylosis; upper extremities > lower","Children; bilateral lower extremities","Athletes; unilateral weakness"],t.s)
B.a9R=new A.e("Central cord syndrome most commonly occurs in which population and affects:",B.Zp,1,"Central cord syndrome: most common incomplete SCI, typically in elderly patients with cervical spondylosis after hyperextension injury. Upper extremity weakness > lower extremity, with hand function most affected (central fibers serve arms). Bowel/bladder dysfunction is variable. Prognosis: legs recover first, then bladder, then arms, then hands.")
B.P_=s(["Bilateral motor and sensory loss","Ipsilateral motor loss and proprioception loss; contralateral pain/temperature loss","Complete sensory loss below the lesion","Isolated posterior column dysfunction"],t.s)
B.a6D=new A.e("Brown-S\xe9quard syndrome (hemisection) presents with:",B.P_,1,"Brown-S\xe9quard (cord hemisection): ipsilateral motor loss (corticospinal tract), ipsilateral proprioception/vibration loss (dorsal columns), and contralateral pain/temperature loss (spinothalamic tract crosses 1-2 levels above entry). It has the best prognosis of incomplete SCI syndromes.")
B.Zn=s(["Loss of proprioception with preserved motor function","Loss of motor function and pain/temperature with preserved proprioception and vibration","Complete loss of all function below the lesion","Isolated loss of temperature sensation"],t.s)
B.a7T=new A.e("Anterior cord syndrome is characterized by:",B.Zn,1,"Anterior cord syndrome: damage to the anterior 2/3 of the cord (corticospinal tracts and spinothalamic tracts) with preservation of the posterior columns (proprioception, vibration, deep pressure). It has the WORST prognosis of incomplete SCI syndromes. Common after anterior spinal artery occlusion.")
B.Rx=s(["Finger flexion","Wrist extension (tenodesis grasp)","Elbow extension","Hand intrinsic function"],t.s)
B.abq=new A.e("A C6 complete SCI patient gains which critical function compared to C5?",B.Rx,1,"C6 adds wrist extension (ECRL/ECRB). This enables the tenodesis grasp: wrist extension passively tightens the finger flexors, allowing functional grip. This is a major functional milestone \u2014 C6 patients can drive with hand controls, perform transfers, and have greater independence than C5.")
B.Su=s(["T12","T10","T6","C4"],t.s)
B.ac5=new A.e("Autonomic dysreflexia occurs in SCI at which level and above?",B.Su,2,"Autonomic dysreflexia occurs in SCI at T6 and above. A noxious stimulus below the injury (full bladder is #1 cause) triggers massive sympathetic discharge that cannot be modulated by descending inhibition. Presents with severe hypertension, bradycardia, headache, and flushing above the level. Emergency: sit up, find and remove stimulus.")
B.QI=s([B.a9R,B.a6D,B.a7T,B.abq,B.ac5],t.b)
B.Is=new A.a6("Spinal Cord Injury 2",B.QI)
B.Sw=s(["No erectile function possible","Psychogenic erections only","Reflexogenic erections (preserved); ejaculation often impaired","Normal sexual function"],t.s)
B.aaO=new A.e("Sexual function in males with UMN SCI (above T12) typically includes:",B.Sw,2,"UMN SCI males: reflexogenic erections are preserved (sacral reflex arc intact at S2-S4), but psychogenic erections are lost (descending pathways disrupted). Ejaculation is impaired in ~95%. LMN SCI: psychogenic erections may occur but reflexogenic are lost. Fertility assistance: vibratory stimulation or electroejaculation.")
B.TZ=s(["The intercostal muscles","The diaphragm (via the phrenic nerve)","The abdominal muscles","The accessory muscles of respiration"],t.s)
B.a7B=new A.e("Respiratory function in C3-C5 SCI is compromised because these levels innervate:",B.TZ,1,"The phrenic nerve (C3-C5) innervates the diaphragm, the primary muscle of respiration. C3-C5 SCI impairs diaphragmatic function. C3 and above = ventilator-dependent. C4 = may wean from ventilator. C5 = diaphragm intact, but intercostals and abdominals lost, so cough is weak and secretion clearance is impaired.")
B.VA=s(["Lung parenchymal disease","Loss of intercostal and abdominal muscle function","Cardiac dysfunction","Upper airway obstruction"],t.s)
B.aaI=new A.e("The FEV1 and forced vital capacity in tetraplegia are reduced primarily due to:",B.VA,1,"Tetraplegia causes restrictive lung disease from paralysis of intercostal muscles (T1-T11) and abdominal muscles (T7-L1). This reduces chest wall expansion, expiratory force, and cough effectiveness. Paradoxically, vital capacity improves in supine position (abdominal contents push diaphragm up, improving length-tension relationship).")
B.YM=s(["Decreased work of breathing","Abdominal contents pushing the diaphragm into a more optimal length-tension position","Reduced dead space","Improved cardiac output"],t.s)
B.a6_=new A.e("In SCI, the improvement in vital capacity when supine compared to sitting is due to:",B.YM,1,"In high SCI, supine positioning allows gravity to push abdominal contents cephalad, placing the diaphragm in a more dome-shaped position with better length-tension mechanics. Sitting causes the abdominal contents to fall away, flattening the diaphragm and reducing efficiency. An abdominal binder helps in seated position.")
B.O2=s(["Having the patient cough independently","Applying manual abdominal thrust synchronized with the patient's cough effort","Chest percussion only","Incentive spirometry alone"],t.s)
B.a3J=new A.e("Assisted cough techniques in SCI involve:",B.O2,1,"Quad cough/assisted cough: a caregiver applies an abdominal thrust (pushing inward and upward on the abdomen) synchronized with the patient's cough effort to augment expiratory force. This compensates for paralyzed abdominal muscles and is critical for airway clearance in high SCI.")
B.M_=s([B.aaO,B.a7B,B.aaI,B.a6_,B.a3J],t.b)
B.It=new A.a6("Spinal Cord Injury 6",B.M_)
B.OQ=s(["First-second","Second-third","Third-fourth","Fourth-fifth"],t.s)
B.a3m=new A.e("Morton's neuroma most commonly affects which intermetatarsal space?",B.OQ,2,"Morton's neuroma most commonly affects the third-fourth intermetatarsal space (between the 3rd and 4th metatarsal heads). It involves the interdigital nerve and presents with burning pain, numbness in adjacent toes, and a positive Mulder's click test.")
B.Ub=s(["Pain at the Achilles insertion that worsens with activity","Heel pain that is worst with the first steps in the morning","Forefoot pain that worsens with toe walking","Lateral ankle pain with weight bearing"],t.s)
B.a5G=new A.e("Plantar fasciitis pain is classically described as:",B.Ub,1,"Plantar fasciitis presents with medial heel pain at the plantar fascial origin on the calcaneal tubercle. It is classically worst with the first steps in the morning (post-static dyskinesia) and after prolonged sitting, then gradually improves with walking.")
B.Pb=s(["Jones fracture","March fracture","Lisfranc fracture","Dancer's fracture"],t.s)
B.abz=new A.e("A stress fracture of the second metatarsal is also known as a:",B.Pb,1,'A "march fracture" is a stress fracture of the second (or third) metatarsal shaft, classically seen in military recruits during basic training. A Jones fracture is at the base of the 5th metatarsal. A dancer\'s fracture is a spiral fracture of the 5th metatarsal shaft.')
B.UK=s(["Talus and calcaneus","Metatarsal bases and tarsal bones (cuneiforms and cuboid)","Phalanges and metatarsal heads","Navicular and cuneiforms"],t.s)
B.aaV=new A.e("The Lisfranc joint complex involves the articulation between which structures?",B.UK,1,"The Lisfranc joint is the tarsometatarsal joint complex where the metatarsal bases articulate with the cuneiform bones and cuboid. Lisfranc injuries are commonly missed on initial X-rays. The key ligament runs from the medial cuneiform to the base of the second metatarsal.")
B.PZ=s(["Lateral deviation of the great toe","Degenerative arthritis of the first MTP joint with limited dorsiflexion","Inflammation of the sesamoid bones","A fracture of the hallux proximal phalanx"],t.s)
B.a5n=new A.e("Hallux rigidus refers to:",B.PZ,1,"Hallux rigidus is osteoarthritis of the first MTP joint characterized by pain and limited dorsiflexion (normally 65-75 degrees). It is the most common arthritic condition of the foot. Hallux valgus (bunion) is lateral deviation of the great toe \u2014 a different condition.")
B.NM=s([B.a3m,B.a5G,B.abz,B.aaV,B.a5n],t.b)
B.Iu=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Foot",B.NM)
B.Wn=s(["Two separate TBI events months apart","A second concussion before the first has fully resolved, causing catastrophic cerebral swelling","Bilateral subdural hematomas","Repeated seizures after TBI"],t.s)
B.a9j=new A.e("Second impact syndrome refers to:",B.Wn,1,"Second impact syndrome: a second concussion before complete recovery from the first causes loss of cerebrovascular autoregulation, massive cerebral edema, and often death. This is why return-to-play protocols require complete symptom resolution before any return to contact sports.")
B.YF=s(["Dopaminergic agents","Typical antipsychotics (haloperidol) and benzodiazepines","SSRIs","Acetaminophen"],t.s)
B.a4E=new A.e("In TBI rehabilitation, which medication class should be AVOIDED because it impairs cognitive recovery?",B.YF,1,"Typical antipsychotics (haloperidol), benzodiazepines, and anticholinergics impair cognitive recovery after TBI and should be avoided when possible. Preferred alternatives: atypical antipsychotics (quetiapine) if needed for agitation, and non-sedating medications for other symptoms.")
B.NY=s(["Infection","Pulmonary embolism","Uncontrolled intracranial hypertension","Cardiac arrhythmia"],t.s)
B.aan=new A.e("The most common cause of death in the first 48 hours after severe TBI is:",B.NY,2,"Uncontrolled intracranial hypertension (brain herniation) is the leading cause of death in the first 48 hours after severe TBI. This is why ICP monitoring and aggressive management (targeting ICP <20 mmHg and CPP 60-70 mmHg) are critical.")
B.Yx=s(["It prevents wound healing","Brain tissue releases thromboplastin, causing DIC and worsening hemorrhage","It requires lifelong anticoagulation","It only occurs in mild TBI"],t.s)
B.a7I=new A.e("Coagulopathy after TBI is concerning because:",B.Yx,1,"Damaged brain tissue releases thromboplastin (tissue factor) into the circulation, triggering disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC). This paradoxically causes both clotting and bleeding, expanding intracranial hemorrhages. Coagulopathy worsens outcomes and must be corrected urgently.")
B.Nk=s(["Bradycardia and hypotension","Tachycardia, hypertension, tachypnea, diaphoresis, and posturing","Seizure activity on EEG","Hypothermia and bradypnea"],t.s)
B.acq=new A.e("Paroxysmal sympathetic hyperactivity (storming) after severe TBI is characterized by:",B.Nk,1,"Paroxysmal sympathetic hyperactivity (formerly dysautonomia/sympathetic storming): episodic tachycardia, hypertension, tachypnea, diaphoresis, hyperthermia, and extensor posturing. Triggered by stimulation. Treated with beta-blockers, bromocriptine, gabapentin, and minimizing triggers.")
B.RN=s([B.a9j,B.a4E,B.aan,B.a7I,B.acq],t.b)
B.Iv=new A.a6("Traumatic Brain Injury 5",B.RN)
B.S0=s(["Outpatient, 2-12 weeks post-event","Inpatient, during acute hospitalization","Long-term community maintenance","Pre-surgical optimization"],t.s)
B.a9u=new A.e("Phase I cardiac rehabilitation takes place during which period?",B.S0,1,"Phase I is inpatient cardiac rehab during acute hospitalization. Phase II is supervised outpatient (2-12 weeks). Phase III is a transitional monitored program. Phase IV is lifelong maintenance.")
B.NT=s(["1-2 METs","3-5 METs","5-7 METs","7-10 METs"],t.s)
B.a7D=new A.e("During Phase I cardiac rehab, activities are typically limited to which MET level?",B.NT,0,"Phase I activities are limited to 1-2 METs (e.g., self-care, slow walking, sitting in a chair). The focus is on preventing deconditioning, patient education, and safe mobilization while still in the acute setting.")
B.TU=s(["Atrial fibrillation","Peripheral artery disease","Stable angina pectoris","Aortic aneurysm"],t.s)
B.a6o=new A.e("Which of the following is a Medicare-covered diagnosis for cardiac rehabilitation?",B.TU,2,"Medicare-covered diagnoses for cardiac rehab include: MI within 12 months, CABG, stable angina, heart valve repair/replacement, PCI (stenting/angioplasty), heart or heart-lung transplant, and stable chronic heart failure with EF \u226435%.")
B.ZW=s(["\u226425%","\u226430%","\u226435%","\u226440%"],t.s)
B.a5w=new A.e("The ejection fraction cutoff for heart failure patients to qualify for Medicare-covered cardiac rehabilitation is:",B.ZW,2,"Stable chronic heart failure patients with an ejection fraction of \u226435% qualify for Medicare-covered cardiac rehabilitation. This specific threshold is a commonly tested board question.")
B.X8=s(["12 sessions","24 sessions","36 sessions","48 sessions"],t.s)
B.aci=new A.e("How many cardiac rehabilitation sessions does Medicare typically reimburse?",B.X8,2,"Medicare reimburses up to 36 sessions of cardiac rehabilitation, with the possibility of an additional 36 sessions if medically necessary. Studies show that attending all 36 sessions is associated with significantly better outcomes than partial attendance.")
B.RF=s([B.a9u,B.a7D,B.a6o,B.a5w,B.aci],t.b)
B.Iw=new A.a6("Cardiac Rehab 3",B.RF)
B.Vi=s([">-1.0","-1.0 to -2.5","\u2264-2.5","<-3.5 only"],t.s)
B.a7m=new A.e("Osteoporosis is defined by a DEXA T-score of:",B.Vi,2,"WHO criteria: T-score \u2265-1.0 = normal. T-score -1.0 to -2.5 = osteopenia. T-score \u2264-2.5 = osteoporosis. T-score \u2264-2.5 with fragility fracture = severe osteoporosis. T-score compares to young adult peak bone mass. Z-score (age-matched) is used in premenopausal women and men <50.")
B.Xf=s(["Skull and ribs","Vertebral body, hip (proximal femur), and distal radius (Colles fracture)","Tibial shaft and fibula","Hands and feet"],t.s)
B.a8L=new A.e("The most common sites for osteoporotic fractures are:",B.Xf,1,"Osteoporotic fracture sites: vertebral compression fractures (most common overall, often asymptomatic), hip fractures (proximal femur \u2014 highest morbidity and mortality, 20-30% 1-year mortality), and distal radius (Colles fracture from FOOSH). These are trabecular bone-rich areas that lose density earliest.")
B.Vy=s(["It increases calcium absorption","Mechanical loading stimulates osteoblast activity and bone formation (Wolff's law)","It replaces medication therapy","It only works in young people"],t.s)
B.a8h=new A.e("Weight-bearing exercise helps prevent osteoporosis because:",B.Vy,1,"Wolff's law: bone adapts to the loads placed upon it. Weight-bearing and resistance exercises create mechanical loading that stimulates osteoblast activity and new bone formation. Best exercises: walking, jogging, stair climbing, resistance training. Swimming/cycling are non-weight-bearing and less effective for bone density. Exercise also improves balance and reduces fall risk.")
B.Mm=s(["Stimulating osteoblasts","Inhibiting osteoclast-mediated bone resorption","Increasing calcium absorption","Replacing estrogen"],t.s)
B.a89=new A.e("Bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate) work by:",B.Mm,1,"Bisphosphonates: bind to hydroxyapatite at bone resorption sites and are taken up by osteoclasts, inhibiting their activity and inducing apoptosis. This reduces bone resorption and net bone loss. First-line treatment for osteoporosis. Must be taken with water on empty stomach, upright for 30 minutes (prevent esophageal ulceration). Rare risks: osteonecrosis of jaw, atypical femoral fractures.")
B.S9=s(["Diagnose osteoporosis on DEXA","Calculate 10-year probability of major osteoporotic fracture and hip fracture to guide treatment decisions","Measure bone formation markers","Determine calcium intake needs"],t.s)
B.a54=new A.e("FRAX score is used to:",B.S9,1,"FRAX: online tool integrating clinical risk factors (age, sex, BMI, prior fracture, family history, smoking, alcohol, steroids, RA, secondary causes) \xb1 femoral neck BMD to calculate 10-year fracture probability. Treatment threshold: \u226520% major osteoporotic fracture OR \u22653% hip fracture. Used for osteopenic patients to decide if pharmacotherapy is warranted.")
B.Ro=s([B.a7m,B.a8L,B.a8h,B.a89,B.a54],t.b)
B.Ix=new A.a6("Osteoporosis 1",B.Ro)
B.RZ=s(["Upward herniation of the cerebellar tonsils","Downward herniation of the cerebellar tonsils and brainstem through the foramen magnum","Agenesis of the corpus callosum","Dandy-Walker malformation"],t.s)
B.a98=new A.e("The Arnold-Chiari II malformation associated with myelomeningocele involves:",B.RZ,1,"Chiari II: downward displacement of the cerebellar vermis, tonsils, and brainstem through the foramen magnum. It is present in virtually all myelomeningocele patients. Can cause hydrocephalus (90% require VP shunt), brainstem dysfunction (stridor, apnea, swallowing difficulties), and syringomyelia.")
B.M3=s(["During the second trimester","At least 1 month before conception","After the baby is born","Only if there is a family history"],t.s)
B.a8K=new A.e("Folic acid supplementation to prevent neural tube defects should begin:",B.M3,1,"Folic acid 0.4 mg/day recommended for all women of childbearing age starting at least 1 month before conception. Women with prior NTD-affected pregnancy should take 4 mg/day. Neural tube closure occurs by day 28 of gestation (often before pregnancy is recognized), making preconception supplementation critical.")
B.No=s(["These children are never exposed to latex","Repeated surgical procedures cause sensitization to latex proteins","Latex causes neural tube defects","It only affects adults"],t.s)
B.a4D=new A.e("Latex allergy is a major concern in myelomeningocele because:",B.No,1,"Myelomeningocele patients have up to 73% latex sensitization rate due to repeated early surgical exposures (shunt placements, closures, catheterizations). Reactions range from contact dermatitis to anaphylaxis. Strict latex-free precautions are mandatory from birth. Cross-reactivity exists with bananas, kiwi, avocado, and chestnuts.")
B.NZ=s(["Improved motor function","Progressive scoliosis, back pain, changing gait, and declining bladder function","Seizures","Upper extremity weakness"],t.s)
B.aaf=new A.e("Tethered cord syndrome in spina bifida presents with:",B.NZ,1,"Tethered cord: the spinal cord is abnormally fixed to surrounding structures, and as the child grows, the cord is stretched. Presents with progressive scoliosis, back/leg pain, changing gait pattern, worsening bladder function, or new foot deformities. MRI confirms; surgical untethering is indicated.")
B.R7=s(["No lower extremity function","Intact quadriceps (knee extension) but weak/absent ankle function","Normal walking ability","Only hip flexion"],t.s)
B.a5L=new A.e("A child with L4 myelomeningocele level would have:",B.R7,1,"L4 level: intact hip flexion (L1-L2), knee extension (L3-L4), and some ankle dorsiflexion (L4). Absent ankle plantarflexion (S1) and hip extension (L5-S1). These children are typically community ambulators with AFOs. Hip subluxation risk due to muscle imbalance (flexors > extensors).")
B.Zs=s([B.a98,B.a8K,B.a4D,B.aaf,B.a5L],t.b)
B.Iy=new A.a6("Pediatrics 3",B.Zs)
B.Pv=s(["Wrist fracture","Rotator cuff injury/shoulder impingement","Elbow dislocation","Cervical radiculopathy"],t.s)
B.a8H=new A.e("The most common upper extremity injury in long-term manual wheelchair users is:",B.Pv,1,"Shoulder pain/injury affects 50-70% of long-term manual wheelchair users. Repetitive propulsion causes rotator cuff impingement, tendinitis, and tears. Prevention: proper wheelchair fit (rear axle position), efficient stroke pattern (semicircular/arc pattern vs. pumping), minimize transfers, and strengthening of scapular stabilizers and rotator cuff.")
B.Qr=s(["Best outdoor performance","Smallest turning radius for indoor maneuverability","Fastest speed","Best hill climbing"],t.s)
B.a9h=new A.e("Power wheelchair drive configurations include mid-wheel, rear-wheel, and front-wheel drive. Mid-wheel drive offers:",B.Qr,1,"Mid-wheel drive: drive wheels at the center of gravity \u2192 smallest turning radius (most maneuverable indoors). Rear-wheel drive: most stable at high speeds, best for outdoor use. Front-wheel drive: best for curb climbing and rough terrain, less tippy going forward. Choice depends on primary use environment.")
B.Vq=s(["Short, rapid pumping strokes","Long, smooth semicircular strokes with the hand dropping below the pushrim during recovery","Only pushing with one arm","Gripping the pushrim tightly at all times"],t.s)
B.a9n=new A.e("The recommended push technique for manual wheelchair propulsion to reduce shoulder injury is:",B.Vq,1,"Semicircular/arc pattern: long push strokes (start behind the axle, push forward), then the hand drops below the pushrim in a smooth arc during recovery. This reduces push frequency (fewer repetitions) and eliminates the abrupt braking/start of pumping patterns. Clinical Practice Guidelines (Preservation of Upper Limb Function) recommend this pattern.")
B.Xe=s(["Forward tipping","Backward tipping during wheelies or ascending ramps","Lateral tipping","The footrests from catching on obstacles"],t.s)
B.a45=new A.e("Anti-tip casters on a wheelchair prevent:",B.Xe,1,"Anti-tip casters: small wheels extending behind the rear axle that prevent backward tipping. Essential for safety, especially with aggressive axle positions (forward for efficiency but less stable). Required for new manual wheelchair users. Can be removed by experienced users who need to perform wheelies for curb negotiation.")
B.Mx=s(["A flat seat cushion only","Custom-molded back support that accommodates (or corrects) the spinal curvature","No special modifications","A reclining-only chair"],t.s)
B.abi=new A.e("Wheelchair seating for a patient with significant scoliosis requires:",B.Mx,1,"Scoliosis seating: custom-molded back with lateral trunk supports that either accommodate a fixed curve (prevent progression) or correct a flexible curve. The goal is balanced, symmetrical sitting with even weight distribution. Off-the-shelf backs are inadequate for significant scoliosis. Requires collaboration between the rehab physician, therapist, and seating specialist.")
B.Zf=s([B.a8H,B.a9h,B.a9n,B.a45,B.abi],t.b)
B.Iz=new A.a6("Wheelchair 2",B.Zf)
B.X5=s(["Adolescents","Children under 5 years","Adults over 40","Elderly patients"],t.s)
B.a4c=new A.e("Nursemaid's elbow (radial head subluxation) most commonly occurs in which age group?",B.X5,1,"Nursemaid's elbow occurs most commonly in children under 5 years old due to their relatively undeveloped annular ligament. It is caused by a sudden pull on the extended, pronated arm. Reduction involves supination with elbow flexion.")
B.W_=s(["Wrist drop with loss of sensation","Finger drop (inability to extend fingers at MCP joints) without sensory loss","Numbness in the thumb and index finger","Weakness of elbow flexion"],t.s)
B.a36=new A.e("Posterior interosseous nerve (PIN) syndrome at the elbow presents with:",B.W_,1,"PIN syndrome causes weakness of finger and thumb extension at the MCP joints without sensory loss (PIN is a motor branch). Wrist extension is partially preserved (ECRL is innervated before the PIN). The Arcade of Frohse is the most common compression site.")
B.WH=s(["Anterior elbow crease","Lateral epicondyle","Posterior tip of the elbow","Medial epicondyle"],t.s)
B.a7L=new A.e("Olecranon bursitis is characterized by swelling at which location?",B.WH,2,'The olecranon bursa lies superficially over the posterior tip of the olecranon process. Bursitis causes visible swelling at this site ("student\'s elbow" or "miner\'s elbow"). It can be traumatic, infectious (septic), or due to gout/RA. Always aspirate if infection is suspected.')
B.RH=s(["Lateral epicondyle fracture, UCL tear, radial head fracture","Elbow dislocation, radial head fracture, coronoid process fracture","Olecranon fracture, UCL tear, radial nerve injury","Supracondylar fracture, radial head fracture, ulnar nerve injury"],t.s)
B.aba=new A.e('The "terrible triad" of the elbow includes which three injuries?',B.RH,1,'The "terrible triad" = posterior elbow dislocation + radial head fracture + coronoid process fracture. It has a high rate of instability and poor outcomes. Surgical fixation of both fractures plus LCL repair is typically required.')
B.X7=s(["Lateral epicondylitis","Olecranon bursitis","Elbow dislocation or fracture-dislocation","Cubital tunnel syndrome"],t.s)
B.a6w=new A.e("Heterotopic ossification (HO) at the elbow is most commonly seen after which type of injury?",B.X7,2,"HO at the elbow is most common after elbow dislocations and fracture-dislocations, especially with associated head injuries or burns. Prophylaxis with indomethacin or low-dose radiation may be considered in high-risk patients. Surgical excision is delayed until the bone matures.")
B.Tj=s([B.a4c,B.a36,B.a7L,B.aba,B.a6w],t.b)
B.IA=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Elbow 2",B.Tj)
B.YT=s(["Adolescent athletes",'Middle-aged "weekend warriors"',"Elderly sedentary patients","Professional athletes"],t.s)
B.acp=new A.e("Achilles tendon rupture classically occurs in which demographic?",B.YT,1,'Achilles ruptures classically occur in middle-aged (30-50) recreational athletes ("weekend warriors") during sudden push-off activities. Risk factors include fluoroquinolone use, corticosteroid injections, and pre-existing tendinopathy.')
B.RT=s(["Dorsiflexing the ankle against resistance","Squeezing the calf and observing for plantarflexion","Palpating the tendon for a gap","Having the patient walk on toes"],t.s)
B.a8z=new A.e("The Thompson test for Achilles tendon rupture is performed by:",B.RT,1,"The Thompson test: patient prone, squeeze the calf \u2014 normal response is passive plantarflexion. If the foot does not plantarflex (positive Thompson), the Achilles tendon is likely ruptured. It is the most reliable clinical test for complete rupture.")
B.YG=s(["Posteriorly behind the lateral malleolus","Anteriorly over the lateral malleolus","Medially under the medial malleolus","Superiorly above the ankle joint"],t.s)
B.a9K=new A.e("Peroneal tendon subluxation most commonly occurs in which direction?",B.YG,1,"Peroneal tendons sublux anteriorly over the lateral malleolus when the superior peroneal retinaculum is torn. It occurs with forceful dorsiflexion and eversion, commonly in skiing. Patients feel a snapping sensation at the lateral ankle.")
B.Ti=s(["Weber classification","Lauge-Hansen classification","Grade I-III severity system","Danis-Weber classification"],t.s)
B.a4q=new A.e("Which classification system is used for lateral ankle sprains?",B.Ti,2,"Lateral ankle sprains use Grade I (stretch/microscopic tear, mild swelling), Grade II (partial tear, moderate swelling, some instability), Grade III (complete tear, significant swelling, gross instability). Weber and Lauge-Hansen classify ankle fractures, not sprains.")
B.NS=s(["Deltoid ligament","Spring ligament","Anterior talofibular ligament","Calcaneofibular ligament"],t.s)
B.a4u=new A.e("Chronic lateral ankle instability most commonly results from repeated injury to which ligament?",B.NS,2,"Chronic lateral ankle instability most commonly results from repeated ATFL injuries that fail to heal properly. Up to 20% of acute ankle sprains progress to chronic instability. The Brostr\xf6m procedure (direct ligament repair) is the surgical treatment of choice.")
B.RG=s([B.acp,B.a8z,B.a9K,B.a4q,B.a4u],t.b)
B.IB=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Ankle 2",B.RG)
B.RA=s(["Hip instability","Foot drop (weak ankle dorsiflexors) and ankle instability","Knee hyperextension only","Trunk support"],t.s)
B.a5R=new A.e("An ankle-foot orthosis (AFO) is primarily prescribed for:",B.RA,1,"AFOs are most commonly prescribed for foot drop (peroneal nerve palsy, L5 radiculopathy, stroke). They prevent the foot from dragging during swing phase and can also control ankle instability, manage spasticity-related equinus, and provide knee control through ground reaction forces.")
B.Od=s(["Free ankle motion","Maximum ankle control with no dorsiflexion or plantarflexion","Only medial-lateral stability","Active ankle push-off"],t.s)
B.aa7=new A.e("A solid ankle AFO provides:",B.Od,1,"Solid ankle AFO: rigid at the ankle, blocks all ankle motion. Provides maximum stability and control. Used for significant spasticity, severe weakness, or when medial-lateral instability is present. Disadvantage: prevents normal ankle rocker mechanism, can cause knee hyperextension thrust if not properly trimmed.")
B.OB=s(["It is made of metal only","It allows controlled dorsiflexion while blocking plantarflexion (preventing foot drop)","It controls the hip joint","It is only for children"],t.s)
B.a34=new A.e("A hinged AFO (articulated AFO) differs from a solid AFO in that:",B.OB,1,"Hinged AFO: allows dorsiflexion (normal tibial advancement in stance) while a plantarflexion stop prevents foot drop in swing. This preserves more normal gait mechanics (third rocker). Best for patients with foot drop who have adequate medial-lateral ankle stability and can control dorsiflexion range.")
B.SO=s(["Only ankle control is needed","Both knee and ankle control are needed (e.g., quadriceps weakness with foot drop)","Only for cosmetic purposes","For upper extremity support"],t.s)
B.a4W=new A.e("A knee-ankle-foot orthosis (KAFO) is indicated when:",B.SO,1,"KAFO: needed when knee instability (quadriceps weakness <3/5) exists in addition to ankle control needs. Common in polio, SCI, myelomeningocele. Adds significant weight and energy cost to ambulation. Knee lock options: drop-lock (manual), bail lock (spring-loaded), stance control (electronic \u2014 locks in stance, free in swing).")
B.Nj=s(["Promote ankle plantarflexion","Create a knee extension moment to control crouch gait","Allow free ankle motion","Provide hip support"],t.s)
B.a7V=new A.e("Ground reaction AFO (floor reaction orthosis) is designed to:",B.Nj,1,"Ground reaction AFO: solid AFO with anterior tibial shell and rigid footplate. It positions the GRF vector ANTERIOR to the knee, creating a knee extension moment. Primary indication: crouch gait (excessive knee flexion in stance) in CP. It requires adequate ankle ROM to at least neutral for proper function.")
B.Z8=s([B.a5R,B.aa7,B.a34,B.a4W,B.a7V],t.b)
B.IC=new A.a6("Prosthetics & Orthotics 4",B.Z8)
B.UR=s(["The peripheral joints only","The sacroiliac joints and spine, causing progressive fusion","The hands and wrists","Only the cervical spine"],t.s)
B.a5d=new A.e("Ankylosing spondylitis (AS) primarily affects:",B.UR,1,"AS: chronic inflammatory disease primarily affecting the SI joints (earliest finding: bilateral sacroiliitis) and spine (ascending). HLA-B27 positive (~90%). Progressive bamboo spine (vertebral fusion). Schober test measures lumbar flexion. Treatment: NSAIDs (first-line), TNF inhibitors for refractory disease, exercise program (extension-based).")
B.Md=s(["Symmetric joint involvement only","DIP joint involvement, dactylitis (sausage digits), nail changes, and asymmetric pattern","Positive rheumatoid factor","Absence of skin changes"],t.s)
B.a7t=new A.e("Psoriatic arthritis can be distinguished from RA by:",B.Md,1,"Psoriatic arthritis: seronegative (RF negative), DIP involvement (unlike RA), dactylitis (sausage digit \u2014 entire digit swollen), nail changes (pitting, onycholysis), asymmetric, and enthesitis (Achilles, plantar fascia). Five patterns: oligoarticular asymmetric (most common), polyarticular symmetric, DIP predominant, spondylitic, arthritis mutilans (severe destructive).")
B.Xm=s(["Calcium pyrophosphate","Monosodium urate (MSU) crystals","Hydroxyapatite","Oxalate crystals"],t.s)
B.ac2=new A.e("Gout is caused by deposition of which crystal?",B.Xm,1,"Gout: MSU crystal deposition from hyperuricemia. Crystals are needle-shaped and negatively birefringent under polarized light (yellow when parallel to compensator). Acute attacks: typically 1st MTP joint (podagra). Treatment: acute = colchicine, NSAIDs, corticosteroids; chronic = urate-lowering therapy (allopurinol, febuxostat) targeting serum urate <6 mg/dL.")
B.Sz=s(["Negatively birefringent and needle-shaped","Positively birefringent and rhomboid-shaped (calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate)","Not visible under microscopy","Made of cholesterol"],t.s)
B.a4P=new A.e("Pseudogout differs from gout in that pseudogout crystals are:",B.Sz,1,"Pseudogout (CPPD disease): calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystals \u2014 rhomboid/square-shaped, weakly positively birefringent (blue when parallel to compensator). Most commonly affects the knee (vs. 1st MTP in gout). Associated with aging, hemochromatosis, hyperparathyroidism, and hypomagnesemia. X-ray shows chondrocalcinosis (cartilage calcification).")
B.U3=s(["Rash, fever, joint pain","Arthritis, urethritis, and conjunctivitis","Headache, neck stiffness, photophobia","Diarrhea, abdominal pain, weight loss"],t.s)
B.a3C=new A.e("Reactive arthritis (formerly Reiter syndrome) classically presents with the triad of:",B.U3,1,"Reactive arthritis triad: \"Can't see (conjunctivitis), can't pee (urethritis), can't climb a tree (arthritis).\" Occurs 1-4 weeks after genitourinary (Chlamydia) or enteric (Salmonella, Shigella, Campylobacter) infection. HLA-B27 associated. Asymmetric oligoarthritis, predominantly lower extremity. Treatment: NSAIDs, treat underlying infection if present.")
B.SM=s([B.a5d,B.a7t,B.ac2,B.a4P,B.a3C],t.b)
B.ID=new A.a6("Rheumatology 2",B.SM)
B.OL=s(["Upper extremity","Lower extremity","Trunk","Neck"],t.s)
B.a4N=new A.e("Common peroneal neuropathy at the fibular head is the most common entrapment neuropathy in which region?",B.OL,1,"Common peroneal neuropathy at the fibular head is the most common lower extremity entrapment neuropathy. It causes foot drop with a steppage gait pattern. Risk factors include leg crossing, prolonged bed rest, weight loss, and fibular head fractures.")
B.PD=s(["Metabolic damage from chronic hyperglycemia","Immune-mediated vasculitis of the vasa nervorum","Direct glucose toxicity to motor neurons","Compression from diabetic lipohypertrophy"],t.s)
B.aaa=new A.e("Diabetic amyotrophy (diabetic lumbosacral radiculoplexus neuropathy) is caused by:",B.PD,1,"Diabetic amyotrophy (DLRPN) is NOT metabolic \u2014 it is immune-mediated vasculitis of the vasa nervorum. It causes severe pain followed by proximal weakness, often after weight loss or improved glycemic control. It is multifocal, asymmetric, and can be bilateral.")
B.ZJ=s(["Diabetic peripheral neuropathy","Carpal tunnel syndrome","Vasculitis","Hereditary neuropathy"],t.s)
B.a9w=new A.e("A mononeuritis multiplex pattern on NCS (multifocal, asymmetric, patchy nerve involvement) is most suggestive of:",B.ZJ,2,"Mononeuritis multiplex (multifocal, asymmetric, patchy nerve involvement) is the hallmark pattern of vasculitis affecting the vasa nervorum. Each nerve is hit randomly as its individual blood supply is attacked. This pattern demands workup for systemic vasculitis.")
B.P3=s(["Circumducting the leg in an arc","Dropping the opposite hip during stance","Exaggerated knee flexion to clear the dropping foot","Waddling side to side"],t.s)
B.ach=new A.e("Foot drop with a steppage gait is characterized by:",B.P3,2,"Steppage gait: the patient lifts the knee higher than normal during swing phase to clear the dropping foot from the ground. It results from ankle dorsiflexor weakness (peroneal nerve or L5 root). Circumduction = UMN/hip weakness. Trendelenburg = gluteus medius weakness.")
B.Z3=s(["It is the largest sensory nerve in the body","It is easily accessible and provides a reliable lower extremity SNAP","It is purely motor","It innervates the entire foot"],t.s)
B.a5u=new A.e("The sural nerve is most useful in NCS because:",B.Z3,1,"The sural nerve (terminal sensory branch of the tibial nerve) is easily accessible at the mid-calf behind the lateral malleolus. It provides a reliable lower extremity SNAP and is routinely tested in neuropathy evaluations. Its absence strongly suggests a generalized neuropathy.")
B.M1=s([B.a4N,B.aaa,B.a9w,B.ach,B.a5u],t.b)
B.IE=new A.a6("Electrodiagnostic 11",B.M1)
B.TA=s(["Large capacity, flaccid bladder","Small capacity, hyperreflexic bladder with detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia","Normal voiding","Stress incontinence only"],t.s)
B.a3y=new A.e("The neurogenic bladder in SCI above the sacral segments (UMN bladder) is characterized by:",B.TA,1,"UMN (spastic) bladder: small capacity, hyperreflexic detrusor contractions, and detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia (DSD \u2014 sphincter contracts simultaneously with detrusor). This causes high intravesical pressures and risk of reflux/renal damage. Management: intermittent catheterization + anticholinergics.")
B.VG=s(["Urinary incontinence","Urinary tract infections","Upper urinary tract deterioration (hydronephrosis and renal failure)","Bladder stones"],t.s)
B.aaN=new A.e("The most important long-term complication to prevent in neurogenic bladder management is:",B.VG,2,"Renal failure was historically the leading cause of death in SCI. Preventing upper tract damage (hydronephrosis, reflux, renal scarring) is the primary goal. This requires keeping intravesical pressures low (<40 cmH2O), regular intermittent catheterization, and annual urodynamic and renal imaging surveillance.")
B.Ph=s(["200 mL","300-400 mL","500 mL","800 mL"],t.s)
B.a9g=new A.e("Intermittent catheterization in SCI aims to keep bladder volumes below:",B.Ph,1,"Target: keep volumes <500 mL per catheterization (ideally 300-400 mL). Catheterize every 4-6 hours (4-6 times daily). Fluid intake is regulated to ~2 L/day to achieve this. Overdistension increases UTI risk and can cause autonomic dysreflexia.")
B.Pq=s(["Occiput","Sacrum and ischial tuberosities","Greater trochanter only","Medial malleolus"],t.s)
B.a7u=new A.e("Pressure injuries in SCI most commonly occur over which bony prominence?",B.Pq,1,"The sacrum (#1 in supine/acute care) and ischial tuberosities (#1 in wheelchair users) are the most common pressure injury sites in SCI. Prevention: regular pressure relief (wheelchair push-ups every 15-30 minutes), appropriate cushion, skin inspection, and nutrition optimization.")
B.OX=s(["5 minutes","15-30 minutes","1 hour","2 hours"],t.s)
B.a9E=new A.e("Pressure relief in a wheelchair should be performed every:",B.OX,1,"Wheelchair users should perform pressure relief every 15-30 minutes for at least 15-30 seconds. Methods: wheelchair push-ups, side leans, forward leans, or power tilt/recline. In bed, repositioning should occur every 2 hours. Pressure mapping can optimize wheelchair cushion selection.")
B.Zi=s([B.a3y,B.aaN,B.a9g,B.a7u,B.a9E],t.b)
B.IF=new A.a6("Spinal Cord Injury 5",B.Zi)
B.OD=s(["Is always chronic","Results from activation of peripheral nociceptors by tissue damage (somatic or visceral)","Is caused by nerve damage and described as burning/shooting","Does not respond to NSAIDs"],t.s)
B.a84=new A.e("Nociceptive pain differs from neuropathic pain in that nociceptive pain:",B.OD,1,"Nociceptive: normal neural processing of noxious stimuli from tissue damage. Somatic (musculoskeletal \u2014 well-localized, aching) or visceral (organ \u2014 diffuse, cramping, referred). Neuropathic: caused by damage/dysfunction of the nervous system itself (burning, shooting, electric, allodynia). Treatment differs: nociceptive responds to NSAIDs/opioids; neuropathic to anticonvulsants/antidepressants.")
B.YN=s(["Strong opioids","Acetaminophen and/or NSAIDs \xb1 adjuvants","Nerve blocks","Intrathecal pump"],t.s)
B.a5J=new A.e("The WHO analgesic ladder Step 1 (mild pain) recommends:",B.YN,1,"WHO ladder: Step 1 (mild, 1-3/10): non-opioid analgesics (acetaminophen, NSAIDs) \xb1 adjuvants. Step 2 (moderate, 4-6/10): weak opioids (tramadol, codeine) + non-opioids. Step 3 (severe, 7-10/10): strong opioids (morphine, hydromorphone, fentanyl) + non-opioids \xb1 adjuvants. The ladder emphasizes multimodal analgesia.")
B.Yj=s(["Blocking opioid receptors","Binding to alpha-2-delta subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels, reducing excitatory neurotransmitter release","Inhibiting COX enzymes","Blocking sodium channels"],t.s)
B.a3W=new A.e("Gabapentin and pregabalin treat neuropathic pain by:",B.Yj,1,"Gabapentinoids bind the alpha-2-delta subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels, reducing calcium influx and thereby decreasing release of excitatory neurotransmitters (glutamate, substance P). First-line for neuropathic pain (diabetic neuropathy, postherpetic neuralgia, central pain). Pregabalin has more predictable pharmacokinetics than gabapentin.")
B.Z5=s(["It works better at higher doses","Beyond 4g/day there is no additional analgesic benefit but hepatotoxicity risk increases dramatically","It has no side effects","It can be combined with any other drug safely"],t.s)
B.a6f=new A.e("The ceiling effect of acetaminophen means:",B.Z5,1,"Acetaminophen: analgesic ceiling at 3-4g/day for adults (2g/day for hepatic impairment or chronic alcohol use). Beyond this dose, no additional pain relief but significant risk of hepatotoxicity (leading cause of acute liver failure in the US). It has NO anti-inflammatory effect (unlike NSAIDs). Always check for acetaminophen in combination products.")
B.Tk=s(["Decreased spinal cord excitability","Amplified CNS processing causing allodynia (pain from non-painful stimuli) and hyperalgesia","Normal pain processing","Only peripheral nerve changes"],t.s)
B.abZ=new A.e("Central sensitization in chronic pain is characterized by:",B.Tk,1,"Central sensitization: increased excitability of CNS neurons causing: allodynia (pain from normally innocuous stimuli), hyperalgesia (exaggerated pain response), temporal summation (wind-up), and expanded receptive fields. This is a key mechanism in chronic pain conditions (fibromyalgia, chronic low back pain, complex regional pain syndrome).")
B.R8=s([B.a84,B.a5J,B.a3W,B.a6f,B.abZ],t.b)
B.IG=new A.a6("Pain 1",B.R8)
B.Zd=s(["Foveal artery (ligamentum teres)","Medial femoral circumflex artery","Obturator artery","Superior gluteal artery"],t.s)
B.a7A=new A.e("The blood supply to the femoral head primarily comes from the:",B.Zd,1,"The medial femoral circumflex artery provides the primary blood supply to the femoral head via the retinacular vessels. Femoral neck fractures can disrupt this supply, causing AVN. The foveal artery (via ligamentum teres) provides only a small contribution.")
B.UW=s(["Iliopsoas","Gluteus maximus","Gluteus medius","Quadriceps femoris"],t.s)
B.a8Y=new A.e("A positive Trendelenburg sign indicates weakness of which muscle?",B.UW,2,"A positive Trendelenburg sign occurs when the pelvis drops on the unsupported side during single-leg stance, indicating gluteus medius weakness on the stance side. The gluteus medius (L5, superior gluteal nerve) is the primary hip abductor that stabilizes the pelvis during gait.")
B.VV=s(["Incomplete/valgus impacted fracture","Complete but non-displaced fracture","Complete with partial displacement","Complete fracture with full displacement"],t.s)
B.a9G=new A.e("Femoral neck fractures are classified using the Garden classification. Garden Type IV indicates:",B.VV,3,"Garden classification: Type I = incomplete/impacted (good prognosis). Type II = complete, non-displaced. Type III = complete, partially displaced. Type IV = completely displaced (highest AVN risk). Types III and IV typically require arthroplasty rather than fixation.")
B.VH=s(["Hip extension and external rotation","Hip flexion >90\xb0, adduction, and internal rotation","Hip abduction and external rotation","Hip flexion <45\xb0 and abduction"],t.s)
B.acu=new A.e("After total hip arthroplasty with a posterior approach, which movement is restricted to prevent dislocation?",B.VH,1,"Posterior approach hip precautions: avoid flexion >90\xb0, adduction past midline, and internal rotation. These positions stress the posterior capsule repair. An elevated toilet seat, hip abduction pillow, and avoiding low chairs are standard precautions.")
B.Q2=s(["Diabetes mellitus","Corticosteroid use","Smoking","Osteoarthritis"],t.s)
B.a6L=new A.e("Avascular necrosis of the femoral head is most commonly associated with which risk factor?",B.Q2,1,"Corticosteroid use is the most common non-traumatic cause of femoral head AVN. Other risk factors include alcohol abuse, sickle cell disease, systemic lupus, and femoral neck fractures. Steroid-induced AVN is thought to result from fat emboli and increased intraosseous pressure.")
B.RW=s([B.a7A,B.a8Y,B.a9G,B.acu,B.a6L],t.b)
B.IH=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Hip 1",B.RW)
B.Qz=s(["10 mmHg","20 mmHg","30 mmHg (or within 30 mmHg of diastolic pressure)","50 mmHg"],t.s)
B.acy=new A.e("Acute compartment syndrome requires emergent fasciotomy when compartment pressure exceeds:",B.Qz,2,"Compartment syndrome requires fasciotomy when pressure >30 mmHg or within 30 mmHg of diastolic (delta P <30). The 5 P's (pain out of proportion, pain with passive stretch, paresthesias, pallor, pulselessness) are classic but pulselessness is a late and ominous sign.")
B.Ws=s(["Pulselessness","Paralysis","Pain with passive stretch of the affected compartment muscles","Pallor"],t.s)
B.a4k=new A.e("The most reliable early clinical finding of acute compartment syndrome is:",B.Ws,2,"Pain with passive stretch of the muscles in the affected compartment is the earliest and most reliable clinical sign. For the anterior compartment, passively plantarflexing the foot stretches the tibialis anterior. Waiting for pulselessness means irreversible damage has occurred.")
B.Oe=s(["Anterior compartment syndrome","Stress reaction at the posteromedial tibial cortex","Achilles tendon rupture","Peroneal nerve entrapment"],t.s)
B.a79=new A.e("Medial tibial stress syndrome (shin splints) involves:",B.Oe,1,"Medial tibial stress syndrome is a stress reaction/periostitis at the posteromedial tibial border, typically in the distal two-thirds. It is caused by repetitive loading (running on hard surfaces). It exists on a continuum with tibial stress fractures.")
B.Nv=s(["Gastrocnemius and soleus","Tibialis posterior and FDL","Tibialis anterior, EHL, EDL, and peroneus tertius","Peroneus longus and brevis"],t.s)
B.a3H=new A.e("The anterior compartment of the leg contains which muscles?",B.Nv,2,"The anterior compartment (deep peroneal nerve, anterior tibial artery) contains: tibialis anterior (dorsiflexion/inversion), EHL (great toe extension), EDL (toe extension), and peroneus tertius (dorsiflexion/eversion). Injury causes foot drop.")
B.Qo=s(["It requires emergent fasciotomy","It causes irreversible muscle necrosis","Symptoms resolve with rest and recur with exercise","It only affects the posterior compartment"],t.s)
B.aa8=new A.e("Chronic exertional compartment syndrome differs from acute compartment syndrome in that:",B.Qo,2,"Chronic exertional compartment syndrome causes exercise-induced pain that resolves with rest (usually within 15-30 minutes). It does not cause permanent damage. Diagnosis requires pre- and post-exercise compartment pressure measurements. Fasciotomy is curative for refractory cases.")
B.QQ=s([B.acy,B.a4k,B.a79,B.a3H,B.aa8],t.b)
B.II=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Lower Leg",B.QQ)
B.S3=s(["ACL deficiency","Weak vastus medialis obliquus (VMO)","Hamstring overactivity","Lateral meniscus tear"],t.s)
B.a5v=new A.e("Patellofemoral pain syndrome is the most common cause of anterior knee pain. Which of the following is a key contributing factor?",B.S3,1,"VMO weakness allows the patella to track laterally, increasing patellofemoral contact pressures. VMO strengthening (especially terminal knee extension and isometric quad sets) is central to treatment. Other factors include IT band tightness, increased Q-angle, and femoral anteversion.")
B.Vt=s(["MCL instability","Lateral patellar tracking and patellofemoral pain","ACL deficiency","Posterior knee pain"],t.s)
B.a5X=new A.e("The Q-angle is measured from the ASIS to the center of the patella to the tibial tubercle. An increased Q-angle is associated with:",B.Vt,1,"An increased Q-angle (normal: ~14\xb0 males, ~17\xb0 females) creates a lateral vector force on the patella during quadriceps contraction, promoting lateral tracking and patellofemoral pain. Women have larger Q-angles due to wider pelves.")
B.UM=s(["Inferior pole of the patella","Tibial tubercle apophysis","Femoral condyle","Popliteal fossa"],t.s)
B.aaB=new A.e("Osgood-Schlatter disease involves inflammation at which location?",B.UM,1,"Osgood-Schlatter is a traction apophysitis at the tibial tubercle where the patellar tendon inserts. It occurs in active adolescents during growth spurts. Sinding-Larsen-Johansson is the counterpart at the inferior pole of the patella.")
B.U_=s(["Infrapatellar bursa","Semimembranosus-gastrocnemius bursa (knee joint capsule)","Pes anserine bursa","Prepatellar bursa"],t.s)
B.a7h=new A.e("Baker's cyst (popliteal cyst) is a fluid collection that communicates with which structure?",B.U_,1,"Baker's cysts arise from the semimembranosus-gastrocnemius bursa, which communicates with the knee joint through a one-way valve. They are usually secondary to intra-articular pathology (meniscal tears, arthritis). Rupture can mimic DVT (pseudothrombophlebitis).")
B.Y1=s(["Lateral knee","Posterior knee","Medial knee, approximately 2 inches below the joint line","Anterior knee at the tibial tubercle"],t.s)
B.abk=new A.e("Pes anserine bursitis causes pain at which location?",B.Y1,2,'The pes anserinus ("goose foot") is the conjoined tendon insertion of the sartorius, gracilis, and semitendinosus on the medial proximal tibia, ~2 inches below the joint line. Bursitis causes medial knee pain, especially with stair climbing. Common in obese women with knee OA.')
B.Nt=s([B.a5v,B.a5X,B.aaB,B.a7h,B.abk],t.b)
B.IJ=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Knee 2",B.Nt)
B.X0=s(["FEV1/FVC ratio <70%","Reduced total lung capacity with normal or increased FEV1/FVC ratio","Air trapping","Wheezing on examination"],t.s)
B.aap=new A.e("Restrictive lung disease is characterized by:",B.X0,1,"Restrictive disease: reduced TLC (lungs cannot fully expand). FEV1 and FVC both reduced proportionally, so FEV1/FVC ratio is preserved or increased (>70%). Causes: interstitial lung disease, chest wall deformity (kyphoscoliosis), neuromuscular disease (SCI, ALS, muscular dystrophy), obesity. Distinguished from obstructive disease by preserved ratio.")
B.Yf=s(["21%","40%","60%","100% only"],t.s)
B.a7r=new A.e("Oxygen toxicity can occur with prolonged FiO2 above:",B.Yf,2,"Prolonged FiO2 >60% risks oxygen toxicity: alveolar damage, inflammation, atelectasis (absorption atelectasis from nitrogen washout), and eventually ARDS. In COPD patients, even moderate supplemental O2 can suppress hypoxic ventilatory drive. Target SpO2 88-92% in COPD. Higher FiO2 is acceptable short-term in acute respiratory failure.")
B.Wp=s(["Supplemental oxygen alone","Continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP)","Bronchodilators","Chest physiotherapy"],t.s)
B.a91=new A.e("Sleep-disordered breathing (obstructive sleep apnea) is treated primarily with:",B.Wp,1,"CPAP: gold standard for OSA. Delivers continuous positive pressure through a mask, splinting the upper airway open and preventing pharyngeal collapse during sleep. Reduces apneic events, improves daytime sleepiness, reduces cardiovascular risk. BiPAP (bilevel) is used for patients who cannot tolerate CPAP or have hypoventilation syndromes.")
B.Nl=s(["Abruptly discontinuing mechanical ventilation","Progressive reduction of ventilator support with gradual increases in spontaneous breathing trials","Permanent ventilator dependence","Only using CPAP"],t.s)
B.a3d=new A.e("Ventilator weaning in rehabilitation involves:",B.Nl,1,"Ventilator weaning: progressive reduction of support through spontaneous breathing trials (SBT), pressure support reduction, or T-piece trials. Parameters for readiness: adequate oxygenation (PaO2/FiO2 >200), intact respiratory drive, manageable secretions, hemodynamic stability. Success markers: rapid shallow breathing index (RR/TV) <105, negative inspiratory force >-20 cmH2O.")
B.Vv=s(["Hypoxemia alone (PaO2 <60)","Hypoxemia WITH hypercapnia (PaO2 <60, PaCO2 >50)","Normal blood gases","Only elevated CO2"],t.s)
B.a8s=new A.e("Respiratory failure is classified as Type I or Type II. Type II is defined by:",B.Vv,1,"Type I (hypoxemic): PaO2 <60 mmHg with normal/low PaCO2 (V/Q mismatch, shunt). Type II (hypercapnic/ventilatory): PaO2 <60 WITH PaCO2 >50 mmHg (pump failure \u2014 the patient cannot ventilate adequately). Causes of Type II: COPD, neuromuscular disease, chest wall deformity, CNS depression. Treatment: address both oxygenation AND ventilation.")
B.Ol=s([B.aap,B.a7r,B.a91,B.a3d,B.a8s],t.b)
B.IK=new A.a6("Pulmonary Rehab 3",B.Ol)
B.TV=s(["3 hours","4.5 hours","6 hours","12 hours"],t.s)
B.a5m=new A.e("The window for IV tPA administration in acute ischemic stroke is within how many hours of symptom onset?",B.TV,1,"IV tPA (alteplase) can be administered within 4.5 hours of symptom onset in eligible patients. The earlier it is given, the better the outcomes. Mechanical thrombectomy may extend the window to 24 hours for large vessel occlusions.")
B.Qx=s(["Glasgow Coma Scale","NIH Stroke Scale (NIHSS)","Modified Rankin Scale","Barthel Index"],t.s)
B.a77=new A.e("Which standardized assessment tool is most commonly used to quantify stroke severity and guide treatment decisions?",B.Qx,1,"The NIH Stroke Scale (NIHSS) is the standard tool for quantifying stroke severity acutely. It scores 0-42, assessing consciousness, gaze, visual fields, facial palsy, motor function, ataxia, sensory, language, dysarthria, and extinction/inattention.")
B.Yw=s(["140/90 mmHg","160/100 mmHg","180/105 mmHg","220/120 mmHg"],t.s)
B.a6Y=new A.e("Blood pressure management in the first 24 hours after tPA administration requires maintaining BP below which threshold?",B.Yw,2,"After tPA administration, blood pressure must be maintained below 180/105 mmHg to reduce the risk of hemorrhagic transformation. In patients NOT receiving tPA, permissive hypertension up to 220/120 is generally allowed.")
B.Uy=s(["Immediate aggressive blood pressure lowering","Permissive hypertension \u2014 do not treat","Lower blood pressure to 140/90","Administer tPA"],t.s)
B.a7e=new A.e("A patient with acute ischemic stroke who did NOT receive tPA has a blood pressure of 200/110 mmHg. What is the appropriate management?",B.Uy,1,"In patients with acute ischemic stroke who did not receive tPA, permissive hypertension is allowed up to 220/120 mmHg. The elevated BP helps maintain cerebral perfusion to the ischemic penumbra. Only treat if BP exceeds 220/120.")
B.Zo=s(["Barthel Index","FIM (Functional Independence Measure)","Modified Rankin Scale (mRS)","NIH Stroke Scale"],t.s)
B.a6F=new A.e("Which functional outcome measure classifies disability on a scale of 0 (no symptoms) to 6 (death) and is the primary endpoint in most stroke trials?",B.Zo,2,"The Modified Rankin Scale (mRS) is the primary outcome measure in most stroke clinical trials. It ranges from 0 (no symptoms) to 6 (death), with scores of 0-2 generally considered a favorable outcome.")
B.ZS=s([B.a5m,B.a77,B.a6Y,B.a7e,B.a6F],t.b)
B.IL=new A.a6("Cerebrovascular Accident 4",B.ZS)
B.Zr=s(["A violation of patient autonomy","An exercise of patient autonomy through delegation","Evidence of lack of decisional capacity","Paternalism by the physician"],t.s)
B.a7y=new A.e("A patient with decisional capacity voluntarily asks their physician to make treatment decisions on their behalf. Which of the following best describes this situation?",B.Zr,1,"Autonomy includes the right to delegate decision-making. If a patient voluntarily and with understanding chooses to have someone else decide, that delegation itself is an exercise of autonomy, not a violation of it.")
B.YW=s(["Decisional capacity","Competence","Informed consent","Beneficence"],t.s)
B.a3P=new A.e("Which of the following is determined by a judge, not a physician?",B.YW,1,"Competence is a legal determination made by a judge. Physicians assess decisional capacity, which is a clinical assessment that is task-specific and time-dependent.")
B.Qf=s(["Justice and non-maleficence","Beneficence and autonomy","Non-maleficence and justice","Autonomy and competence"],t.s)
B.a7X=new A.e("A post-stroke patient with dysphagia is recommended a PEG tube but refuses, preferring oral intake despite aspiration risk. The physician respects this decision. Which ethical principles are in conflict?",B.Qf,1,"The PEG tube recommendation represents beneficence (preventing aspiration pneumonia), while the patient's refusal represents autonomy. In modern bioethics, autonomy generally prevails when the patient has capacity.")
B.U1=s(["Non-comparative justice","Distributive justice","Comparative justice","Procedural justice"],t.s)
B.a4a=new A.e("An inpatient rehab unit has one bed remaining and two consults: a 25-year-old with TBI and high recovery potential, and a 95-year-old with end-stage dementia unlikely to make functional gains. Allocating the bed based on recovery potential is an example of which type of justice?",B.U1,2,"Comparative justice involves weighing patients against each other based on need and potential benefit. Non-comparative justice applies equal rules regardless of individual need (e.g., insurance caps on therapy visits).")
B.Rq=s(["Patient competence or capacity","Voluntary decision-making","Written documentation signed by the patient","Disclosure of important information"],t.s)
B.a67=new A.e("Which of the following is NOT one of the four elements required for valid informed consent?",B.Rq,2,"The four elements of informed consent are: (1) patient competence/capacity, (2) voluntary decision-making, (3) disclosure of important information, and (4) authorization to proceed. Written documentation is good practice but not one of the four required elements.")
B.Zg=s([B.a7y,B.a3P,B.a7X,B.a4a,B.a67],t.b)
B.IM=new A.a6("Bioethics",B.Zg)
B.Z4=s(["95% of those who test positive truly have the disease","95% of those who have the disease will test positive","5% of healthy individuals will test positive","95% of all test results are correct"],t.s)
B.a8g=new A.e("A screening test has 95% sensitivity. This means:",B.Z4,1,"Sensitivity = true positive rate = proportion of people WITH the disease who test positive. A sensitivity of 95% means the test catches 95% of true cases. High sensitivity is important for screening (SnNOut: Sensitive test, Negative result, rules Out disease).")
B.SQ=s(["Screening large populations","Ruling out disease when negative","Confirming disease when positive","Estimating disease prevalence"],t.s)
B.a4j=new A.e("A test with high specificity is most useful for:",B.SQ,2,"High specificity means few false positives (SpPIn: Specific test, Positive result, rules In disease). Specific tests are best for confirming a diagnosis. Sensitive tests are best for screening/ruling out disease.")
B.Xh=s(["You fail to reject a false null hypothesis","You reject a true null hypothesis","The sample size is too small","The confidence interval is too wide"],t.s)
B.a8C=new A.e("A Type I error (alpha error) occurs when:",B.Xh,1,"Type I error (alpha/false positive) = rejecting a true null hypothesis (finding a difference when none exists). Type II error (beta/false negative) = failing to reject a false null hypothesis (missing a real difference). Standard alpha = 0.05.")
B.XP=s(["The probability of a Type I error","The probability of finding a difference when one truly exists","The size of the treatment effect","The confidence level of the study"],t.s)
B.a46=new A.e("Power of a study is defined as:",B.XP,1,"Power = 1 - beta = the probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis (detecting a real effect). Standard power is 0.80 (80%). Power is increased by: larger sample size, larger effect size, and higher alpha level.")
B.UE=s(["Excluded from the analysis","Analyzed as part of the treatment group regardless","Moved to the control group","Analyzed separately"],t.s)
B.a7F=new A.e("In an intention-to-treat analysis, patients who drop out of the treatment group are:",B.UE,1,"Intention-to-treat (ITT) analysis keeps all randomized participants in their original assigned groups regardless of compliance, withdrawal, or crossover. It preserves the benefits of randomization and provides a more conservative, real-world estimate of treatment effect.")
B.PL=s([B.a8g,B.a4j,B.a8C,B.a46,B.a7F],t.b)
B.IN=new A.a6("Biostatistics",B.PL)
B.Xd=s(["Posterior tibial translation","Anterior tibial translation","Valgus stress","Varus stress"],t.s)
B.a3i=new A.e("The anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) primarily resists:",B.Xd,1,"The ACL prevents anterior translation of the tibia on the femur. It is tested with the Lachman test (most sensitive) and anterior drawer test. ACL tears are common in cutting/pivoting sports and have a female predominance (3-6x higher risk).")
B.VB=s(["It tests at full extension","At 20-30\xb0 flexion, the hamstrings are relaxed and can't mask laxity","It tests the posterior cruciate ligament simultaneously","It requires less examiner skill"],t.s)
B.a4A=new A.e("The Lachman test is considered more sensitive than the anterior drawer test for ACL tears because:",B.VB,1,"The Lachman test is performed at 20-30\xb0 flexion where hamstring guarding is minimized. The anterior drawer (90\xb0 flexion) allows the hamstrings to act as ACL synergists, potentially masking a tear. Lachman sensitivity is ~85-95% vs ~60-70% for anterior drawer.")
B.Pd=s(["ACL, LCL, and lateral meniscus","ACL, MCL, and medial meniscus","PCL, MCL, and lateral meniscus","ACL, PCL, and medial meniscus"],t.s)
B.a3I=new A.e('The "unhappy triad" (O\'Donoghue triad) involves injury to which three structures?',B.Pd,1,"The classic unhappy triad: ACL tear + MCL tear + medial meniscus tear. It results from a valgus force with external rotation (e.g., a lateral blow to the knee). Note: some modern literature shows the lateral meniscus may be more commonly injured than medial.")
B.OP=s(["ACL","PCL","Meniscus","MCL"],t.s)
B.a6Q=new A.e("The McMurray test evaluates which knee structure?",B.OP,2,"McMurray's test: with the knee flexed, apply valgus + external rotation (medial meniscus) or varus + internal rotation (lateral meniscus) while extending. A pop or click with pain = positive for meniscal tear.")
B.Qe=s(["Lachman test","Anterior drawer test","Posterior drawer test / posterior sag sign","Pivot shift test"],t.s)
B.a4r=new A.e("The PCL is best tested with which maneuver?",B.Qe,2,"The posterior drawer test: at 90\xb0 flexion, push the tibia posteriorly. PCL tears also show a posterior sag sign \u2014 with knees flexed at 90\xb0, the tibial plateau sags posteriorly compared to the uninjured side. Dashboard injuries (tibia pushed back) are the classic mechanism.")
B.Ou=s([B.a3i,B.a4A,B.a3I,B.a6Q,B.a4r],t.b)
B.IO=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Knee 1",B.Ou)
B.Mk=s(["At Guyon's canal","5-8 cm proximal to the wrist","At the cubital tunnel","At the axilla"],t.s)
B.aay=new A.e("The dorsal ulnar cutaneous (DUC) nerve branches from the ulnar nerve at what location?",B.Mk,1,"The DUC branches 5-8 cm proximal to the wrist and does NOT travel through Guyon's canal. This is a critical landmark: DUC abnormal = lesion at or above the elbow. DUC normal = lesion at wrist (Guyon's canal).")
B.U4=s(["First dorsal interosseous","Adductor pollicis","Opponens pollicis","Flexor pollicis longus"],t.s)
B.a3u=new A.e("Froment's sign tests for weakness of which muscle?",B.U4,1,"Froment's sign: patient tries to hold paper between thumb and index finger. The adductor pollicis (ulnar nerve) is weak, so the patient compensates by flexing the thumb IP joint using FPL (median nerve). IP flexion during pinch = positive Froment sign = ulnar neuropathy.")
B.Wz=s(["Radiation causes fasciculations; tumor causes fibrillations","Radiation shows myokymic discharges; tumor is typically painful with Horner syndrome","They cannot be differentiated on EMG","Radiation affects lower trunk; tumor affects upper trunk"],t.s)
B.abJ=new A.e("How do you differentiate radiation-induced brachial plexopathy from tumor recurrence on EMG?",B.Wz,1,"Key differentiators: Radiation plexopathy = painless, upper trunk predominant, myokymic discharges (marching soldiers on EMG). Tumor recurrence = painful, lower trunk predominant, may have Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhydrosis). Myokymia is the EMG key.")
B.P9=s(["Thumb and index finger","Index and middle fingers","Ring and little fingers","All five digits equally"],t.s)
B.a6S=new A.e("Ulnar claw hand affects primarily which digits?",B.P9,2,"Ulnar claw hand primarily affects the 4th and 5th digits. The unopposed EDC hyperextends the MCPs, and passive tension on the flexor tendons curls the PIP/DIP joints. The index and middle fingers are relatively spared because their lumbricals are median-innervated.")
B.M5=s(["Inability to cross the fingers","The small finger drifting into abduction at rest due to weak interossei","Weakness of thumb opposition","Inability to extend the wrist"],t.s)
B.a6c=new A.e("Wartenberg's sign is demonstrated by:",B.M5,1,"Wartenberg's sign: the small finger drifts into abduction because the third palmar interosseous (ulnar nerve) is weak and cannot adduct the 5th digit against the unopposed action of EDM. It is a subtle early sign of ulnar neuropathy.")
B.OH=s([B.aay,B.a3u,B.abJ,B.a6S,B.a6c],t.b)
B.IP=new A.a6("Electrodiagnostic 9",B.OH)
B.Wv=s(["10-20%","30-40%","50-80%","90-100%"],t.s)
B.abt=new A.e("Post-stroke shoulder pain occurs in approximately what percentage of hemiplegic patients?",B.Wv,2,"Hemiplegic shoulder pain is extremely common, occurring in approximately 50-80% of stroke patients. It is one of the most significant barriers to rehabilitation progress and has multiple contributing factors.")
B.Zq=s(["Aggressive passive range of motion","Proper positioning and support of the affected arm","Corticosteroid injection","Surgical repair"],t.s)
B.a5x=new A.e("Which of the following is the most important initial intervention to prevent hemiplegic shoulder subluxation?",B.Zq,1,"Proper positioning and arm support (using arm troughs, lap boards, or slings when upright) is the most important initial intervention. Aggressive passive ROM, particularly overhead pulleys, should be avoided as they can worsen impingement and pain.")
B.S6=s(["First-line therapy for shoulder subluxation","Beneficial for increasing ROM","Contraindicated due to risk of impingement and pain","Only appropriate for flaccid stage"],t.s)
B.a8P=new A.e("Overhead pulleys for hemiplegic shoulder rehabilitation are generally considered:",B.S6,2,"Overhead pulleys are generally contraindicated in hemiplegic shoulders. They can cause impingement, rotator cuff injury, and worsen pain because the patient cannot control the affected arm during the motion.")
B.OW=s(["Flaccidity with no voluntary movement","Spasticity appears, synergy patterns emerge","Spasticity peaks, voluntary synergy patterns","Spasticity decreases, isolated movements emerge"],t.s)
B.ac0=new A.e("Brunnstrom stages of motor recovery describe the progression from flaccidity through spasticity to voluntary movement. What does Stage 3 represent?",B.OW,2,"Brunnstrom Stage 3 is characterized by peak spasticity and the ability to voluntarily initiate synergy patterns. Stage 1 = flaccidity, Stage 2 = spasticity begins, Stage 3 = spasticity peaks with voluntary synergies, Stage 4 = spasticity decreases with some isolated movement.")
B.Zv=s(["NSAIDs","Opioids","Anticonvulsants or tricyclic antidepressants","Muscle relaxants"],t.s)
B.a97=new A.e("Central post-stroke pain (thalamic pain syndrome) is best treated initially with which class of medications?",B.Zv,2,"Central post-stroke pain (Dejerine-Roussy syndrome) is a neuropathic pain condition best treated with anticonvulsants (gabapentin, pregabalin) or tricyclic antidepressants (amitriptyline). It does not respond well to NSAIDs or opioids.")
B.Yo=s([B.abt,B.a5x,B.a8P,B.ac0,B.a97],t.b)
B.IQ=new A.a6("Cerebrovascular Accident 5",B.Yo)
B.Ow=s(["Joint destruction only","Dry eyes (keratoconjunctivitis sicca) and dry mouth (xerostomia) from autoimmune destruction of exocrine glands","Skin rash only","Isolated lung disease"],t.s)
B.a9W=new A.e("Sj\xf6gren syndrome is characterized by:",B.Ow,1,"Sj\xf6gren: autoimmune destruction of lacrimal and salivary glands causing sicca symptoms (dry eyes, dry mouth). Can be primary or secondary (associated with RA, SLE). Anti-SSA (Ro) and Anti-SSB (La) antibodies. Complications: dental caries, oral candidiasis, parotid enlargement, and increased lymphoma risk. Treatment: artificial tears, sialogogues (pilocarpine).")
B.VF=s(["Exercise should last exactly 2 hours","If joint pain persists >2 hours after activity, the activity was too intense and should be modified","Pain should be ignored for 2 hours","Take medication 2 hours before exercise"],t.s)
B.a6g=new A.e("The 2-hour pain rule in arthritis rehabilitation means:",B.VF,1,"The 2-hour pain rule: if a patient has increased joint pain lasting >2 hours after completing an activity/exercise, the intensity was too much. The next session should be reduced in duration, intensity, or both. This prevents disease flares while maintaining activity. If pain resolves within 2 hours, the activity level is appropriate.")
B.TM=s(["Continuous joint swelling","Episodic vasospasm of digital arteries causing white \u2192 blue \u2192 red color changes in response to cold or stress","Only large vessel disease","Chronic skin thickening of the trunk"],t.s)
B.a4H=new A.e("Raynaud phenomenon involves:",B.TM,1,"Raynaud: episodic vasospasm of digital arteries triggered by cold or emotional stress. Classic triphasic color change: white (ischemia/vasospasm) \u2192 blue (cyanosis/deoxygenation) \u2192 red (reactive hyperemia/reperfusion). Primary (idiopathic, benign) or secondary (associated with scleroderma, SLE, other CTDs). Treatment: avoid cold, calcium channel blockers (nifedipine).")
B.MH=s(["Responds well to steroids","Is the most common inflammatory myopathy in patients >50, affects distal AND proximal muscles, and is resistant to immunosuppression","Only affects children","Causes skin rash"],t.s)
B.abY=new A.e("Inclusion body myositis (IBM) differs from polymyositis in that IBM:",B.MH,1,"IBM: most common inflammatory myopathy in adults >50 years. Unique features: asymmetric weakness, DISTAL involvement (finger flexors, knee extensors \u2014 unlike PM/DM which are purely proximal), slowly progressive, POOR response to immunosuppressive therapy. Pathology: rimmed vacuoles with inclusion bodies. Often misdiagnosed as treatment-resistant polymyositis.")
B.YB=s(["Cold water reduces inflammation","Buoyancy reduces joint loading while warm water decreases pain and spasticity","It replaces medication therapy","It is only for mild disease"],t.s)
B.a5q=new A.e("Aquatic therapy is beneficial in arthritis because:",B.YB,1,"Aquatic therapy: buoyancy reduces joint loading (body weight reduced ~50% at waist depth, ~90% at neck depth), enabling exercise with less pain. Warm water (92-96\xb0F) relaxes muscles, reduces pain, and improves ROM. Hydrostatic pressure reduces edema. Ideal for patients who cannot tolerate land-based exercise. Contraindicated: open wounds, unstable cardiac conditions.")
B.YY=s([B.a9W,B.a6g,B.a4H,B.abY,B.a5q],t.b)
B.IR=new A.a6("Rheumatology 5",B.YY)
B.Xs=s(["Vibration and proprioception","Light touch and pin prick (sharp/dull)","Temperature and deep pressure","Two-point discrimination and vibration"],t.s)
B.a9S=new A.e("The ASIA sensory examination tests which two modalities at each dermatome?",B.Xs,1,"The ASIA exam tests light touch (anterior spinothalamic/dorsal columns) and pin prick/sharp-dull (lateral spinothalamic) at each key sensory point (28 dermatomes bilaterally). Each is scored 0 (absent), 1 (impaired), or 2 (normal). Total sensory score ranges from 0 to 112 per modality.")
B.Nq=s(["The level of the vertebral fracture","The most caudal segment with normal motor AND sensory function on both sides","The highest level with any abnormality","The level determined by MRI"],t.s)
B.a5T=new A.e("The neurological level of injury (NLI) is defined as:",B.Nq,1,"The NLI is the most caudal segment with normal sensory AND motor function bilaterally. Motor level requires grade \u22653 with the segment above being grade 5. Sensory and motor levels can differ, and right and left sides can differ. The single NLI is the most rostral of all these levels.")
B.Nn=s(["Testing knee reflexes","Checking for voluntary anal contraction and deep anal pressure and S4-S5 light touch/pin prick","Performing a rectal exam for prostate","Testing plantar flexion strength"],t.s)
B.ab8=new A.e("Sacral sparing (indicative of incomplete injury) is assessed by:",B.Nn,1,"Sacral sparing = any preserved function at S4-S5: light touch, pin prick, deep anal pressure (sensory), or voluntary anal contraction (motor). Sacral sparing indicates an incomplete injury (AIS B-D) because the most central fibers (sacral) traverse the cord periphery and can be partially spared.")
B.WV=s(["Intact upper extremity function, intercostal muscles partially intact, no lower extremity function","No voluntary function below the chin","Intact hand function and walking ability","Intact diaphragm but no intercostal function"],t.s)
B.a6I=new A.e("A T10 ASIA A paraplegic would be expected to have:",B.WV,0,"T10 paraplegia: full upper extremity function, intact diaphragm, intact upper intercostals (down to T10), partial abdominal muscles (upper rectus intact). No lower extremity motor function. Bowel/bladder management required. Uses manual wheelchair. May ambulate short distances with KAFOs and significant energy expenditure.")
B.Pn=s(["Curing the spinal cord injury","Enhancing muscle function for exercise, standing, or limited ambulation","Replacing the need for wheelchairs permanently","Treating autonomic dysreflexia"],t.s)
B.abE=new A.e("Functional electrical stimulation (FES) in SCI is used primarily for:",B.Pn,1,"FES applies electrical stimulation to paralyzed muscles to produce functional movements. Uses include FES cycling (cardiovascular fitness, bone density), FES-assisted standing/walking (limited community distances), and FES hand grasp systems. It requires intact lower motor neurons (LMN) to be effective.")
B.Uu=s([B.a9S,B.a5T,B.ab8,B.a6I,B.abE],t.b)
B.IS=new A.a6("Spinal Cord Injury 7",B.Uu)
B.Q_=s(["Motor coordination only","Social communication/interaction AND restricted/repetitive behaviors","Language only","Cognitive function exclusively"],t.s)
B.a8i=new A.e("Autism spectrum disorder is characterized by deficits in:",B.Q_,1,"DSM-5 ASD criteria require deficits in BOTH: (1) social communication and social interaction across contexts, and (2) restricted, repetitive patterns of behavior, interests, or activities. Symptoms must be present in early development. Severity levels 1-3 (requiring support \u2192 requiring very substantial support).")
B.XC=s(["X-ray at birth","Barlow and Ortolani maneuvers in newborns; ultrasound at 4-6 weeks","MRI at 6 months","CT scan at 1 year"],t.s)
B.a4x=new A.e("Developmental dysplasia of the hip (DDH) is best screened for using:",B.XC,1,'Barlow (adduction/posterior pressure dislocates an unstable hip) and Ortolani (abduction reduces a dislocated hip with a "clunk"). Ultrasound is the imaging modality of choice before 4-6 months (femoral head is cartilaginous, not visible on X-ray). Risk factors: breech presentation, female sex, family history. Pavlik harness is first-line treatment.')
B.TT=s(["A fracture of the femoral neck","Idiopathic avascular necrosis of the femoral head in children ages 4-10","Slipped capital femoral epiphysis","Hip dysplasia in newborns"],t.s)
B.a9s=new A.e("Legg-Calv\xe9-Perthes disease is:",B.TT,1,"Legg-Calv\xe9-Perthes: idiopathic AVN of the femoral head in children (peak 4-8 years), 4-5x more common in boys. Presents with limp, hip/knee pain, limited ROM (especially internal rotation and abduction). Management: containment of the femoral head within the acetabulum (bracing or surgery). Younger onset (<6 years) = better prognosis.")
B.QG=s(["Systemic JIA","Polyarticular JIA","Oligoarticular (pauciarticular) JIA","Enthesitis-related JIA"],t.s)
B.acr=new A.e("Juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JIA) is the most common chronic rheumatic disease in children. The most common subtype is:",B.QG,2,"Oligoarticular JIA (\u22644 joints in first 6 months) is most common (~50%). Typically affects large joints (knee) in young girls. KEY complication: uveitis (asymptomatic eye inflammation) \u2014 requires regular slit-lamp screening. ANA-positive patients at highest uveitis risk. Polyarticular affects \u22655 joints. Systemic (Still disease) has quotidian fevers and salmon-colored rash.")
B.Ru=s(["Cervical spine fracture","Congenital muscular torticollis (SCM tightness)","Brain tumor","Meningitis"],t.s)
B.a8t=new A.e("Torticollis in an infant is most commonly caused by:",B.Ru,1,'Congenital muscular torticollis: tightness/fibrosis of the SCM causes head tilt toward the affected side with chin rotation to the opposite side. Often from intrauterine positioning. May have a palpable SCM mass ("sternocleidomastoid tumor"). Treatment: stretching exercises, positioning. Most resolve by 1 year. Plagiocephaly (skull flattening) can co-occur.')
B.XE=s([B.a8i,B.a4x,B.a9s,B.acr,B.a8t],t.b)
B.IT=new A.a6("Pediatrics 5",B.XE)
B.UP=s(["Joint inflammation visible on imaging","Widespread musculoskeletal pain, fatigue, sleep disturbance, and cognitive difficulties without structural pathology","Elevated inflammatory markers","Positive autoimmune antibodies"],t.s)
B.a8b=new A.e("Fibromyalgia is characterized by:",B.UP,1,'Fibromyalgia: central sensitization syndrome with widespread pain (\u22653 months), fatigue, non-restorative sleep, and cognitive dysfunction ("fibro fog"). Labs and imaging are NORMAL. 2010/2016 ACR criteria replaced tender points with widespread pain index and symptom severity scale. Treatment: exercise (strongest evidence), duloxetine, pregabalin, milnacipran, CBT.')
B.Xa=s(["Opioid receptor activation","Serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibition (SNRI) enhancing descending inhibitory pain pathways","COX inhibition","Muscle relaxation"],t.s)
B.a62=new A.e("Duloxetine (Cymbalta) treats pain in fibromyalgia through:",B.Xa,1,"Duloxetine (SNRI): inhibits reuptake of both serotonin and norepinephrine, strengthening descending inhibitory pain pathways from the brainstem. FDA-approved for fibromyalgia, diabetic peripheral neuropathy, and chronic musculoskeletal pain. Also treats comorbid depression/anxiety. Three FDA-approved drugs for fibromyalgia: duloxetine, milnacipran, pregabalin.")
B.Q6=s(["Acute postoperative pain","Chronic neuropathic pain (failed back surgery syndrome, CRPS) refractory to conservative treatment","All types of headache","Acute disc herniation"],t.s)
B.a7G=new A.e("Spinal cord stimulation (SCS) is indicated for:",B.Q6,1,"SCS: electrodes placed in the epidural space deliver electrical impulses that modulate pain signals (gate control theory). Primary indications: failed back surgery syndrome (most common), CRPS, peripheral neuropathy. A trial period (1 week) must demonstrate \u226550% pain reduction before permanent implant. Does not work for nociceptive pain.")
B.Pi=s(["Is always generalized","Is regional with specific trigger points that reproduce the pain pattern","Has elevated CRP","Requires surgery"],t.s)
B.a37=new A.e("Myofascial pain syndrome differs from fibromyalgia in that myofascial pain:",B.Pi,1,"Myofascial pain: REGIONAL pain with identifiable trigger points in taut bands that reproduce the specific pain pattern on palpation. Fibromyalgia: WIDESPREAD pain without specific trigger points, associated with central sensitization. They can coexist. Myofascial pain responds well to trigger point treatment; fibromyalgia requires a multimodal central-acting approach.")
B.Se=s(["Personal history of substance abuse","Family history of addiction","Younger age","Taking opioids for 3 days after surgery"],t.s)
B.a9N=new A.e("Opioid use disorder (OUD) risk factors include all EXCEPT:",B.Se,3,"OUD risk factors: personal/family history of substance abuse, psychiatric comorbidity (depression, PTSD, anxiety), younger age, history of childhood trauma, and social factors. Short-term opioid use (<7 days for acute pain) has low addiction risk. Chronic opioid therapy (>90 days), high daily doses (>50 MME), and concurrent benzodiazepines increase risk substantially.")
B.SJ=s([B.a8b,B.a62,B.a7G,B.a37,B.a9N],t.b)
B.IU=new A.a6("Pain 3",B.SJ)
B.XB=s(["Only motor function","Motor and cognitive functional independence in children 6 months to 7 years","Academic performance","Behavioral problems"],t.s)
B.a5M=new A.e("The WeeFIM (Functional Independence Measure for Children) assesses:",B.XB,1,"WeeFIM: pediatric version of the FIM, assessing 18 items across self-care, mobility, and cognition domains. Each item scored 1-7 (total 18-126). Used for children 6 months to 7 years (or older children with developmental disabilities functioning below age 7). Tracks functional progress in rehabilitation.")
B.U5=s(["It is always bilateral","The child CAN walk flat-footed when asked, and there are no UMN signs","It is more severe","It always requires surgery"],t.s)
B.acf=new A.e("Idiopathic toe-walking differs from CP-related toe-walking because:",B.U5,1,"Idiopathic toe-walking: diagnosis of exclusion. Children can walk flat-footed when asked (volitional), have full passive ankle dorsiflexion, normal neurological exam (no spasticity, hyperreflexia, or clonus), and normal development. It is a habit. Treatment: stretching, serial casting if heel cords tighten. Must rule out mild CP, muscular dystrophy, and autism.")
B.Q1=s(["Continued observation only","Microsurgical nerve repair/grafting","Amputation","Botulinum toxin injection"],t.s)
B.a4_=new A.e("Neonatal brachial plexus palsy that fails to recover biceps function by 3-6 months should be considered for:",B.Q1,1,"If biceps function (elbow flexion against gravity) does not recover by 3-6 months, the prognosis for spontaneous recovery is poor, and microsurgical intervention (nerve grafting, neurotization) should be considered. Earlier surgery (by 6-9 months) generally yields better outcomes. Most Erb palsy recovers by 3 months; those that do not are surgical candidates.")
B.NI=s(["Moving between hospital floors","Planned transition of adolescents with disabilities from pediatric to adult healthcare systems","Changing therapists","Moving from ICU to rehab unit"],t.s)
B.abQ=new A.e("Transitional care in pediatric rehabilitation refers to:",B.NI,1,"Transition: planned process of moving adolescents/young adults with chronic conditions from pediatric to adult healthcare. Should begin by age 12-14. Addresses medical management, self-advocacy, vocational planning, sexuality, and independence. Poor transition leads to gaps in care, lost to follow-up, and preventable complications.")
B.Zk=s(["The Gustilo classification","The ISO/ISPO classification system (transverse vs. longitudinal)","The Neer classification","The Salter-Harris classification"],t.s)
B.a9V=new A.e("Congenital limb deficiency classification uses:",B.Zk,1,"Congenital limb deficiencies are classified as transverse (limb appears amputated at a specific level \u2014 all elements distal are absent) or longitudinal (specific bone/ray is absent \u2014 e.g., radial deficiency, fibular hemimelia). This replaces old eponymous terms. Fibular hemimelia is the most common long bone deficiency.")
B.PN=s([B.a5M,B.acf,B.a4_,B.abQ,B.a9V],t.b)
B.IV=new A.a6("Pediatrics 9",B.PN)
B.XR=s(["Post-ganglionic (distal to DRG)","Pre-ganglionic (proximal to DRG)","At the neuromuscular junction","In the peripheral nerve trunk"],t.s)
B.a5A=new A.e("A patient has clinical sensory loss in a dermatomal distribution but normal SNAPs. The lesion is most likely:",B.XR,1,"Pre-ganglionic lesions (radiculopathy) preserve the SNAP because the sensory neuron cell body in the DRG and its peripheral axon remain intact. Post-ganglionic lesions (plexopathy, peripheral neuropathy) damage the axon distal to the DRG, causing abnormal SNAPs.")
B.Tq=s(["25%","40%","50%","75%"],t.s)
B.abM=new A.e("Conduction block on NCS is defined as a drop in CMAP amplitude of greater than what percentage between distal and proximal stimulation sites?",B.Tq,2,"Conduction block is defined as >50% drop in CMAP amplitude between proximal and distal stimulation sites. It is a hallmark of focal demyelination and indicates that some axons fail to conduct past the lesion site.")
B.T8=s(["Grade 1 (neuropraxia)","Grade 2 (axonotmesis)","Grade 3","Grade 4"],t.s)
B.a92=new A.e("In the Sunderland classification, which grade involves disruption of the endoneurium with intact perineurium?",B.T8,2,"Sunderland Grade 3 involves disruption of the endoneurium with intact perineurium. Grade 1 = neuropraxia (myelin only). Grade 2 = axonotmesis (axon damaged, endoneurium intact). Grade 4 = perineurium damaged. Grade 5 = complete transection (neurotmesis).")
B.WM=s(["Axonal loss of large fibers","Different conduction velocities of demyelinated fibers","Neuromuscular junction block","Electrode placement errors"],t.s)
B.a7Y=new A.e("Temporal dispersion on NCS is caused by:",B.WM,1,"Temporal dispersion occurs when different fibers conduct at different speeds due to variable degrees of demyelination. Fast and slow fibers arrive at the recording electrode at different times, spreading out and reducing the CMAP amplitude with a widened, polyphasic waveform.")
B.SH=s(["Axonal: slow velocity, normal amplitude","Demyelinating: low amplitude, normal velocity","Axonal: low amplitude, normal velocity","Both show identical findings"],t.s)
B.a8u=new A.e("The key NCS finding that distinguishes axonal injury from demyelinating injury is:",B.SH,2,"Axonal injury = reduced amplitude (fewer functioning axons) with normal or near-normal conduction velocity. Demyelinating injury = slow conduction velocity with initially preserved amplitude. This distinction is fundamental to all EDX interpretation.")
B.WC=s([B.a5A,B.abM,B.a92,B.a7Y,B.a8u],t.b)
B.IW=new A.a6("Electrodiagnostic 4",B.WC)
B.S5=s(["Only musculoskeletal medicine","All core PM&R domains including neurological, musculoskeletal, and medical rehabilitation","Only electrodiagnostic medicine","Only spinal cord injury management"],t.s)
B.abR=new A.e("The ABPMR written board examination tests knowledge across:",B.S5,1,"The ABPMR written exam covers all core PM&R domains: neurological rehabilitation (TBI, SCI, stroke), musculoskeletal medicine, electrodiagnostic medicine, pediatric rehabilitation, medical rehabilitation (cardiac, pulmonary, cancer), pain management, prosthetics/orthotics, and foundational sciences (anatomy, physiology, pharmacology).")
B.Xc=s(["0 to 100","18 to 126","1 to 7","0 to 10"],t.s)
B.a3b=new A.e("Functional Independence Measure (FIM) scores range from:",B.Xc,1,"FIM: 18 items (13 motor + 5 cognitive), each scored 1-7. Total range: 18 (total dependence) to 126 (complete independence). Scoring: 1 = total assist, 2 = maximal assist, 3 = moderate assist, 4 = minimal contact assist, 5 = supervision, 6 = modified independence (device), 7 = complete independence. It is the most widely used functional outcome measure in rehabilitation.")
B.Py=s(["Case reports \u2192 cohort \u2192 RCT \u2192 meta-analysis","Systematic review/meta-analysis \u2192 RCT \u2192 cohort \u2192 case-control \u2192 case series \u2192 expert opinion","Expert opinion \u2192 cohort \u2192 RCT","All study designs are equivalent"],t.s)
B.aaL=new A.e("Evidence-based medicine levels of evidence rank from strongest to weakest as:",B.Py,1,"Evidence pyramid (strongest to weakest): systematic review/meta-analysis of RCTs (Level I) \u2192 individual RCTs (Level II) \u2192 cohort studies (Level III) \u2192 case-control studies (Level IV) \u2192 case series/case reports (Level V) \u2192 expert opinion (Level VI). Higher levels provide stronger evidence for clinical decision-making.")
B.MQ=s(["Only body structure and function","Body functions/structures, activities, participation, environmental factors, and personal factors","Only disease diagnosis","Only disability percentage"],t.s)
B.a3B=new A.e("The ICF (International Classification of Functioning, Disability and Health) framework includes:",B.MQ,1,"ICF (WHO model): health and disability framework with components: (1) Body functions and structures (impairments), (2) Activities (limitations), (3) Participation (restrictions), (4) Environmental factors (barriers/facilitators), (5) Personal factors. Replaces the older ICIDH model. Emphasizes functioning and contextual factors rather than just disability.")
B.MP=s(["To only perform procedures","To serve as the team leader coordinating medical management and rehabilitation goals across all disciplines","To provide psychological counseling only","To handle administrative duties only"],t.s)
B.a4i=new A.e("The PM&R physician's role in the interdisciplinary team is:",B.MP,1,"The physiatrist leads the interdisciplinary rehabilitation team: coordinates medical management, sets rehabilitation goals, prescribes therapies, manages complications, and integrates input from PT, OT, speech therapy, neuropsychology, social work, recreation therapy, and nursing. The physiatrist ensures all disciplines work toward unified patient-centered goals and makes disposition decisions.")
B.Tg=s([B.abR,B.a3b,B.aaL,B.a3B,B.a4i],t.b)
B.IX=new A.a6("The Board Exam",B.Tg)
B.XO=s(["Sedentary K0-K1 ambulators","Active K3-K4 ambulators who need energy return for walking and running","Non-ambulatory patients","Patients who refuse therapy"],t.s)
B.aaH=new A.e("Energy-storing prosthetic feet (dynamic response feet) are best for:",B.XO,1,"Energy-storing feet (Flex-Foot, Carbon fiber designs): the keel deforms during stance (storing energy) and recoils during push-off (returning energy). This reduces metabolic cost and enables higher activity levels. Best for K3-K4 ambulators. The carbon fiber leaf spring design revolutionized prosthetic feet for active amputees.")
B.YV=s(["Loose socket fit","Active negative pressure between the liner and socket wall, reducing pistoning and improving proprioception","Only cosmetic benefit","The same effect as a supracondylar strap"],t.s)
B.a8y=new A.e("Vacuum-assisted suspension (elevated vacuum) in prosthetics provides:",B.YV,1,"Elevated vacuum (e-vac): a pump maintains active negative pressure in the space between liner and socket. Benefits: eliminates pistoning, reduces volume fluctuation, improves proprioception, better residual limb health (prevents edema and skin breakdown). Considered the most advanced suspension for transtibial prosthetics.")
B.Wr=s(["Good prosthetic fit","The prosthesis is too long or has inadequate knee flexion in swing","The prosthesis is too short","Normal gait compensation"],t.s)
B.a3E=new A.e("In prosthetic gait analysis, vaulting (rising on the sound-side toes) suggests:",B.Wr,1,"Vaulting: the patient rises onto the toes of the sound limb during swing phase of the prosthetic side. Causes: prosthesis too long, inadequate knee flexion during swing (excessive friction, inadequate suspension), or fear of stumbling. Other swing-phase deviations: circumduction and lateral trunk lean also suggest length or clearance issues.")
B.Z0=s(["Socket is too tight proximally","Socket is too loose \u2014 the limb is pistoning and bottoming out","The foot is too stiff","The alignment is perfect"],t.s)
B.a3w=new A.e("Socket fit issues causing distal end pain in a transtibial prosthesis most likely indicate:",B.Z0,1,"Distal end pain: usually from the residual limb settling too deeply into a loose socket (bottoming out/pistoning). The distal tibia impacts the socket bottom. Solutions: add socks to restore fit, re-evaluate suspension, consider socket replacement if volume loss is significant. Poor suspension allows pistoning that causes this problem.")
B.Yk=s(["Bone healing","Myoelectric prosthesis control by providing additional EMG signal sites","Passive ROM","Cosmetic appearance only"],t.s)
B.a55=new A.e("Targeted muscle reinnervation (TMR) in upper extremity amputees improves:",B.Yk,1,'TMR: residual nerves are surgically transferred to denervated chest or upper arm muscles. These muscles then serve as biological amplifiers of motor intent \u2014 the patient "thinks" about hand open/close, and the reinnervated muscles produce distinct EMG signals for intuitive myoelectric prosthetic control. Also reduces neuroma pain.')
B.S1=s([B.aaH,B.a8y,B.a3E,B.a3w,B.a55],t.b)
B.IY=new A.a6("Prosthetics & Orthotics 9",B.S1)
B.W4=s(["Hepatotoxicity","Seizures","Renal failure","Pulmonary embolism"],t.s)
B.a7d=new A.e("Brain tumor patients undergoing rehabilitation should be monitored for which common complication?",B.W4,1,"Seizures are a very common complication in brain tumor patients (occurring in 20-40% of cases). Antiepileptic medications are frequently required. Therapy programs should be adjusted to ensure patient safety during activities.")
B.OS=s(["0 to 5","0 to 10","0 to 100","1 to 7"],t.s)
B.a4U=new A.e("The Karnofsky Performance Status scale ranges from:",B.OS,2,"The Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS) scale ranges from 0 (dead) to 100 (normal, no complaints). It is widely used in oncology to assess functional status. A KPS of 70+ generally indicates ability to care for self; below 50 indicates need for significant assistance.")
B.Tm=s(["Increase risk of metastasis","Have no effect on fatigue","Reduce fatigue and improve quality of life","Be contraindicated during chemotherapy"],t.s)
B.abN=new A.e("Exercise during cancer treatment has been shown to:",B.Tm,2,"Exercise during cancer treatment is the best-studied intervention for cancer-related fatigue. It reduces fatigue, improves quality of life, maintains muscle mass, and may improve treatment tolerance. It is not contraindicated during chemotherapy in most patients.")
B.Sl=s(["Cervical spine","Thoracic spine","Lumbar spine","Sacral spine"],t.s)
B.a5_=new A.e("Spinal cord compression from metastatic cancer most commonly involves which region?",B.Sl,1,"Metastatic spinal cord compression most commonly occurs in the thoracic spine (approximately 70% of cases), followed by the lumbosacral spine, then cervical spine. This reflects the relative volume of the thoracic spine and common metastatic spread patterns.")
B.VN=s(["Lung, liver, pancreas, colon","Breast, prostate, lung, kidney, thyroid","Brain, ovarian, gastric, esophageal","Melanoma, sarcoma, lymphoma, leukemia"],t.s)
B.a5Q=new A.e("Which cancers most commonly metastasize to bone?",B.VN,1,'The cancers most likely to metastasize to bone can be remembered with the mnemonic "BLT with a Kosher Pickle" \u2014 Breast, Lung, Thyroid, Kidney, Prostate. These account for the vast majority of skeletal metastases.')
B.YK=s([B.a7d,B.a4U,B.abN,B.a5_,B.a5Q],t.b)
B.IZ=new A.a6("Cancer Rehab 3",B.YK)
B.UF=s(["Deltoid ligament","Calcaneofibular ligament","Anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL)","Posterior talofibular ligament"],t.s)
B.a8q=new A.e("The most commonly injured ligament in ankle sprains is:",B.UF,2,"The ATFL is the weakest lateral ankle ligament and the most commonly injured in inversion ankle sprains. It is taut in plantarflexion and resists anterior translation of the talus. The injury sequence is ATFL \u2192 CFL \u2192 PTFL.")
B.OO=s(["Deltoid ligament","Calcaneofibular ligament","Anterior talofibular ligament","Syndesmosis"],t.s)
B.a7i=new A.e("The anterior drawer test of the ankle primarily assesses the integrity of which structure?",B.OO,2,"The anterior drawer test assesses the ATFL by stabilizing the tibia and pulling the calcaneus/talus anteriorly. Excessive anterior translation compared to the uninjured side indicates ATFL insufficiency.")
B.Xv=s(["Anterior talofibular ligament","Achilles tendon insertion","Posterior edge of the distal 6 cm of the fibula (lateral malleolus)","Anterior tibialis tendon"],t.s)
B.a9e=new A.e("The Ottawa Ankle Rules state that ankle X-rays are indicated if there is bone tenderness at which of the following locations?",B.Xv,2,"Ottawa Ankle Rules indicate X-ray if there is: bone tenderness at the posterior edge of the distal 6 cm of the lateral malleolus OR medial malleolus, OR inability to bear weight for 4 steps immediately after injury and in the ED.")
B.VD=s(["Anterior talofibular ligament","Deltoid ligament","Tibiofibular syndesmosis","Spring ligament"],t.s)
B.aaU=new A.e("A high ankle sprain involves injury to which structure?",B.VD,2,"A high ankle sprain involves the tibiofibular syndesmosis (the interosseous membrane and anterior/posterior tibiofibular ligaments connecting the distal tibia and fibula). It is more serious than a lateral ankle sprain and requires longer recovery. The squeeze test and external rotation test help diagnose it.")
B.Zu=s(["Lateral side; resists inversion","Medial side; resists eversion","Lateral side; resists eversion","Medial side; resists inversion"],t.s)
B.abP=new A.e("The deltoid ligament is located on which side of the ankle and resists which motion?",B.Zu,1,"The deltoid ligament is on the medial side of the ankle and is the strongest ankle ligament. It resists eversion. Deltoid injuries are less common than lateral ligament injuries because the deltoid is so strong \u2014 an eversion force is more likely to cause a fracture (especially fibula) than a deltoid tear.")
B.U7=s([B.a8q,B.a7i,B.a9e,B.aaU,B.abP],t.b)
B.J_=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Ankle 1",B.U7)
B.Tt=s(["Supraspinatus","Infraspinatus","Subscapularis","Teres minor"],t.s)
B.a6i=new A.e("The lift-off test (Gerber test) evaluates which rotator cuff muscle?",B.Tt,2,"The lift-off test: hand placed behind the back (internally rotated), patient pushes away from the back. Inability to lift the hand off = subscapularis tear. The belly press test is an alternative for patients who cannot reach behind their back.")
B.MS=s(["Infraspinatus","Subscapularis","Teres minor","Supraspinatus"],t.s)
B.a40=new A.e("Calcific tendinitis most commonly affects which rotator cuff tendon?",B.MS,3,"Calcific tendinitis most commonly affects the supraspinatus tendon in its critical zone of relative hypovascularity. The resorptive phase is the most painful. Treatment ranges from NSAIDs and PT to needle aspiration or ultrasound-guided barbotage.")
B.O4=s(["Deltoid weakness and lateral arm numbness","Hand intrinsic weakness and medial forearm/hand numbness","Shoulder pain with overhead activities","Biceps weakness and lateral forearm numbness"],t.s)
B.abs=new A.e("Thoracic outlet syndrome (TOS) with lower trunk involvement (C8-T1) presents with:",B.O4,1,"Neurogenic TOS affecting the lower trunk (C8-T1) presents with hand intrinsic muscle weakness/atrophy and numbness in the medial forearm and hand (ulnar distribution). Upper trunk (C5-C6) involvement is less common and affects shoulder/arm.")
B.Rn=s(["Resisted shoulder internal rotation","Extending the neck, rotating toward the affected side, and taking a deep breath while palpating the radial pulse","Cross-body adduction of the arm","Overhead arm elevation for 3 minutes"],t.s)
B.a85=new A.e("The Adson test for thoracic outlet syndrome involves:",B.Rn,1,"Adson's test: extend the neck, rotate toward the affected side, deep inspiration \u2014 palpate the radial pulse. Diminished pulse or reproduction of symptoms = positive. However, false positive rates are high (~50% of normals).")
B.XG=s(["AC ligament sprain only","AC ligament tear with CC ligaments intact","Complete AC and CC ligament disruption with clavicle elevated 25-100%","Posterior dislocation of the clavicle"],t.s)
B.a8e=new A.e("In acromioclavicular joint separations, a Rockwood Type III involves:",B.XG,2,"Rockwood classification: Type I = AC sprain. Type II = AC torn, CC intact. Type III = both AC and CC disrupted, clavicle elevated 25-100% (controversial for surgical vs conservative). Types IV-VI involve more severe displacement and usually require surgery.")
B.Yy=s([B.a6i,B.a40,B.abs,B.a85,B.a8e],t.b)
B.J0=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Shoulder 4",B.Yy)
B.PO=s(["Time only","Space only","Both time and space","Neither time nor space"],t.s)
B.a4s=new A.e("Multiple sclerosis is characterized by demyelination that is disseminated in:",B.PO,2,"The McDonald criteria require dissemination in space (lesions in \u22652 different CNS regions) AND time (new lesions at different time points). This distinguishes MS from single demyelinating events. MRI with gadolinium can satisfy both criteria if enhancing and non-enhancing lesions coexist.")
B.Rp=s(["Seizures","Optic neuritis","Bilateral lower extremity paralysis","Dementia"],t.s)
B.abe=new A.e("The most common initial presentation of MS is:",B.Rp,1,"Optic neuritis (unilateral vision loss with painful eye movements) is the most common initial MS presentation. Other common early symptoms include sensory disturbances, limb weakness, and diplopia. Lhermitte sign (electric shock sensation with neck flexion) is characteristic but not specific.")
B.Pf=s(["Improvement of symptoms with heat","Worsening of symptoms with elevated body temperature","Permanent worsening after infection","Improvement with exercise"],t.s)
B.a7o=new A.e("Uhthoff phenomenon in MS refers to:",B.Pf,1,"Uhthoff phenomenon: heat sensitivity causing temporary worsening of MS symptoms (commonly vision) with increased body temperature. This occurs because demyelinated nerves conduct even more poorly at higher temperatures. Symptoms resolve with cooling. Patients should avoid hot baths, saunas, and overheating during exercise.")
B.MM=s(["Primary progressive (PPMS)","Secondary progressive (SPMS)","Relapsing-remitting (RRMS)","Progressive-relapsing (PRMS)"],t.s)
B.a9F=new A.e("The most common type of MS at diagnosis is:",B.MM,2,"RRMS accounts for ~85% of initial diagnoses. It features clearly defined relapses followed by complete or partial recovery (remissions). Over time, many RRMS patients transition to SPMS (steady progression with or without relapses). PPMS (~15%) has steady progression from onset without clear relapses.")
B.Ys=s(["Cognitive function","Ambulatory ability","Upper extremity function","Bladder function"],t.s)
B.a3f=new A.e("The Expanded Disability Status Scale (EDSS) in MS focuses primarily on:",B.Ys,1,"The EDSS (Kurtzke scale, 0-10) is heavily weighted toward ambulatory function. EDSS 0 = normal exam. EDSS 4.0 = significant disability but ambulatory \u2265500m. EDSS 6.0 = requires unilateral assistance to walk 100m. EDSS 7.0 = wheelchair-bound. EDSS 10 = death from MS. Its heavy gait focus is a known limitation.")
B.U9=s([B.a4s,B.abe,B.a7o,B.a9F,B.a3f],t.b)
B.J1=new A.a6("Multiple Sclerosis 1",B.U9)
B.M6=s(["As wide as possible for comfort","Widest point of hips/thighs plus 1-2 inches (one finger width on each side)","6 inches wider than the patient","The same width as a standard chair"],t.s)
B.aao=new A.e("The proper seat width for a manual wheelchair is:",B.M6,1,"Seat width = widest body point (hips/thighs) + 1-2 inches total (0.5-1 inch clearance on each side). Too narrow causes pressure on greater trochanters. Too wide reduces propulsion efficiency (wider push), makes the chair harder to maneuver through doorways, and provides less trunk support.")
B.Z9=s(["Optimal posture","Posterior pelvic tilt and sacral sitting (slouching), increasing sacral pressure injury risk","Improved breathing","Better arm reach for propulsion"],t.s)
B.a8d=new A.e("Seat-to-back angle of 90\xb0 in a wheelchair can cause:",B.Z9,1,"A 90\xb0 seat-to-back angle often causes posterior pelvic tilt (sacral sitting/slouching). Opening the seat-to-back angle slightly (95-100\xb0) or adding a slight seat dump (seat angled posteriorly) helps maintain a neutral pelvis. Posterior pelvic tilt increases sacral pressure and risk of skin breakdown.")
B.XJ=s(["Make the chair look better","Identify areas of high pressure on the seated surface to optimize cushion selection","Measure wheelchair speed","Determine insurance coverage"],t.s)
B.aaT=new A.e("Wheelchair cushion pressure mapping is used to:",B.XJ,1,"Pressure mapping: a sensor array between the patient and cushion creates a visual map of pressure distribution. Identifies high-pressure areas (typically ischial tuberosities and sacrum) to guide cushion selection. Goal: distribute pressure as evenly as possible. Used for SCI, stroke, and any patient at risk for pressure injuries.")
B.Vu=s(["Speed","Pressure redistribution by changing the angle of the entire seat relative to the ground without changing posture","Standing","Folding for transport"],t.s)
B.a7O=new A.e("A tilt-in-space wheelchair is used primarily for:",B.Vu,1,"Tilt-in-space: the entire seat-and-back unit tilts posteriorly while maintaining the same seat-to-back angle. This redistributes pressure from the ischial tuberosities to the back without changing the patient's posture. Used for patients who cannot perform independent pressure relief (high-level SCI, severe CP, advanced MS). Minimum 25-45\xb0 tilt recommended.")
B.PI=s(["They cost less","Reduced weight and rigid frame increase propulsion efficiency and reduce repetitive strain injury risk","They are required by insurance","They fold more easily"],t.s)
B.aaj=new A.e("Ultra-lightweight manual wheelchairs are prescribed for active users because:",B.PI,1,"Ultra-lightweight rigid-frame manual wheelchairs: reduced weight (under 20 lbs) + rigid frame (no energy lost to frame flex) = more efficient propulsion. Active users (SCI K3-K4) benefit from reduced shoulder strain, faster speed, and better maneuverability. Adjustable axle position optimizes biomechanics. Significantly reduces long-term upper extremity overuse injury.")
B.U0=s([B.aao,B.a8d,B.aaT,B.a7O,B.aaj],t.b)
B.J2=new A.a6("Wheelchair 1",B.U0)
B.QM=s(["AIS A","AIS B","AIS C","AIS D"],t.s)
B.a7J=new A.e("The ASIA Impairment Scale (AIS) grade for a complete spinal cord injury is:",B.QM,0,"AIS A = complete: no motor or sensory function preserved in the sacral segments S4-S5. AIS B = sensory incomplete. AIS C = motor incomplete (<50% of key muscles below the level have grade \u22653). AIS D = motor incomplete (\u226550% grade \u22653). AIS E = normal.")
B.Pp=s(["Hemorrhagic shock from spinal injury bleeding","Temporary loss of all reflex activity below the level of injury","Permanent paralysis","Hypotension due to cardiac failure"],t.s)
B.ab5=new A.e("Spinal shock is characterized by:",B.Pp,1,"Spinal shock: temporary areflexia, flaccidity, and loss of autonomic function below the injury level. It can last hours to weeks. Resolution is marked by return of the bulbocavernosus reflex. Only after spinal shock resolves can the injury be accurately classified as complete or incomplete.")
B.ME=s(["Stroking the plantar surface of the foot","Squeezing the glans penis or tugging the Foley catheter and observing anal sphincter contraction","Tapping the patellar tendon","Stimulating the cremasteric reflex"],t.s)
B.a3X=new A.e("The bulbocavernosus reflex returns to signal the end of spinal shock. It is elicited by:",B.ME,1,"The bulbocavernosus reflex: squeeze the glans penis (or clitoris) or tug on the Foley catheter \u2192 anal sphincter contraction. Its return marks the end of spinal shock and is the earliest reflex to return. Once spinal shock has resolved, an AIS A injury is likely permanent.")
B.Sq=s(["Blood loss","Loss of sympathetic tone below the injury causing hypotension and bradycardia","Spinal cord edema compressing the heart","Cardiogenic shock"],t.s)
B.a3Q=new A.e("Neurogenic shock in acute SCI is caused by:",B.Sq,1,"Neurogenic shock: loss of sympathetic tone below the injury (T6 and above) causes vasodilation (hypotension), bradycardia, and hypothermia. It is distinct from spinal shock (areflexia). Treatment: IV fluids, vasopressors (phenylephrine/norepinephrine), and atropine for bradycardia.")
B.Vx=s(["Wrist extension","Elbow flexion and shoulder function","Hand grip","Triceps function"],t.s)
B.a3U=new A.e("A C5 complete SCI patient would retain which functional ability?",B.Vx,1,"C5 level: intact deltoid (shoulder abduction) and biceps (elbow flexion). No wrist extension (C6), no triceps (C7), no hand function (C8-T1). A C5 patient can feed themselves with adaptive equipment but needs assistance for most ADLs and uses a power wheelchair.")
B.W7=s([B.a7J,B.ab5,B.a3X,B.a3Q,B.a3U],t.b)
B.J3=new A.a6("Spinal Cord Injury 1",B.W7)
B.T4=s(["FDS and FDP","EPL and EPB","APL and EPB (first dorsal compartment)","ECRL and ECRB"],t.s)
B.aaF=new A.e("De Quervain's tenosynovitis involves which tendons?",B.T4,2,'De Quervain\'s involves the first dorsal compartment: abductor pollicis longus (APL) and extensor pollicis brevis (EPB). The Finkelstein test (ulnar deviation with thumb in fist) reproduces pain. Common in new mothers ("mommy thumb").')
B.Yr=s(["Tendon rupture","Thickening of the A1 pulley with tendon nodule entrapment","Dupuytren's contracture","Joint dislocation"],t.s)
B.a8G=new A.e("Trigger finger is caused by:",B.Yr,1,"Trigger finger results from thickening of the A1 pulley and/or nodule formation on the flexor tendon. The tendon catches as it passes through the narrowed pulley, causing clicking, locking, or a fixed flexion contracture.")
B.OM=s(["The flexor tendons","The palmar fascia (palmar aponeurosis)","The extensor tendons","The joint capsule"],t.s)
B.a8A=new A.e("Dupuytren's contracture primarily involves:",B.OM,1,"Dupuytren's is a fibroproliferative disorder of the palmar fascia causing nodules and cords that progressively flex the MCP and PIP joints. The ring finger is most commonly affected. Risk factors include Northern European descent, diabetes, alcohol use, and smoking.")
B.Z6=s(["Radial collateral ligament of the thumb MCP","Ulnar collateral ligament of the thumb MCP","Volar plate of the thumb IP joint","Scapholunate ligament"],t.s)
B.a6h=new A.e("A Gamekeeper's (Skier's) thumb involves injury to which structure?",B.Z6,1,"Gamekeeper's thumb is a sprain/tear of the UCL of the thumb MCP joint. Caused by forceful abduction (ski pole falls, breaking a fall). A Stener lesion (adductor aponeurosis interposition) requires surgical repair as it prevents ligament healing.")
B.MF=s(["It is the largest carpal bone","Its retrograde blood supply puts it at high risk for avascular necrosis","It articulates with no other carpal bones","It is always visible on initial X-ray"],t.s)
B.a6k=new A.e("The scaphoid fracture is significant because:",B.MF,1,"The scaphoid has a retrograde blood supply (entering distally via the dorsal ridge). Proximal pole fractures are at highest risk for AVN. Scaphoid fractures may not be visible on initial X-rays \u2014 suspect with anatomical snuffbox tenderness and repeat imaging at 2 weeks or get MRI.")
B.YS=s([B.aaF,B.a8G,B.a8A,B.a6h,B.a6k],t.b)
B.J4=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Hand",B.YS)
B.Ug=s(["Anterior cerebral artery (ACA)","Middle cerebral artery (MCA)","Posterior cerebral artery (PCA)","Basilar artery"],t.s)
B.a80=new A.e("Which artery supplies the lateral surface of the cerebral hemisphere and is the most commonly affected vessel in ischemic stroke?",B.Ug,1,"The MCA is the most commonly affected artery in ischemic stroke. It supplies the lateral hemisphere including the motor and sensory cortex for face and upper extremity, Broca's area, and Wernicke's area.")
B.Xb=s(["Middle cerebral artery","Anterior cerebral artery","Posterior cerebral artery","Posterior inferior cerebellar artery"],t.s)
B.a7v=new A.e("A patient presents with contralateral leg weakness and sensory loss with relative sparing of the face and arm. Which vascular territory is most likely affected?",B.Xb,1,"The ACA supplies the medial surface of the hemisphere, which includes the motor and sensory representation of the lower extremity. ACA strokes classically present with contralateral leg weakness greater than arm or face.")
B.SK=s(["Anterior cerebral artery","Middle cerebral artery","Posterior cerebral artery","Anterior choroidal artery"],t.s)
B.ac9=new A.e("Homonymous hemianopia without motor deficits is most characteristic of a stroke in which vascular territory?",B.SK,2,"The PCA supplies the occipital lobe (visual cortex). PCA strokes classically present with contralateral homonymous hemianopia. Motor deficits are typically absent since the motor cortex is supplied by the ACA and MCA.")
B.Pe=s(["Anterior communicating arteries","Posterior communicating arteries","Ophthalmic arteries","Lenticulostriate arteries"],t.s)
B.aa9=new A.e("In the Circle of Willis, which arteries connect the anterior and posterior circulation?",B.Pe,1,"The posterior communicating arteries (PComm) connect the internal carotid arteries (anterior circulation) to the posterior cerebral arteries (posterior/vertebrobasilar circulation). The anterior communicating artery connects the two ACAs.")
B.TE=s(["Large vessel atherosclerotic stroke","Lacunar infarct","Cardioembolic stroke","Watershed infarct"],t.s)
B.a9k=new A.e("Which type of ischemic stroke is caused by cardiogenic embolism and is associated with atrial fibrillation?",B.TE,2,"Cardioembolic strokes account for approximately 20% of ischemic strokes and are strongly associated with atrial fibrillation. The embolus typically lodges in the MCA territory. Treatment includes anticoagulation rather than antiplatelet therapy alone.")
B.Ot=s([B.a80,B.a7v,B.ac9,B.aa9,B.a9k],t.b)
B.J5=new A.a6("Cerebrovascular Accident 2",B.Ot)
B.Zm=s(["Pain radiating below the knee with positive SLR","Axial pain that worsens with extension and rotation","Pain that improves with standing and walking","Bilateral lower extremity weakness"],t.s)
B.a64=new A.e("Facet joint (zygapophyseal) pain is characterized by:",B.Zm,1,"Facet joint pain is typically axial (does not radiate below the knee), worsens with extension and ipsilateral rotation (which loads the facet joints), and improves with flexion. Diagnosis is confirmed by medial branch block with local anesthetic.")
B.XH=s(["MRI of the lumbar spine","Positive straight leg raise","Relief of pain with fluoroscopy-guided SI joint injection","CT myelogram"],t.s)
B.a5y=new A.e("Sacroiliac joint dysfunction is best confirmed by:",B.XH,2,"SI joint pain is confirmed when fluoroscopy-guided intra-articular injection of local anesthetic provides >75% pain relief. Provocative tests (FABER, Gaenslen, compression, distraction) are sensitive but not specific individually \u2014 3+ positive tests increase diagnostic confidence.")
B.W9=s(["It is the primary hip flexor","It provides segmental stabilization of the lumbar spine","It controls cervical rotation","It is the largest muscle in the body"],t.s)
B.acj=new A.e("The multifidus muscle is important in spine rehabilitation because:",B.W9,1,"The multifidus provides critical segmental stabilization of the lumbar spine. It atrophies rapidly after injury/pain and does not recover spontaneously. Targeted activation (core stabilization exercises) is a key component of evidence-based spine rehabilitation.")
B.Tu=s(["Disc herniation with radiculopathy","Facet-mediated pain confirmed by diagnostic medial branch blocks","Cauda equina syndrome","Spinal cord compression"],t.s)
B.a4B=new A.e("Radiofrequency ablation (RFA) of the medial branch nerves is indicated for:",B.Tu,1,"RFA denervates the facet joints by ablating the medial branch nerves. It requires confirmation with diagnostic blocks first (\u226580% pain relief on two occasions). Pain relief typically lasts 6-12 months before nerve regeneration occurs.")
B.M4=s(["MRI findings","Response to repeated movements (directional preference)","Nerve conduction study results","Patient age and BMI"],t.s)
B.a8D=new A.e("The McKenzie method classifies mechanical back pain based on:",B.M4,1,"McKenzie classifies pain by directional preference: derangement (most common \u2014 symptoms centralize with repeated movements in one direction), dysfunction (pain at end-range from shortened tissue), and postural (sustained positioning pain). Centralization with extension is the most common pattern.")
B.Y2=s([B.a64,B.a5y,B.acj,B.a4B,B.a8D],t.b)
B.J6=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Spine 5",B.Y2)
B.Ur=s(["Hereditary shows conduction block; acquired shows uniform slowing","Hereditary shows uniform slowing; acquired shows conduction block and temporal dispersion","There is no electrodiagnostic difference","Hereditary affects sensory nerves only"],t.s)
B.a9r=new A.e("The key NCS distinction between hereditary and acquired demyelinating neuropathies is:",B.Ur,1,"Hereditary demyelinating neuropathies (CMT1) show uniform slowing across all nerves without conduction block or temporal dispersion. Acquired demyelinating neuropathies (GBS, CIDP) show patchy, non-uniform slowing WITH conduction block and temporal dispersion.")
B.UQ=s(["SOD1","Dystrophin","PMP-22","SMN1"],t.s)
B.a6P=new A.e("Charcot-Marie-Tooth Type 1 is caused by duplication of which gene on chromosome 17?",B.UQ,2,"CMT1 (the most common hereditary neuropathy) is caused by PMP-22 gene duplication on chromosome 17. This produces too much peripheral myelin protein, leading to abnormal myelination. Conduction velocities are severely and uniformly slowed (<38 m/s).")
B.Pt=s(["PMP-22 duplication","PMP-22 deletion","SOD1 mutation","SMN1 deletion"],t.s)
B.a5F=new A.e("Hereditary Neuropathy with Liability to Pressure Palsies (HNPP) is caused by:",B.Pt,1,"HNPP is caused by PMP-22 deletion \u2014 the reciprocal of CMT1 (which is PMP-22 duplication). HNPP patients develop prolonged nerve palsies (weeks-long foot drop or wrist drop) from minor compression that would not affect normal individuals.")
B.Mr=s(["Low protein, high white cells","High protein, normal cell count (albuminocytologic dissociation)","Normal protein, high white cells","Low protein, low glucose"],t.s)
B.a8J=new A.e("Guillain-Barr\xe9 syndrome (GBS) is characterized by which CSF finding?",B.Mr,1,"GBS shows albuminocytologic dissociation: elevated CSF protein with normal cell count. This classic CSF pattern, combined with ascending weakness and areflexia, is highly suggestive of GBS. Treatment is IVIG or plasmapheresis \u2014 NOT steroids.")
B.TB=s(["EMG findings","CSF protein levels","Time course \u2014 GBS peaks by 4 weeks; CIDP continues beyond 8 weeks","Presence of conduction block"],t.s)
B.a3s=new A.e("The distinction between GBS and CIDP is primarily based on:",B.TB,2,"GBS is acute \u2014 it reaches nadir within 4 weeks. CIDP (Chronic Inflammatory Demyelinating Polyneuropathy) progresses beyond 8 weeks and has a relapsing-remitting or chronic progressive course. Unlike GBS, CIDP responds to steroids.")
B.Ra=s([B.a9r,B.a6P,B.a5F,B.a8J,B.a3s],t.b)
B.J7=new A.a6("Electrodiagnostic 12",B.Ra)
B.Sv=s(["The intrathecal space","The epidural space (between the ligamentum flavum and dura mater)","The facet joint","The muscle"],t.s)
B.a8I=new A.e("Epidural steroid injections deliver medication to:",B.Sv,1,"ESI: corticosteroid \xb1 local anesthetic injected into the epidural space to reduce inflammation around compressed nerve roots. Approaches: interlaminar (midline), transforaminal (targeted to specific nerve root \u2014 most specific), and caudal (through sacral hiatus). Most effective for acute/subacute radicular pain with imaging-confirmed pathology.")
B.SS=s(["Low back pain","Sympathetically maintained pain of the upper extremity (CRPS)","Knee arthritis","Headaches only"],t.s)
B.abB=new A.e("A stellate ganglion block is used to diagnose and treat:",B.SS,1,"Stellate ganglion block: local anesthetic injected at the C6 or C7 transverse process targeting the cervicothoracic sympathetic ganglion. Diagnostic: if pain resolves, it confirms sympathetically maintained pain. Therapeutic: can provide lasting relief in CRPS of the upper extremity. Signs of successful block: ipsilateral Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis).")
B.PC=s(["The epidural space","The cerebrospinal fluid directly, requiring 1/300th of the oral dose","The bloodstream","The muscle"],t.s)
B.aab=new A.e("Intrathecal drug delivery systems (pain pumps) deliver medication to:",B.PC,1,"Intrathecal pump: delivers medication directly to the CSF, bypassing the blood-brain barrier. Morphine or ziconotide (non-opioid, N-type calcium channel blocker) are FDA-approved. Requires ~1/300th of the oral morphine dose, dramatically reducing systemic side effects. Indicated for refractory cancer pain or chronic non-cancer pain failing other treatments.")
B.Zy=s(["Blocking opioid receptors","Inhibiting cyclooxygenase (COX) enzymes, reducing prostaglandin synthesis","Enhancing serotonin levels","Blocking calcium channels"],t.s)
B.aca=new A.e("Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) work by:",B.Zy,1,"NSAIDs inhibit COX enzymes: COX-1 (constitutive \u2014 protects gastric mucosa, supports platelet function, maintains renal blood flow) and COX-2 (inducible \u2014 mediates inflammation and pain). Non-selective NSAIDs (ibuprofen, naproxen) inhibit both. COX-2 selective (celecoxib) has less GI toxicity but carries cardiovascular risk. All NSAIDs have renal and GI risks.")
B.Xk=s(["Pain is purely a physical phenomenon","Pain results from the interaction of biological, psychological, and social factors","Only psychological factors matter","Surgery is always needed for chronic pain"],t.s)
B.a6T=new A.e("The biopsychosocial model of chronic pain emphasizes:",B.Xk,1,"Biopsychosocial model: chronic pain involves biological (tissue damage, central sensitization), psychological (depression, catastrophizing, fear-avoidance), and social (disability, family dynamics, work status) factors. Treatment must address all three domains \u2014 interdisciplinary pain programs using medical management, CBT, physical therapy, and vocational counseling have the strongest evidence.")
B.Wi=s([B.a8I,B.abB,B.aab,B.aca,B.a6T],t.b)
B.J8=new A.a6("Pain 4",B.Wi)
B.Xy=s(["Spastic","Dyskinetic (dystonic/choreoathetoid)","Ataxic","Peripheral neuropathic"],t.s)
B.aar=new A.e("Cerebral palsy classification by movement type includes all EXCEPT:",B.Xy,3,"CP is classified by motor type: spastic (~80%), dyskinetic (10-15%, includes dystonic and choreoathetoid subtypes from basal ganglia injury), ataxic (~5%, from cerebellar injury), and mixed. Peripheral neuropathic is NOT a CP classification \u2014 CP is by definition a central (brain) lesion.")
B.UV=s(["IQ only","Functional capabilities and caregiver assistance in self-care, mobility, and social function","Academic achievement","Spasticity severity"],t.s)
B.acd=new A.e("The Pediatric Evaluation of Disability Inventory (PEDI) measures:",B.UV,1,"PEDI (now PEDI-CAT computer adaptive test): measures functional performance in children ages 6 months to 7.5 years (or older with disabilities). Three domains: self-care, mobility, social/cognitive. Assesses capability, caregiver assistance level, and environmental modifications. Used for program planning and outcome measurement.")
B.Oq=s(["It is more severe","The bony defect is present but the spinal cord and meninges are NOT exposed","It always requires surgery","It causes complete paraplegia"],t.s)
B.aaE=new A.e("Spina bifida occulta differs from myelomeningocele in that:",B.Oq,1,"Spina bifida occulta: incomplete fusion of posterior vertebral arch with intact skin covering. Often incidental finding on X-ray. Usually asymptomatic. May have cutaneous markers (hairy patch, dimple, lipoma). Myelomeningocele: open defect with exposed spinal cord/meninges = most severe form with significant neurological deficits.")
B.Ps=s(["Medication only","Ventriculoperitoneal (VP) shunt","Observation","Lumbar puncture series"],t.s)
B.a7K=new A.e("Hydrocephalus in myelomeningocele is treated with:",B.Ps,1,"VP shunt is the standard treatment for hydrocephalus in myelomeningocele (needed in ~90% due to Chiari II malformation). Shunt malfunction is a lifelong risk: signs include headache, vomiting, lethargy, bulging fontanelle (infants), and sunsetting eyes. Endoscopic third ventriculostomy (ETV) is an alternative in select patients.")
B.Wo=s(["All types of CP","Spastic diplegic CP (GMFCS II-III) with good underlying strength","Purely dystonic CP","Ataxic CP"],t.s)
B.a9I=new A.e("Selective dorsal rhizotomy (SDR) in cerebral palsy is most appropriate for:",B.Wo,1,"SDR best candidates: spastic diplegia, GMFCS II-III, age 4-8 years, good underlying strength beneath the spasticity, no fixed contractures, no significant dystonia, and motivated family for intensive post-operative rehabilitation (12+ months). It permanently reduces spasticity but requires strength to be present for functional improvement.")
B.NK=s([B.aar,B.acd,B.aaE,B.a7K,B.a9I],t.b)
B.J9=new A.a6("Pediatrics 11",B.NK)
B.Qj=s(["Single organ involvement only","Multisystem autoimmune disease with ANA positivity, butterfly rash, and nephritis risk","Only joint involvement","Male predominance"],t.s)
B.a4K=new A.e("Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is characterized by:",B.Qj,1,"SLE: multisystem autoimmune disease predominantly in women (9:1 female-to-male). ANA positive (sensitive, not specific). Anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith (specific). Features: malar (butterfly) rash, photosensitivity, oral ulcers, arthritis, serositis, nephritis (class III-V = serious), hematologic abnormalities. Treatment: hydroxychloroquine (all patients), immunosuppressives for organ involvement.")
B.SG=s(["Diffuse skin thickening","Calcinosis, Raynaud's, Esophageal dysmotility, Sclerodactyly, Telangiectasia","Only pulmonary fibrosis","Renal crisis as the primary feature"],t.s)
B.a7w=new A.e("Scleroderma (systemic sclerosis) limited type (CREST) includes:",B.SG,1,"CREST syndrome (limited cutaneous scleroderma): Calcinosis (calcium deposits in skin), Raynaud phenomenon (vasospasm), Esophageal dysmotility (acid reflux, dysphagia), Sclerodactyly (skin tightening of fingers), Telangiectasia (dilated blood vessels). Anti-centromere antibody positive. Better prognosis than diffuse type but risk of pulmonary arterial hypertension.")
B.Qh=s(["Skin rash as the defining feature","Proximal muscle weakness with elevated CK and inflammatory changes on muscle biopsy","Normal CK levels","Distal weakness predominance"],t.s)
B.a8W=new A.e("Polymyositis and dermatomyositis share which finding?",B.Qh,1,"Both: symmetric proximal muscle weakness, elevated CK (5-50x normal), EMG showing myopathic pattern with spontaneous activity, and inflammatory infiltrate on muscle biopsy. Dermatomyositis additionally has: heliotrope rash (purple eyelid), Gottron papules (knuckle rash), V-sign, shawl sign. Both have increased malignancy risk (screen with age-appropriate cancer screening).")
B.R2=s(["Muscle weakness","Pain and stiffness in the shoulders and hips in patients >50 years with markedly elevated ESR","Skin thickening","Raynaud phenomenon"],t.s)
B.a9a=new A.e("Polymyalgia rheumatica (PMR) is characterized by:",B.R2,1,"PMR: pain/stiffness in shoulder and hip girdles in patients >50 years. ESR typically >40 mm/hr (often >100). CK is NORMAL (distinguishes from polymyositis \u2014 PMR is pain, not weakness). Dramatic response to low-dose prednisone (10-20 mg/day) is virtually diagnostic. Association with giant cell arteritis (temporal arteritis) \u2014 screen for headache, visual symptoms, jaw claudication.")
B.Sg=s(["Complete rest and immobilization","Avoiding positions of deformity, using larger joints, distributing loads, and maintaining ROM","Maximum-intensity exercise","Ignoring pain during activity"],t.s)
B.a3h=new A.e("The joint protection principle in arthritis rehabilitation emphasizes:",B.Sg,1,"Joint protection: avoid positions of deformity (don't rest hand in ulnar deviation), use the largest joint possible for tasks, distribute loads across multiple joints, use adaptive equipment to reduce stress, respect pain (2-hour pain rule \u2014 if pain persists >2 hours after activity, it was too much), and maintain ROM through daily gentle stretching.")
B.Qg=s([B.a4K,B.a7w,B.a8W,B.a9a,B.a3h],t.b)
B.Ja=new A.a6("Rheumatology 3",B.Qg)
B.TS=s(["Iliopsoas tendon inflammation","Gluteus medius/minimus tendinopathy","Hamstring tendon avulsion","IT band snapping"],t.s)
B.a88=new A.e("Greater trochanteric pain syndrome (formerly trochanteric bursitis) most commonly involves:",B.TS,1,'Greater trochanteric pain syndrome is now understood to primarily involve gluteus medius and minimus tendinopathy rather than isolated bursitis. The tendons insert on the greater trochanter and can undergo degenerative changes similar to rotator cuff disease ("rotator cuff of the hip").')
B.Ss=s(["Quadriceps tendon and patella","Hip joint and sacroiliac joint","Lumbar spine and sciatic nerve","Knee meniscus and ACL"],t.s)
B.a59=new A.e("The FABER (Patrick) test assesses for pathology in which structures?",B.Ss,1,"FABER = Flexion, ABduction, External Rotation. Pain in the groin = hip joint pathology. Pain in the posterior buttock = sacroiliac joint pathology. This test stresses both structures simultaneously.")
B.Vk=s(["Excessive acetabular coverage (overcoverage)","Abnormal bony prominence on the femoral head-neck junction","Labral hypertrophy","Femoral head AVN"],t.s)
B.a3c=new A.e("Femoroacetabular impingement (FAI) of the cam type involves:",B.Vk,1,"Cam impingement: abnormal bone on the femoral head-neck junction (loss of normal concavity) that jams into the acetabulum during flexion. Pincer impingement: excessive acetabular coverage. Many patients have mixed (cam + pincer) type.")
B.O0=s(["Originates from the piriformis muscle","Passes through or under the piriformis muscle","Is compressed by the piriformis at the knee","Is a branch of the piriformis nerve"],t.s)
B.a9z=new A.e("Piriformis syndrome can mimic sciatica because the sciatic nerve:",B.O0,1,"The sciatic nerve typically passes beneath the piriformis muscle. In anatomic variants (~15-20% of population), the nerve (or its peroneal division) may pass through or above the piriformis. Spasm or hypertrophy of the piriformis can compress the nerve, causing buttock pain radiating down the leg.")
B.QB=s(["They always heal with rest alone","They are at higher risk of displacement and require surgical fixation","They are less painful than compression-side fractures","They only occur in elderly patients"],t.s)
B.abS=new A.e("Stress fractures of the femoral neck on the tension side (superior/lateral) are important because:",B.QB,1,"Tension-side (superior) femoral neck stress fractures are at high risk for displacement and completion \u2014 they require prophylactic surgical fixation. Compression-side (inferior/medial) fractures are lower risk and can often be managed conservatively with protected weight-bearing.")
B.UO=s([B.a88,B.a59,B.a3c,B.a9z,B.abS],t.b)
B.Jb=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Hip 2",B.UO)
B.Y4=s(["Different genes","Duchenne has absent dystrophin; Becker has reduced/abnormal dystrophin (in-frame vs. out-of-frame mutations)","One affects girls, the other boys","They have identical presentations"],t.s)
B.a75=new A.e("Duchenne vs. Becker muscular dystrophy: the key difference is:",B.Y4,1,'Both involve the dystrophin gene on Xp21. DMD: out-of-frame deletion \u2192 NO functional dystrophin \u2192 severe, wheelchair by 12. BMD: in-frame deletion \u2192 reduced/truncated but partially functional dystrophin \u2192 milder, ambulation into 20s-30s. This is the "reading frame hypothesis."')
B.QW=s(["Scoliosis is always cosmetic","Progressive curves can compromise respiratory function and seating","It only occurs in adults","All curves require immediate surgery"],t.s)
B.a4T=new A.e("Scoliosis screening in neuromuscular conditions is important because:",B.QW,1,"Neuromuscular scoliosis (CP, DMD, SMA) tends to be progressive, long C-curves involving the pelvis. Unlike idiopathic scoliosis, bracing is rarely effective. Severe curves compromise respiratory function (already impaired in NMD), complicate seating/positioning, and cause pain. Spinal fusion is often needed when curves exceed 40-50\xb0 in non-ambulatory patients.")
B.On=s(["Always indicative of epilepsy","Common (2-5% of children), typically benign, occurring with fever in ages 6 months to 5 years","Always require lifelong anticonvulsants","Only occur in children with brain damage"],t.s)
B.a9q=new A.e("Febrile seizures in children are:",B.On,1,"Simple febrile seizures: generalized, <15 minutes, single occurrence in 24 hours, age 6mo-5yr. No increased risk of epilepsy (only slightly above baseline). Complex febrile seizures (focal, prolonged, or recurrent) carry slightly higher epilepsy risk. Most children need reassurance only, not anticonvulsant prophylaxis.")
B.QF=s(["The Americans with Disabilities Act only","IDEA Part C (Individuals with Disabilities Education Act) for children birth to 3 years","Medicare","No federal law mandates this"],t.s)
B.a8c=new A.e("Early intervention services for developmental delays are mandated by:",B.QF,1,"IDEA Part C: federal mandate for early intervention services for infants/toddlers (0-3 years) with developmental delays or conditions with high probability of delay. Services include PT, OT, speech, and family support. Individualized Family Service Plan (IFSP) is developed. Part B covers ages 3-21 with an IEP (Individualized Education Program).")
B.U6=s(["Children never need rehabilitation","The developing brain has both greater plasticity potential and vulnerability to disrupting developmental trajectories","Recovery principles are identical","Only children over 12 benefit from rehabilitation"],t.s)
B.a8k=new A.e("Pediatric acquired brain injury rehabilitation differs from adult TBI rehabilitation in that:",B.U6,1,'Pediatric ABI: greater neuroplasticity can aid recovery, but injury disrupts ongoing brain development. Deficits may not be apparent immediately ("growing into deficits") \u2014 executive function, social cognition, and academic problems often emerge years later. Long-term follow-up is critical. Family/school reintegration and educational accommodations are essential components.')
B.WE=s([B.a75,B.a4T,B.a9q,B.a8c,B.a8k],t.b)
B.Jc=new A.a6("Pediatrics 10",B.WE)
B.Rw=s(["Upper motor neuron signs only","Lower motor neuron signs only","Both upper and lower motor neuron signs in the same patient","Sensory and motor deficits"],t.s)
B.a51=new A.e("ALS is characterized by the combination of:",B.Rw,2,"ALS uniquely combines UMN signs (spasticity, hyperreflexia, Babinski) AND LMN signs (atrophy, fasciculations, fibrillations) in the same patient, often in the same limb. This combination is the hallmark that distinguishes ALS from pure UMN or LMN diseases.")
B.Y8=s(["Motor, sensory, and autonomic","Sensation, bowel/bladder, and eye movements","Cognition, respiration, and swallowing","Reflexes, coordination, and balance"],t.s)
B.a90=new A.e("Which three systems are characteristically SPARED in ALS?",B.Y8,1,"The ALS sparing triad: (1) Sensation \u2014 SNAPs are normal, (2) Bowel/bladder \u2014 sphincters spared until very late, (3) Eye movements \u2014 extraocular muscles spared until end-stage. If any of these are prominently affected early, reconsider the diagnosis.")
B.T5=s(["1 of 4 regions","2 of 4 regions","3 of 4 regions","All 4 regions"],t.s)
B.a8l=new A.e("To diagnose ALS using the El Escorial criteria, EMG must show active denervation and chronic reinnervation in at least how many body regions?",B.T5,2,"El Escorial criteria require findings in 3 of 4 body segments: (1) bulbar/cranial, (2) cervical, (3) thoracic, (4) lumbosacral. Each region must show both active denervation (fibrillations/PSWs) and chronic reinnervation (large polyphasic MUAPs).")
B.R0=s(["Always fatal","Treatable with IVIG and shows conduction block on NCS","Identical to ALS on EMG","Associated with sensory loss"],t.s)
B.a3l=new A.e("Multifocal motor neuropathy (MMN) is an important ALS mimic because it is:",B.R0,1,"MMN is treatable with IVIG \u2014 making it critical to distinguish from ALS. Key features: conduction block on NCS (ALS has NO conduction block), anti-GM1 antibodies, and pure motor involvement. Unlike ALS, MMN has a normal life expectancy with treatment.")
B.MY=s(["Birth to 6 months","6-18 months","After 18 months","Adolescence"],t.s)
B.aad=new A.e("Spinal Muscular Atrophy Type I (Werdnig-Hoffmann) presents at what age?",B.MY,0,"SMA Type I (Werdnig-Hoffmann): onset before 6 months, floppy baby, never achieves sitting, most severe. Type II: 6-18 months, can sit but never walks. Type III (Kugelberg-Welander): after 18 months, walks then loses function. All are autosomal recessive (SMN1 gene).")
B.YI=s([B.a51,B.a90,B.a8l,B.a3l,B.aad],t.b)
B.Jd=new A.a6("Electrodiagnostic 15",B.YI)
B.S_=s(["Transhumeral","Transradial","Shoulder disarticulation","Wrist disarticulation"],t.s)
B.a9A=new A.e("The most common upper extremity amputation level is:",B.S_,1,"Transradial (below-elbow) is the most common upper extremity amputation level. Unlike lower extremity (mostly dysvascular), upper extremity amputations are predominantly trauma-related (young males in industrial/military settings). Preserving the elbow joint greatly improves prosthetic function.")
B.TH=s(["Myoelectric signals","A cable system activated by shoulder/scapular movement","Voice commands","Brain-computer interface"],t.s)
B.abd=new A.e("Body-powered upper extremity prostheses use which mechanism for terminal device control?",B.TH,1,"Body-powered prostheses: a cable system connects the terminal device to a harness (typically figure-8 design). Shoulder flexion/scapular abduction generates cable tension to open the terminal device (voluntary opening) or close it (voluntary closing). Advantages: durable, provides proprioceptive feedback through cable tension, lower cost, waterproof.")
B.Wt=s(["Nerve impulses directly","EMG signals from residual limb muscles to control motorized components","Brain waves","Temperature changes"],t.s)
B.aam=new A.e("Myoelectric prostheses detect:",B.Wt,1,"Myoelectric prostheses: surface electrodes in the socket detect EMG signals from residual muscles (e.g., wrist extensors = open, wrist flexors = close). A battery-powered motor actuates the terminal device. Advantages: cosmetically superior, no harness needed, stronger grip. Disadvantages: expensive, fragile, no proprioceptive feedback, moisture-sensitive.")
B.RE=s(["Only a hook","A hook (split hook) or a prosthetic hand","Only a cosmetic hand","A powered elbow only"],t.s)
B.a4Y=new A.e("The terminal device in an upper extremity prosthesis can be:",B.RE,1,"Terminal devices: voluntary-opening hook (most functional, best visual feedback of grip, most popular for work), voluntary-closing hook (grip force proportional to cable pull), or prosthetic hand (cosmetic, less functional grip). Many patients use a hook for work/function and switch to a cosmetic hand for social situations.")
B.ZL=s(["5%","20-30%","50-75%","Nearly 100%"],t.s)
B.a9X=new A.e("Rejection rates for upper extremity prostheses are approximately:",B.ZL,1,"Upper extremity prosthetic rejection rates (~20-30%) are significantly higher than lower extremity. Reasons: limited proprioceptive feedback, weight, heat, difficulty with fine motor tasks, cosmetic concerns, and functional limitations compared to the intact hand. Early fitting (within 30 days) and intensive training improve acceptance.")
B.Rb=s([B.a9A,B.abd,B.aam,B.a4Y,B.a9X],t.b)
B.Je=new A.a6("Prosthetics & Orthotics 5",B.Rb)
B.R_=s(["Chronic back pain","Metal implants, pacemakers, and over malignancies","Osteoarthritis","Mild muscle strain"],t.s)
B.a7M=new A.e("Shortwave diathermy is contraindicated in patients with:",B.R_,1,"Shortwave diathermy (SWD): deep heating using electromagnetic energy (27.12 MHz). Contraindicated with metal implants (focal overheating), pacemakers/electronic implants (interference), malignancy, pregnancy, active bleeding, DVT, sensory impairment, and over fluid-filled cavities. Metal concentrates the electromagnetic field, creating dangerous hot spots.")
B.LX=s(["Diagnose nerve injuries","Activate and strengthen weakened muscles through electrical stimulation of the motor nerve","Measure nerve conduction velocity","Reduce bone density"],t.s)
B.a86=new A.e("Neuromuscular electrical stimulation (NMES) is used to:",B.LX,1,"NMES: electrical current applied over a motor point causes muscle contraction. Uses: muscle re-education after surgery/injury, strengthening (quadriceps after ACL repair), reducing atrophy in immobilized limbs, functional electrical stimulation (FES) in SCI/stroke. Parameters: typically 35-50 Hz, on/off duty cycle, sufficient intensity for visible contraction.")
B.Xp=s(["Ultrasound waves","Direct electrical current that drives charged drug molecules into tissue","Magnetic fields","Infrared light"],t.s)
B.a6j=new A.e("Iontophoresis delivers medication through the skin using:",B.Xp,1,"Iontophoresis: direct current drives charged medication ions through the skin. The medication is placed under the electrode with the SAME charge (like charges repel \u2014 driving the drug into tissue). Common medications: dexamethasone (anti-inflammatory, negative pole), lidocaine (anesthetic, positive pole), acetic acid (calcium deposits). Low-level current (1-4 mA).")
B.Oo=s(["Electrical current","Ultrasound waves to drive topical medications through the skin","Laser light","Magnetic resonance"],t.s)
B.a7x=new A.e("Phonophoresis differs from iontophoresis in that phonophoresis uses:",B.Oo,1,"Phonophoresis: ultrasound energy enhances transdermal delivery of topical medications (typically hydrocortisone or diclofenac gel). The ultrasound coupling gel is mixed with or replaced by the medication. Less studied than iontophoresis, and not all medications transmit well through ultrasound. Contraindications are the same as therapeutic ultrasound.")
B.WK=s(["Permanent tissue damage","Cyclical vasodilation after 15-20 minutes of sustained cold application","Increased nerve conduction","Pain elimination"],t.s)
B.a3z=new A.e("The hunting response (Lewis reaction) during cryotherapy refers to:",B.WK,1,"Hunting response (Lewis reaction): after prolonged cold application (15-20+ minutes), the body initiates brief periods of vasodilation (to protect tissue from frost injury) alternating with vasoconstriction. This is a protective mechanism. It does not negate the therapeutic benefit of cold. Application time should be 15-20 minutes to achieve therapeutic cooling without tissue injury.")
B.QV=s([B.a7M,B.a86,B.a6j,B.a7x,B.a3z],t.b)
B.Jf=new A.a6("Physical Modalities 2",B.QV)
B.Yq=s(["Cortical infarct","Periventricular leukomalacia (PVL)","Cerebellar hemorrhage","Basal ganglia injury"],t.s)
B.a5j=new A.e("Spastic diplegia in CP is most commonly associated with which brain lesion?",B.Yq,1,"PVL is the hallmark lesion of spastic diplegia. The periventricular white matter is a vascular watershed zone vulnerable in premature infants. Motor fibers to the lower extremities pass closest to the ventricles, explaining why legs are affected more than arms.")
B.WX=s(["Generalized weakness","Focal spasticity (e.g., equinus from gastrocnemius spasticity)","Seizures","Intellectual disability"],t.s)
B.a6Z=new A.e("Botulinum toxin in pediatric CP is most commonly used to treat:",B.WX,1,"Botox in pediatric CP targets focal spasticity to improve function and delay surgery. Most common target: gastrocnemius/soleus for equinus (toe-walking). Other targets: hamstrings (crouch gait), hip adductors (scissoring). It is an adjunct to stretching, serial casting, and orthotics. Effects last 3-6 months; repeated injections needed.")
B.Y6=s(["All CP children eventually need hip replacement","Spastic hip muscles cause progressive subluxation/dislocation, especially in GMFCS IV-V","Hip fractures are common in CP","It is only needed in hemiplegic CP"],t.s)
B.a43=new A.e("Hip surveillance in cerebral palsy is important because:",B.Y6,1,"Hip displacement in CP: spastic hip flexors/adductors pull the femoral head out of the acetabulum progressively. GMFCS V children have ~90% risk of hip displacement. Screening: AP pelvis X-rays measuring migration percentage. Preventive management: stretching, botox, bracing. Surgical reconstruction if migration percentage >40-50%.")
B.RK=s(["Mild spasticity in GMFCS I","Severe generalized spasticity (GMFCS IV-V) failing oral medications","Dystonic CP only","Ataxic CP"],t.s)
B.a4f=new A.e("Intrathecal baclofen (ITB) pump in pediatric CP is best indicated for:",B.RK,1,"ITB in pediatric CP: best for severe generalized spasticity in non-ambulatory children (GMFCS IV-V) who have failed oral medications. Benefits: improved comfort, ease of care, reduced orthopedic deformities. A screening trial (lumbar puncture with baclofen bolus) must show benefit before pump implantation.")
B.Oc=s(["Performing one surgery at a time over several years","Correcting all significant deformities in a single surgical session","Only soft tissue procedures","Surgery before age 2"],t.s)
B.a4G=new A.e("Single-event multilevel surgery (SEMLS) in CP involves:",B.Oc,1,"SEMLS: multiple orthopedic procedures (tendon lengthenings, transfers, bony corrections) performed in one session, typically at age 7-10 when gait analysis is reliable. Advantages: single anesthesia, single rehabilitation period, addresses crouch gait pattern comprehensively. Requires intensive post-op rehab (6-12 months).")
B.Rf=s([B.a5j,B.a6Z,B.a43,B.a4f,B.a4G],t.b)
B.Jg=new A.a6("Pediatrics 7",B.Rf)
B.Nf=s(["Shoulder abduction and elbow flexion","Wrist extension and biceps (elbow flexion)","Triceps and wrist flexion","Hand intrinsic muscles"],t.s)
B.a6m=new A.e("A C6 radiculopathy presents with weakness of:",B.Nf,1,"C6 radiculopathy: weakness of wrist extension (ECRL/ECRB) and biceps (elbow flexion). Sensory loss is in the thumb and lateral forearm. The biceps and brachioradialis reflexes are diminished. The most common cause is C5-C6 disc herniation.")
B.Pr=s(["Cervical myelopathy","Non-organic (psychogenic) components of low back pain","Cauda equina syndrome","Lumbar spinal stenosis"],t.s)
B.acz=new A.e("The Waddell signs are used to identify:",B.Pr,1,"Waddell signs (5 categories): superficial/non-anatomic tenderness, simulation tests (axial loading, rotation), distraction tests (SLR discrepancy), regional disturbances (non-dermatomal weakness/sensory loss), and overreaction. Three or more positive categories suggest non-organic factors.")
B.O8=s(["Cervical spine pain","Lumbar disc herniation","Sacroiliac joint inflammation (sacroiliitis)","Peripheral joint arthritis"],t.s)
B.a7g=new A.e("Ankylosing spondylitis most commonly begins with:",B.O8,2,"Ankylosing spondylitis (HLA-B27 associated) typically begins with bilateral sacroiliitis in young adults (20-30 years). It progresses cranially with bamboo spine appearance. Morning stiffness >30 minutes that improves with exercise is characteristic. It affects males more than females.")
B.NU=s(["Dens (odontoid) fracture","Burst fracture of the C1 (atlas) ring","Hangman's fracture of C2","Compression fracture of C7"],t.s)
B.a3R=new A.e("The Jefferson fracture involves:",B.NU,1,"The Jefferson fracture is a burst fracture of the C1 (atlas) ring from axial loading (diving into shallow water, hitting head on car roof). It typically involves fractures of both anterior and posterior arches. It is usually stable because the ring fragments spread outward.")
B.Uw=s(["C1 (atlas)","C2 (axis) \u2014 bilateral pars interarticularis fractures","C3","C7"],t.s)
B.a70=new A.e("A hangman's fracture involves which vertebra?",B.Uw,1,"A hangman's fracture is a bilateral fracture of the C2 pars interarticularis (pedicles) from hyperextension and axial loading. Despite the name, it is now most commonly caused by motor vehicle accidents. It is often neurologically intact because the fracture widens the canal.")
B.Zw=s([B.a6m,B.acz,B.a7g,B.a3R,B.a70],t.b)
B.Jh=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Spine 3",B.Zw)
B.Vp=s(["Resisted external rotation at 0\xb0 abduction","Passive forward flexion with the scapula stabilized","Resisted abduction in the empty can position","Cross-body adduction"],t.s)
B.a9M=new A.e("Neer's impingement test involves:",B.Vp,1,"Neer's test: the examiner stabilizes the scapula and passively forward flexes the arm overhead. Pain indicates impingement of the supraspinatus tendon against the anterior acromion.")
B.PK=s(["Flat","Curved","Hooked","Absent"],t.s)
B.a8F=new A.e("Type III acromion morphology is associated with increased risk of rotator cuff impingement because it is:",B.PK,2,"Bigliani classification: Type I = flat, Type II = curved, Type III = hooked. The hooked (Type III) acromion narrows the subacromial space, increasing mechanical impingement of the supraspinatus tendon.")
B.MX=s(["Resisted external rotation at the side","Forward flexing the arm to 90\xb0 then internally rotating","Resisted abduction in the scapular plane","Cross-body adduction with the arm at 90\xb0"],t.s)
B.ack=new A.e("The Hawkins-Kennedy test for shoulder impingement is performed by:",B.MX,1,"Hawkins-Kennedy: flex the arm to 90\xb0 then forcefully internally rotate. This drives the supraspinatus tendon against the coracoacromial ligament. Pain = positive for impingement.")
B.YR=s(["Axillary nerve","Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve"],t.s)
B.a66=new A.e("Scapular winging is most commonly caused by injury to which nerve?",B.YR,2,"The long thoracic nerve innervates the serratus anterior, which holds the scapula against the chest wall. Injury causes medial winging \u2014 the scapula protrudes posteriorly, especially when pushing against a wall.")
B.RY=s(["Flexion","Abduction","External rotation","Internal rotation"],t.s)
B.a3o=new A.e("Adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder) is characterized by loss of both active AND passive ROM. Which motion is typically lost first?",B.RY,2,"External rotation is typically lost first and most severely in adhesive capsulitis because the inferior glenohumeral ligament (tightest in external rotation) is the first capsular structure to contract. The capsular pattern is external rotation > abduction > internal rotation.")
B.Wk=s([B.a9M,B.a8F,B.ack,B.a66,B.a3o],t.b)
B.Ji=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Shoulder 2",B.Wk)
B.Uc=s(["The spine","The upper extremities","The paralyzed lower extremities, especially the distal femur and proximal tibia","The skull"],t.s)
B.aaw=new A.e("Osteoporosis in SCI occurs primarily in:",B.Uc,2,"SCI osteoporosis is most severe in the sublesional skeleton, particularly the distal femur and proximal tibia. Bone loss begins within days of injury and can reach 30-50% in the first year. This dramatically increases fracture risk from minor trauma. Weight-bearing, FES cycling, and bisphosphonates may help.")
B.Qi=s(["Easy to treat with NSAIDs","Burning, shooting pain below the injury level caused by spinal cord damage","Always temporary","Only present in incomplete injuries"],t.s)
B.a6d=new A.e("Pain in SCI is classified as nociceptive or neuropathic. Below-level neuropathic pain is:",B.Qi,1,"Below-level neuropathic pain occurs at or below the injury level due to spinal cord damage itself. It is often burning, shooting, or electric in quality. It is notoriously difficult to treat. First-line: gabapentin/pregabalin or TCAs. It occurs in both complete and incomplete injuries.")
B.Tx=s(["The heart cannot regulate temperature","Loss of sympathetic control of sweating and vasomotor tone below the injury","The thyroid gland is damaged","CSF cannot circulate properly"],t.s)
B.a7z=new A.e("Temperature regulation is impaired in SCI above T6 because:",B.Tx,1,"Poikilothermia: SCI above T6 disrupts sympathetic control of sweating (thermoregulation) and vasomotor tone below the lesion. The body cannot sweat or vasoconstrict/vasodilate below the injury to regulate temperature. Patients are at risk for hypothermia and hyperthermia based on environmental temperature.")
B.MJ=s(["It always indicates recovery","It can assist with transfers, maintain muscle bulk, and improve venous return","It prevents pressure ulcers","It improves bowel function"],t.s)
B.a82=new A.e("Spasticity in SCI can sometimes be beneficial because:",B.MJ,1,"Beneficial spasticity: maintains muscle bulk (preventing atrophy), assists with standing transfers (extensor spasms keep legs straight), improves venous return (muscle contractions pump blood), and can assist with bladder emptying. Treatment should only target spasticity that interferes with function or causes pain.")
B.PR=s(["Renal failure","Respiratory complications (pneumonia)","Cardiovascular disease","Pressure ulcer sepsis"],t.s)
B.aae=new A.e("The most common cause of death in chronic SCI is:",B.PR,2,"Cardiovascular disease has overtaken renal failure as the leading cause of death in chronic SCI (due to improved urological management). SCI patients have accelerated atherosclerosis from sedentary lifestyle, abnormal lipid profiles, and metabolic syndrome. Respiratory complications remain a close second, especially in tetraplegia.")
B.Pk=s([B.aaw,B.a6d,B.a7z,B.a82,B.aae],t.b)
B.Jj=new A.a6("Spinal Cord Injury 8",B.Pk)
B.XS=s(["Skin breakdown","Bowel impaction","Bladder distension","Deep vein thrombosis"],t.s)
B.a9f=new A.e("The most common cause of autonomic dysreflexia is:",B.XS,2,"Bladder distension (blocked catheter, UTI, overdistended bladder) accounts for ~85% of autonomic dysreflexia episodes. The second most common cause is bowel impaction. First step: sit the patient up (orthostatic drop helps lower BP), then check the catheter and bladder.")
B.Yc=s(["Administering IV labetalol immediately","Sitting the patient upright and checking for a kinked catheter","Ordering a CT head","Giving oral clonidine and waiting"],t.s)
B.a60=new A.e("A T4 paraplegic patient with sudden severe headache and BP of 220/120 should be managed by:",B.Yc,1,"Autonomic dysreflexia emergency protocol: (1) Sit patient upright immediately, (2) Loosen clothing/binder, (3) Check bladder \u2014 straight catheterize or flush existing catheter, (4) If no bladder cause, check for fecal impaction (use lidocaine jelly). Antihypertensives (nitropaste, nifedipine) only if BP remains elevated after removing the stimulus.")
B.W3=s(["Areflexic bowel with absent anal tone","Hyperreflexic bowel with intact anal wink and digital stimulation effective","Normal bowel function","Constant fecal incontinence"],t.s)
B.a4n=new A.e("Neurogenic bowel in upper motor neuron SCI (above the conus) is characterized by:",B.W3,1,"UMN bowel (above conus): intact reflex arc, increased anal tone, digital stimulation triggers reflex defecation. LMN bowel (conus/cauda equina): areflexic, lax anal tone, no reflex defecation \u2014 requires manual evacuation and Valsalva. The bowel program schedule (every other day, timed) is critical for both.")
B.Xw=s(["First 2 weeks post-injury","Months 3-6","After 1 year","Only during acute hospitalization"],t.s)
B.a3Y=new A.e("SCI patients are at highest risk for deep vein thrombosis (DVT) during which period?",B.Xw,0,"DVT risk is highest in the first 2 weeks after SCI (up to 100% without prophylaxis in some studies). Prophylaxis includes LMWH or unfractionated heparin, compression stockings, and pneumatic compression devices. DVT prophylaxis is typically continued for 8-12 weeks post-injury.")
B.So=s(["Shoulder","Elbow","Hip","Knee"],t.s)
B.a7l=new A.e("Heterotopic ossification (HO) in SCI most commonly occurs around which joint?",B.So,2,"The hip is the most common site for HO in SCI (as in TBI). It occurs below the level of injury. Presents with decreased ROM, swelling, and warmth. Triple-phase bone scan is the most sensitive early test. Alkaline phosphatase is elevated. Treatment: NSAIDs (indomethacin), etidronate; surgical excision only after maturation (12-18 months).")
B.PU=s([B.a9f,B.a60,B.a4n,B.a3Y,B.a7l],t.b)
B.Jk=new A.a6("Spinal Cord Injury 3",B.PU)
B.WY=s(["Different genes are affected","Duchenne has an out-of-frame deletion (no dystrophin); Becker has an in-frame deletion (some dystrophin)","Duchenne is autosomal dominant; Becker is autosomal recessive","Duchenne affects Type I fibers; Becker affects Type II fibers"],t.s)
B.abC=new A.e('The "reading frame hypothesis" explains the difference between Duchenne and Becker muscular dystrophy as:',B.WY,1,"Both affect the dystrophin gene on the X chromosome. Duchenne = out-of-frame deletion producing no functional dystrophin (severe, wheelchair by 12, death by 20s). Becker = in-frame deletion producing shortened but partially functional dystrophin (milder, later onset).")
B.RX=s(["Low amplitude MUAPs","Short duration MUAPs","Polyphasic MUAPs","Decreased recruitment (reduced interference pattern)"],t.s)
B.aaq=new A.e("The classic EMG triad of myopathy includes all EXCEPT:",B.RX,3,"Myopathic EMG triad: low amplitude, short duration, polyphasic MUAPs (sounds like rain on a tin roof). Crucially, recruitment is EARLY \u2014 the brain compensates for weak motor units by recruiting more units sooner. Decreased recruitment is a NEUROPATHIC pattern.")
B.Yi=s(["Myasthenia gravis","Duchenne muscular dystrophy","ALS","Guillain-Barr\xe9 syndrome"],t.s)
B.a52=new A.e("Gower's sign (walking hands up the thighs to stand) is pathognomonic for:",B.Yi,1,'Gower\'s sign is pathognomonic for Duchenne MD. The child uses their arms to "climb up" their own legs because proximal hip and thigh muscles are too weak to stand independently. Also characteristic: pseudohypertrophy of calves (fat/fibrosis replacement).')
B.Z7=s(["Proximal weakness only","Distal weakness with multisystem involvement","Pure motor neuron degeneration","Immune-mediated muscle inflammation"],t.s)
B.a5V=new A.e("Myotonic dystrophy (Steinert disease) is unique among myopathies because it causes:",B.Z7,1,"Myotonic dystrophy is distinctive for causing DISTAL weakness (opposite of most myopathies), plus multisystem effects: hatchet face, frontal balding, ptosis, cataracts, cardiac conduction defects, diabetes, testicular atrophy. EMG shows dive-bomber myotonic discharges.")
B.T9=s(["Marching soldiers","Rain on a tin roof","Dive bombers","Machine gun fire"],t.s)
B.a7f=new A.e("The characteristic EMG sound in myotonic dystrophy is described as:",B.T9,2,'Myotonic discharges produce a "dive bomber" sound \u2014 a waxing and waning frequency and amplitude pattern. Myotonia is worsened by cold and improved by warm-up (repeated muscle activation). This auditory pattern is one of the most recognizable EMG findings.')
B.TW=s([B.abC,B.aaq,B.a52,B.a5V,B.a7f],t.b)
B.Jl=new A.a6("Electrodiagnostic 14",B.TW)
B.Yg=s(["Supraspinatus","Infraspinatus","Deltoid","Teres minor"],t.s)
B.aac=new A.e("The rotator cuff is composed of four muscles. Which muscle is NOT part of the rotator cuff?",B.Yg,2,"The rotator cuff consists of the SITS muscles: Supraspinatus, Infraspinatus, Teres minor, and Subscapularis. The deltoid is the primary shoulder abductor but is not part of the rotator cuff.")
B.MV=s(["Subscapularis","Infraspinatus","Supraspinatus","Teres minor"],t.s)
B.a7P=new A.e("Which rotator cuff muscle is most commonly torn?",B.MV,2,'The supraspinatus is most commonly torn due to its location in the subacromial space and its relatively poor blood supply in the "critical zone" near its insertion on the greater tuberosity.')
B.XX=s(["Infraspinatus","Subscapularis","Supraspinatus","Teres minor"],t.s)
B.abo=new A.e('The "empty can" test primarily evaluates which muscle?',B.XX,2,"The empty can (Jobe) test: arm at 90\xb0 abduction, 30\xb0 forward flexion, internally rotated (thumb down). Resist downward pressure. Weakness or pain = supraspinatus pathology.")
B.PE=s(["Reduced blood supply","Inherent instability","Limited nerve innervation","Decreased muscle attachment points"],t.s)
B.a4S=new A.e("The glenohumeral joint has the greatest range of motion of any joint in the body. What is the primary trade-off?",B.PE,1,"The glenohumeral joint sacrifices stability for mobility. The glenoid fossa is shallow (covers only ~25% of the humeral head), making it the most commonly dislocated joint. Stability relies on the labrum, capsule, rotator cuff, and ligaments.")
B.PJ=s(["Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Axillary nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve"],t.s)
B.a3e=new A.e("Anterior shoulder dislocations most commonly injure which nerve?",B.PJ,2,'The axillary nerve wraps around the surgical neck of the humerus and is most vulnerable during anterior dislocations. Test for deltoid weakness and decreased sensation over the lateral "regimental badge" area. Incidence increases with age.')
B.O3=s([B.aac,B.a7P,B.abo,B.a4S,B.a3e],t.b)
B.Jm=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Shoulder 1",B.O3)
B.Re=s(["Bone marrow only","Skin, muscle, and nerve in the radiation field","The entire body systemically","Only lymphatic tissue"],t.s)
B.aal=new A.e("Radiation fibrosis syndrome most commonly affects which tissues?",B.Re,1,"Radiation fibrosis is a progressive process affecting skin, subcutaneous tissue, muscle, and nerves within the radiation field. It can cause contractures, neuropathy, and lymphedema. It may present months to years after radiation therapy.")
B.XM=s(["Diuretic therapy","Surgical debulking","Complete decongestive therapy (CDT)","Compression stockings alone"],t.s)
B.aax=new A.e("The cornerstone treatment for cancer-related lymphedema is:",B.XM,2,"Complete decongestive therapy (CDT) is the gold standard for lymphedema management. It includes manual lymphatic drainage, compression bandaging, exercise, and skin care. Diuretics are generally not effective for lymphedema.")
B.RS=s(["Gentle exercise","Blood pressure measurement and venipuncture","Wearing loose clothing","Elevation of the extremity"],t.s)
B.a94=new A.e("A patient with a history of breast cancer and axillary node dissection should avoid which of the following in the affected arm?",B.RS,1,"Blood pressure measurement, venipuncture, and IV access should be avoided in the affected arm after axillary lymph node dissection to reduce lymphedema risk. Gentle exercise and elevation are encouraged.")
B.Qb=s(["Myasthenia gravis","Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome","Dermatomyositis","Polymyositis"],t.s)
B.aat=new A.e("Which paraneoplastic syndrome is associated with proximal muscle weakness that improves with repeated use?",B.Qb,1,"Lambert-Eaton syndrome is a paraneoplastic condition (commonly associated with small cell lung cancer) featuring proximal weakness that characteristically improves with repeated use (opposite of myasthenia gravis). It involves antibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels.")
B.Qc=s(["Humerus","Femur","Vertebra","Pelvis"],t.s)
B.a6O=new A.e("Pathologic fractures in cancer patients most commonly occur in which bone?",B.Qc,1,"The femur is the most common site for pathologic fractures due to metastatic disease. The proximal femur bears significant weight-loading forces. Prophylactic fixation should be considered when >50% of cortical bone is destroyed.")
B.ZI=s([B.aal,B.aax,B.a94,B.aat,B.a6O],t.b)
B.Jn=new A.a6("Cancer Rehab 2",B.ZI)
B.XU=s(["An entire motor unit","A single muscle fiber","A group of motor units","The neuromuscular junction"],t.s)
B.ac6=new A.e("Fibrillation potentials on EMG represent the spontaneous firing of:",B.XU,1,"Fibrillations are spontaneous discharges of individual denervated muscle fibers. They are not visible through the skin (unlike fasciculations) and produce a high-pitched ticking sound at 20-200 \u03bcV amplitude.")
B.Y_=s(["Benign fasciculations fire at higher rates","Pathologic fasciculations are larger in amplitude","You cannot \u2014 clinical context and the company they keep (fibrillations) are key","Benign fasciculations occur only at rest"],t.s)
B.acl=new A.e("How can you distinguish benign fasciculations from pathologic fasciculations on EMG?",B.Y_,2,'Fasciculations cannot be reliably differentiated as benign vs pathologic by EMG waveform alone. The "company they keep" principle: fasciculations accompanied by fibrillation potentials and other signs of denervation suggest pathology (ALS, radiculopathy).')
B.M2=s(["Rain on a tin roof","Dive bombers","Marching soldiers","Machine gun fire"],t.s)
B.a99=new A.e("Myokymic discharges on EMG produce a characteristic sound described as:",B.M2,2,'Myokymia produces a "marching soldiers" sound \u2014 rhythmic grouped bursts of motor unit potentials. It is classically associated with radiation-induced plexopathy and helps distinguish radiation damage from tumor recurrence.')
B.VT=s(["Simple","Biphasic","Triphasic","Polyphasic"],t.s)
B.ac1=new A.e("A motor unit action potential (MUAP) with more than 4 phases is described as:",B.VT,3,"Normal MUAPs have 2-4 phases. MUAPs with >4 phases are polyphasic, indicating remodeling of the motor unit \u2014 either from reinnervation (neuropathic) or fiber splitting/regeneration (myopathic). Up to 15% polyphasic MUAPs can be normal.")
B.Um=s(["The number of fibers near the needle tip","The entire territory (spatial extent) of the motor unit","The conduction velocity of the nerve","The thickness of the myelin sheath"],t.s)
B.acm=new A.e("MUAP duration on EMG primarily reflects:",B.Um,1,"MUAP duration represents the entire motor unit territory \u2014 it reflects the timing of all muscle fibers in that motor unit firing. Duration increases in neuropathic conditions (reinnervation creates larger territories) and decreases in myopathies (lost fibers = smaller territory).")
B.Xg=s([B.ac6,B.acl,B.a99,B.ac1,B.acm],t.b)
B.Jo=new A.a6("Electrodiagnostic 6",B.Xg)
B.Zt=s(["Full cervical immobilization","Moderate restriction of flexion/extension with limited rotational control","Only rotational control","No meaningful motion restriction"],t.s)
B.a8S=new A.e("A cervical orthosis (Philadelphia collar) provides:",B.Zt,1,"Philadelphia collar: semi-rigid, two-piece design (anterior + posterior). Restricts ~70% of flexion/extension but only ~30-50% of rotation and lateral bending. NOT adequate for unstable cervical fractures. For greater immobilization, a halo vest (restricts ~96% of motion) or SOMI brace is needed.")
B.T3=s(["C1 to the sacrum","T6 to the sacrum","The lumbar spine only","The cervical spine only"],t.s)
B.abp=new A.e("A TLSO (thoracolumbosacral orthosis) controls motion primarily from:",B.T3,1,"TLSO: controls motion from approximately T6 to the sacrum. It does not effectively control upper thoracic or cervical motion. For fractures above T6, a cervicothoracolumbosacral orthosis (CTLSO) or halo vest is needed. Common TLSOs include the Jewett hyperextension brace and custom-molded TLSOs.")
B.Xr=s(["Lateral bending","Flexion of the thoracolumbar spine","Extension of the cervical spine","Rotation of the lumbar spine"],t.s)
B.a6a=new A.e("The Jewett hyperextension brace is designed to prevent:",B.Xr,1,"Jewett brace: three-point pressure system (sternal pad, suprapubic pad, posterior pad) that prevents flexion of the thoracolumbar spine. Used for stable compression fractures T6-L1. It does NOT control rotation or lateral bending. Contraindicated for burst fractures or posterior element injuries (which need circumferential control).")
B.QK=s(["Cervical fractures","Adolescent idiopathic scoliosis","Ankle instability","Carpal tunnel syndrome"],t.s)
B.a8p=new A.e("A Boston brace is used to treat:",B.QK,1,"Boston brace: underarm TLSO used for adolescent idiopathic scoliosis. It applies corrective forces through pads placed over the convexity of the curve. Indications: curves 25-40\xb0 in skeletally immature patients (Risser 0-2). Worn 18-23 hours/day. Goal: prevent progression, not correct the curve. Surgery indicated for curves >40-50\xb0.")
B.RL=s(["Minor cervical sprains","Unstable cervical fractures (e.g., C2 fractures, burst fractures)","Lumbar disc herniations","Thoracic scoliosis"],t.s)
B.a3M=new A.e("Halo vest orthosis provides the MOST cervical spine immobilization and is indicated for:",B.RL,1,"Halo vest: gold standard for cervical immobilization (~96% restriction of all motions). Four skull pins attach the halo ring; vest provides counterforce. Indications: unstable C-spine fractures (odontoid type II, hangman, unstable burst fractures), post-surgical stabilization. Complications: pin loosening, infection, pin-site ring sequestrum, restricted pulmonary function.")
B.W1=s([B.a8S,B.abp,B.a6a,B.a8p,B.a3M],t.b)
B.Jp=new A.a6("Prosthetics & Orthotics 6",B.W1)
B.Q7=s(["0 to 100","7 to 49","18 to 126","0 to 42"],t.s)
B.a9b=new A.e("The FIM (Functional Independence Measure) scores range from:",B.Q7,2,"The FIM has 18 items, each scored 1-7, giving a total range of 18 (total dependence) to 126 (complete independence). It measures motor (13 items, max 91) and cognitive (5 items, max 35) domains.")
B.My=s(["Patient age","Initial stroke severity","Time to rehabilitation admission","Type of stroke (ischemic vs hemorrhagic)"],t.s)
B.a4p=new A.e("In stroke rehabilitation, the strongest predictor of functional outcome is:",B.My,1,"Initial stroke severity (as measured by NIHSS or initial functional status) is the strongest predictor of functional outcome. While age and time to rehab admission matter, the severity of the initial neurological deficit is most predictive.")
B.WA=s(["Within 24 hours","Within 72 hours","As soon as medically stable","After 2 weeks of acute care"],t.s)
B.a9x=new A.e("The optimal time to begin inpatient rehabilitation after stroke is:",B.WA,2,"Current guidelines recommend beginning inpatient rehabilitation as soon as the patient is medically stable and can participate in at least 3 hours of therapy per day. Earlier initiation is associated with better outcomes, but the patient must be stable.")
B.Po=s(["Patient requires one therapy discipline only","Patient can tolerate 3 hours of therapy per day","Patient requires only nursing care","Patient has completed all acute medical treatment"],t.s)
B.a5i=new A.e("Which of the following is a criterion for admission to an acute inpatient rehabilitation facility (IRF)?",B.Po,1,'IRF admission requires that the patient can participate in and benefit from at least 3 hours of therapy per day (the "3-hour rule"), needs at least 2 therapy disciplines, requires physician supervision, and is expected to make functional improvement.')
B.MN=s(["Left (dominant)","Right (non-dominant)","Bilateral","Posterior fossa"],t.s)
B.abV=new A.e("A stroke patient demonstrates neglect of the left side, anosognosia, and impaired spatial awareness. The lesion is most likely in which hemisphere?",B.MN,1,"Left-sided neglect, anosognosia (denial of deficit), and impaired spatial awareness are classic findings of right (non-dominant) hemisphere strokes. Left hemisphere strokes more commonly produce aphasia. Neglect is a significant predictor of poorer rehabilitation outcomes.")
B.VE=s([B.a9b,B.a4p,B.a9x,B.a5i,B.abV],t.b)
B.Jq=new A.a6("Cerebrovascular Accident 6",B.VE)
B.Uj=s(["A socket-based attachment system","A titanium implant directly fixed to the bone, with an abutment percutaneously connecting to the prosthesis","A suction suspension method","An elastic sleeve"],t.s)
B.a5I=new A.e("Osseointegration in prosthetics involves:",B.Uj,1,"Osseointegration: a titanium fixture is surgically implanted into the residual bone (femur or humerus). After integration, a percutaneous abutment connects to the external prosthesis. Benefits: eliminates socket-related problems, improved proprioception (osseoperception), better ROM. Risks: infection at skin-implant interface, periprosthetic fracture.")
B.PS=s(["No wrapping needed","A shrinker sock or elastic bandage in a figure-8 pattern","A rigid cast","Hot packs to increase circulation"],t.s)
B.a8m=new A.e("The appropriate volume management tool for a newly amputated residual limb that is ready for prosthetic fitting is:",B.PS,1,"Shrinker socks (elastic compressive garments) or figure-8 elastic bandage wrapping: applied to mature the residual limb (reduce edema, shape for socket fitting). Figure-8 wrapping should be distal-to-proximal with more pressure distally. The limb must be edema-free and conically shaped before definitive prosthetic casting.")
B.TN=s(["It is heavier","It allows free knee flexion during swing phase while locking in stance for stability","It does not control the knee at all","It only works with a specific shoe"],t.s)
B.a5Z=new A.e("A stance-control KAFO differs from a locked KAFO in that:",B.TN,1,"Stance-control KAFO (SCKAFO): electronic or mechanical system locks the knee during stance (preventing buckling) but allows free knee motion during swing (more natural gait). Advantages over locked KAFO: more normal gait pattern, reduced energy cost, improved cosmesis. Requires adequate hip flexor strength and proprioception to trigger the unlock.")
B.WQ=s(["Age alone","Overall cardiovascular reserve and cognitive ability to learn prosthetic use","Insurance status","The cause of amputation"],t.s)
B.aah=new A.e("The most critical factor in determining prosthetic candidacy for a dysvascular amputee is:",B.WQ,1,"Prosthetic candidacy depends on: cardiovascular reserve (can the patient meet the energy demands?), cognitive function (can they learn safe prosthetic use?), motivation, wound healing status, vision, upper extremity strength for transfers, and social support. Age alone is NOT a contraindication. Many elderly dysvascular amputees succeed with appropriate prosthetic prescription.")
B.S4=s(["Socket friction and shear","Allergic contact dermatitis to liner materials","Poor socket hygiene","Wearing the prosthesis for too few hours per day"],t.s)
B.ac4=new A.e("Skin problems are the most common prosthetic complication. Which is NOT a typical cause?",B.S4,3,"Common prosthetic skin issues: friction/shear (mechanical), allergic contact dermatitis (liner materials), folliculitis (hair follicle infection from friction/moisture), verrucous hyperplasia (from distal suction in a poorly fitting socket), and excessive sweating. Wearing TOO MUCH (not too little) causes most skin problems. Daily socket and liner hygiene is essential.")
B.TC=s([B.a5I,B.a8m,B.a5Z,B.aah,B.ac4],t.b)
B.Jr=new A.a6("Prosthetics & Orthotics 10",B.TC)
B.VQ=s(["Conduction","Convection","Conversion (mechanical vibration \u2192 thermal energy)","Radiation"],t.s)
B.ab6=new A.e("Therapeutic ultrasound heats tissue through:",B.VQ,2,"Ultrasound: acoustic energy (1-3 MHz) is absorbed by tissue and CONVERTED to heat (conversion heating). It is a deep heating modality reaching 3-5 cm. Frequency affects depth: 1 MHz = deeper (3-5 cm), 3 MHz = superficial (1-2 cm). Intensity: 1-2 W/cm\xb2. Heats collagen-rich tissues preferentially (tendon, ligament, scar tissue, joint capsule).")
B.WR=s(["Normal muscle tissue","Growth plates (epiphyseal plates) in children, malignancies, and the pregnant uterus","Chronic tendinopathy","Post-surgical scar tissue"],t.s)
B.a9H=new A.e("Ultrasound is contraindicated over:",B.WR,1,"Ultrasound contraindications: growth plates (risk of premature closure), malignancy (could accelerate growth/metastasis), pregnant uterus, thrombophlebitis/DVT (risk of embolization), cemented joint prostheses (loosening), pacemakers, eyes, brain, spinal cord (after laminectomy), reproductive organs, and infected tissue.")
B.M7=s(["Hot packs (hydrocollator)","Paraffin wax","Therapeutic ultrasound","Fluidotherapy"],t.s)
B.a8B=new A.e("Superficial heating modalities include all EXCEPT:",B.M7,2,"Superficial heating (penetrates to ~1-2 cm): hot packs, paraffin wax, fluidotherapy, warm whirlpool, infrared lamps. These heat skin and subcutaneous tissue only. Deep heating modalities (penetrate >3 cm): therapeutic ultrasound, shortwave diathermy, microwave diathermy. The distinction matters for treating deep vs. superficial pathology.")
B.Xl=s(["Slowing nerve conduction velocity","Reducing muscle spindle activity","Causing vasodilation","Reducing metabolic rate and inflammation"],t.s)
B.ab3=new A.e("Cryotherapy (cold) reduces pain through all EXCEPT:",B.Xl,2,"Cryotherapy mechanisms: slows nerve conduction (pain signals), reduces muscle spindle firing (decreases spasticity), reduces metabolic rate (limits secondary injury), causes vasoconstriction (reduces edema/inflammation), and increases pain threshold. Initial vasoconstriction occurs; hunting response (Lewis reaction) is brief vasodilation after prolonged cold \u2014 NOT the primary therapeutic mechanism.")
B.MO=s(["Directly healing tissue","Gate control theory \u2014 large fiber stimulation inhibits small fiber pain transmission at the spinal cord","Muscle strengthening","Increasing blood pressure"],t.s)
B.a8v=new A.e("TENS (transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation) works primarily through:",B.MO,1,"TENS: applies electrical stimulation to large myelinated A-beta fibers, which inhibit pain transmission from small A-delta and C fibers at the dorsal horn (gate control theory, Melzack and Wall). Conventional/high-frequency TENS (50-100 Hz): gate control. Low-frequency/acupuncture-like TENS (2-4 Hz): endogenous endorphin release. Non-invasive, minimal side effects.")
B.Mi=s([B.ab6,B.a9H,B.a8B,B.ab3,B.a8v],t.b)
B.Js=new A.a6("Physical Modalities 1",B.Mi)
B.OA=s(["Presynaptic \u2014 reduced ACh release","Postsynaptic \u2014 antibodies against ACh receptors","Presynaptic \u2014 blocked calcium channels","Synaptic \u2014 excess acetylcholinesterase"],t.s)
B.a72=new A.e("In myasthenia gravis (MG), the neuromuscular junction defect is:",B.OA,1,"MG is a postsynaptic disorder \u2014 autoantibodies attack ACh receptors, reducing the number of available receptors and simplifying the postsynaptic membrane. Quantum content (release) is normal, but quantum response is reduced.")
B.Sa=s(["Weakness that worsens with use and preserved reflexes","Weakness that improves with exercise and depressed reflexes","Pure sensory symptoms","Normal NMJ function on repetitive nerve stimulation"],t.s)
B.a8_=new A.e("Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (LEMS) differs from MG in that LEMS shows:",B.Sa,1,"LEMS: weakness IMPROVES with exercise (warm-up phenomenon), reflexes are depressed/absent. MG: weakness WORSENS with use, reflexes preserved. LEMS is presynaptic (anti-VGCC antibodies) and associated with small cell lung cancer in ~60% of cases.")
B.UB=s(["Decrement at low rate only","Decrement at low rate with INCREMENT at high rate (post-tetanic potentiation)","No change at any rate","Increment at all rates"],t.s)
B.aa0=new A.e("On repetitive nerve stimulation (RNS), LEMS shows which pattern?",B.UB,1,"LEMS shows decrement at low-rate stimulation (2-5 Hz) \u2014 like MG \u2014 but characteristically shows a dramatic INCREMENT (>100% increase) at high-rate stimulation (20-50 Hz) or after brief maximal voluntary contraction. This post-tetanic potentiation is diagnostic for LEMS.")
B.Oa=s(["Conduction velocity between nodes","Jitter \u2014 the variability in transmission time between nerve and muscle","CMAP amplitude decline","F-wave latency"],t.s)
B.aaY=new A.e("Single-fiber EMG (SFEMG) is the most sensitive test for NMJ disorders because it measures:",B.Oa,1,"SFEMG measures jitter \u2014 the variability in neuromuscular transmission time between successive firings. Increased jitter and blocking (intermittent transmission failure) are the earliest and most sensitive findings in NMJ disorders like MG.")
B.Rs=s(["ACh receptor binding","Acetylcholinesterase activity","SNARE proteins (preventing vesicle exocytosis)","Voltage-gated calcium channels"],t.s)
B.a6l=new A.e("Botulism toxin causes NMJ dysfunction by blocking:",B.Rs,2,"Botulinum toxin blocks SNARE proteins, the molecular machinery required for synaptic vesicle fusion and ACh release (exocytosis). It is a presynaptic disorder like LEMS, but the mechanism is different \u2014 toxin destruction vs autoimmune blockade.")
B.UJ=s([B.a72,B.a8_,B.aa0,B.aaY,B.a6l],t.b)
B.Jt=new A.a6("Electrodiagnostic 13",B.UJ)
B.Vw=s(["Hypernatremia and dehydration","Hyponatremia with concentrated urine and low serum osmolality","Diabetes insipidus","Hyperkalemia"],t.s)
B.a7s=new A.e("Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion (SIADH) after TBI presents with:",B.Vw,1,"SIADH causes excess water retention: hyponatremia, low serum osmolality, concentrated urine (inappropriately high urine osmolality), and euvolemia or mild hypervolemia. Treatment is fluid restriction. Severe hyponatremia can cause seizures.")
B.O6=s(["Adrenal glands","Posterior pituitary/hypothalamus","Anterior pituitary","Thyroid gland"],t.s)
B.a4h=new A.e("Diabetes insipidus (DI) after TBI is caused by damage to which structure?",B.O6,1,"Central DI results from damage to the posterior pituitary or hypothalamus, reducing ADH secretion. It presents with massive polyuria (dilute urine), hypernatremia, and polydipsia. Treatment is desmopressin (DDAVP). It is the opposite of SIADH.")
B.PA=s(["Immediate surgical intervention","Oral baclofen or tizanidine with concurrent stretching/positioning","High-dose benzodiazepines","Immediate botulinum toxin injections"],t.s)
B.a7c=new A.e("Spasticity after TBI is best initially managed with:",B.PA,1,"Initial spasticity management is conservative: stretching, positioning, serial casting, splinting, and oral medications (baclofen, tizanidine, dantrolene). Botulinum toxin is added for focal spasticity. Intrathecal baclofen pump is reserved for severe generalized spasticity not responsive to oral medications.")
B.VP=s(["CN II (optic)","CN I (olfactory)","CN VII (facial)","CN III (oculomotor)"],t.s)
B.a4v=new A.e("The most common cranial nerve injured in TBI is:",B.VP,1,"CN I (olfactory) is the most commonly injured cranial nerve in TBI because the delicate olfactory nerve fibers shear as they pass through the cribriform plate. Anosmia (loss of smell) occurs in up to 30% of severe TBI. It is often permanent and also impairs taste.")
B.OR=s(["Hemiplegia and aphasia","Headache, dizziness, cognitive difficulties, irritability, and sleep disturbance","Bilateral lower extremity weakness","Visual field cuts and prosopagnosia"],t.s)
B.a6s=new A.e("Postconcussion syndrome includes which constellation of symptoms?",B.OR,1,"Postconcussion syndrome: headache (most common), dizziness, fatigue, irritability, cognitive difficulties (memory, concentration), sleep disturbance, and emotional lability. Symptoms typically resolve within 3 months but can persist longer. Management is primarily symptomatic and reassurance.")
B.LZ=s([B.a7s,B.a4h,B.a7c,B.a4v,B.a6s],t.b)
B.Ju=new A.a6("Traumatic Brain Injury 3",B.LZ)
B.Qv=s(["AIS A only","AIS B and C","AIS D","All grades"],t.s)
B.acx=new A.e("The zone of partial preservation (ZPP) is defined in which ASIA grade?",B.Qv,0,"Zone of partial preservation (ZPP) is only defined in AIS A (complete) injuries. It refers to segments below the neurological level that have partial sensory or motor preservation. It has prognostic significance \u2014 a larger ZPP suggests potential for some recovery.")
B.Wy=s(["Biceps","Deltoid","Triceps (elbow extension)","Wrist flexors"],t.s)
B.a3g=new A.e("The key muscle for the C7 myotome is:",B.Wy,2,"C7 key muscle = triceps (elbow extension). C5 = elbow flexors (biceps). C6 = wrist extensors. C8 = finger flexors (FDP). T1 = finger abductors (first dorsal interosseous). These are the ASIA key muscles used to determine motor level.")
B.Mc=s(["Walk with braces","Transfer independently and propel a manual wheelchair","Only use a power wheelchair","Perform all ADLs without equipment"],t.s)
B.a5a=new A.e("A patient with C7 complete SCI can independently:",B.Mc,1,'C7 (intact triceps) is a major functional milestone \u2014 patients can transfer independently (push-up transfer), propel a manual wheelchair effectively, drive with hand controls, and perform most ADLs independently. C7 is often called the "rehab level" because of this dramatic increase in independence.')
B.Tv=s(["Cardiac dysfunction","Loss of sympathetic vasoconstriction below the injury preventing venous return","Excessive fluid loss","Medication side effects only"],t.s)
B.a6H=new A.e("Orthostatic hypotension in SCI is caused by:",B.Tv,1,"Orthostatic hypotension in SCI results from loss of sympathetic vasoconstriction below the injury, causing venous pooling in the lower extremities and abdomen. Management: compression stockings, abdominal binder, gradual upright positioning (tilt table), and midodrine or fludrocortisone if refractory.")
B.X2=s(["Sudden complete paralysis","Progressive loss of pain and temperature sensation (cape-like distribution) and motor function above the original level","Improved motor function","Isolated bowel/bladder changes"],t.s)
B.a4X=new A.e("Syringomyelia after SCI presents with:",B.X2,1,"Post-traumatic syringomyelia: a fluid-filled cyst (syrinx) expands within the cord, progressively destroying the central gray matter. Symptoms ascend above the original injury level: loss of pain/temperature in a cape-like distribution, new weakness, increased spasticity, or pain. MRI confirms; surgical drainage may be needed.")
B.O7=s([B.acx,B.a3g,B.a5a,B.a6H,B.a4X],t.b)
B.Jv=new A.a6("Spinal Cord Injury 4",B.O7)
B.ST=s(["Landau and parachute reflexes","Moro and asymmetric tonic neck reflex (ATNR)","Righting reflexes","Equilibrium reactions"],t.s)
B.a4O=new A.e("Primitive reflexes that should integrate (disappear) by 6 months include:",B.ST,1,"Moro reflex (startle response) integrates by 5-6 months. ATNR (fencing posture) integrates by 6 months. Persistence beyond these ages suggests CNS pathology (CP). Protective/parachute reflexes EMERGE at 6-9 months and persist throughout life. Righting and equilibrium reactions also develop later and persist.")
B.Rc=s(["3 months","6 months","9 months","12 months"],t.s)
B.acs=new A.e("A typically developing child should be able to sit independently by:",B.Rc,1,"Gross motor milestones: head control 3 months, roll over 4-5 months, sit independently 6 months, crawl 8-9 months, pull to stand 9-10 months, walk independently 12 months (range 9-15 months). Delays beyond 2 standard deviations warrant evaluation.")
B.Wa=s(["Scoliosis only","Atlantoaxial instability (C1-C2)","Hip avascular necrosis","Pathologic long bone fractures"],t.s)
B.abn=new A.e("Down syndrome (trisomy 21) is associated with which musculoskeletal concern requiring screening?",B.Wa,1,"Atlantoaxial instability (AAI): present in ~15% of Down syndrome patients due to ligamentous laxity. ADI (atlanto-dens interval) >5mm on lateral flexion/extension X-rays is abnormal. Screening required before participation in sports (Special Olympics). Symptomatic AAI requires surgical fusion. Other concerns: hypotonia, cardiac defects, hypothyroidism.")
B.PB=s(["Constraining the affected limb","Constraining the unaffected limb to force use of the affected limb","Bilateral arm training only","Surgical tendon transfer"],t.s)
B.a3j=new A.e("Constraint-induced movement therapy (CIMT) in pediatric hemiplegia involves:",B.PB,1,"CIMT: the unaffected hand is restrained (cast or mitt) for a period while intensive therapy targets the affected hand. This combats learned non-use and promotes cortical reorganization. Modified CIMT protocols (fewer hours, intermittent constraint) are more practical for children. Evidence supports improved hand function in hemiplegic CP.")
B.Sf=s(["Only motor skills","Cognitive, language, and motor development in infants 1-42 months","Academic achievement","Adaptive behavior only"],t.s)
B.a6K=new A.e("The Bayley Scales of Infant Development assess:",B.Sf,1,"Bayley-IV: comprehensive developmental assessment for infants/toddlers (1-42 months). Five domains: cognitive, language (receptive + expressive), motor (fine + gross), social-emotional, and adaptive behavior. It is the gold standard for identifying developmental delays and qualifying children for early intervention services.")
B.UT=s([B.a4O,B.acs,B.abn,B.a3j,B.a6K],t.b)
B.Jw=new A.a6("Pediatrics 4",B.UT)
B.Yd=s(["Inhibiting osteoblasts","Blocking RANKL, thereby inhibiting osteoclast formation and bone resorption","Increasing intestinal calcium absorption","Stimulating vitamin D production"],t.s)
B.a9v=new A.e("Denosumab (Prolia) treats osteoporosis by:",B.Yd,1,"Denosumab: monoclonal antibody against RANKL (receptor activator of nuclear factor kappa-B ligand). RANKL is essential for osteoclast differentiation and activation. By blocking RANKL, denosumab prevents osteoclast formation \u2192 reduced bone resorption. Given as subcutaneous injection every 6 months. Unlike bisphosphonates, it does not accumulate in bone and is safe in renal impairment.")
B.P4=s(["It inhibits bone resorption like bisphosphonates","It is an anabolic agent (recombinant PTH) that stimulates new bone formation","It is a calcium supplement","It replaces vitamin D"],t.s)
B.a5t=new A.e("Teriparatide (Forteo) is unique among osteoporosis treatments because:",B.P4,1,"Teriparatide: recombinant PTH(1-34) \u2014 the only anabolic (bone-building) agent. Intermittent PTH administration paradoxically stimulates osteoblasts more than osteoclasts, increasing bone formation. Used for severe osteoporosis, multiple fractures, or failure of antiresorptive therapy. Limited to 2 years. Must follow with an antiresorptive (bisphosphonate) to maintain gains.")
B.X4=s(["500 mg","800 mg","1200 mg","2000 mg"],t.s)
B.abm=new A.e("Recommended daily calcium intake for osteoporosis prevention in postmenopausal women is:",B.X4,2,"Recommended calcium: 1200 mg/day for women >50 and men >70. 1000 mg/day for younger adults. Diet first (dairy, leafy greens, fortified foods); supplement the deficit. Calcium carbonate (40% elemental Ca, take with food) or calcium citrate (21% elemental, better absorbed on empty stomach). Excessive supplementation (>2000 mg/day) increases kidney stone risk.")
B.Qp=s(["It directly strengthens collagen","It enhances intestinal calcium absorption and is necessary for bone mineralization","It inhibits PTH secretion only","It is a direct osteoblast stimulant"],t.s)
B.a9T=new A.e("Vitamin D is essential for bone health because:",B.Qp,1,"Vitamin D: promotes intestinal calcium absorption (only 10-15% absorbed without vitamin D vs. 30-40% with adequate levels). Also needed for proper bone mineralization. Target 25-OH vitamin D: \u226530 ng/mL. Recommended intake: 800-1000 IU/day (higher for deficiency). Vitamin D deficiency \u2192 osteomalacia (defective mineralization) and secondary hyperparathyroidism \u2192 increased bone resorption.")
B.VJ=s(["Falls are rare in elderly patients","Most osteoporotic fractures result from falls, so preventing falls prevents fractures","Medications alone are sufficient","Exercise increases fall risk"],t.s)
B.abH=new A.e("Fall prevention is critical in osteoporosis management because:",B.VJ,1,"Over 90% of hip fractures result from falls. Fall prevention strategies: balance/strength training (tai chi has strong evidence), home safety modifications (remove rugs, improve lighting, add grab bars), medication review (reduce sedatives, antihypertensives that cause orthostasis), vision correction, and assistive devices. Combined with pharmacotherapy, this is the most effective fracture prevention approach.")
B.Ob=s([B.a9v,B.a5t,B.abm,B.a9T,B.abH],t.b)
B.Jx=new A.a6("Osteoporosis 2",B.Ob)
B.VY=s(["The cervical spinal cord","The sacral cord segments (S2-S5) at the conus","The thoracic cord","The nerve roots in the cauda equina only"],t.s)
B.a5g=new A.e("Conus medullaris syndrome involves injury to which structure?",B.VY,1,"Conus medullaris syndrome: damage to the sacral cord (conus) at approximately L1-L2 vertebral level. It produces LMN signs (areflexic bladder/bowel, reduced anal tone) and is usually bilateral and symmetric. Distinguished from cauda equina syndrome, which is asymmetric and involves nerve roots.")
B.To=s(["Is always complete","Involves peripheral nerve roots and is typically asymmetric with radicular pain","Is a UMN lesion","Does not affect bladder function"],t.s)
B.abh=new A.e("Cauda equina syndrome differs from conus medullaris syndrome in that cauda equina:",B.To,1,"Cauda equina = peripheral nerve root injury (below the conus): asymmetric, radicular pain, LMN pattern, variable bladder/bowel dysfunction. Conus = cord injury: symmetric, less pain, UMN + LMN mixed pattern, early bladder/bowel dysfunction. Recovery potential is better with cauda equina (peripheral nerve regeneration possible).")
B.NN=s(["Motor function (corticospinal tracts) and pain/temperature (spinothalamic tracts)","Proprioception and vibration","All motor function","All sensory function"],t.s)
B.a65=new A.e("Posterior cord syndrome (rare) would spare:",B.NN,0,"Posterior cord syndrome: damage to the posterior columns only. Loss of proprioception, vibration, and deep pressure. Motor function (anterior corticospinal tracts) and pain/temperature (lateral spinothalamic tracts) are preserved. This is the rarest incomplete SCI syndrome. Causes include posterior spinal artery occlusion or B12 deficiency.")
B.ZO=s(["It is rare in SCI","Major depression affects approximately 30% of SCI patients and significantly impacts rehabilitation participation","It only occurs in the first month","It does not affect outcomes"],t.s)
B.a8a=new A.e("SCI patients should be screened for depression because:",B.ZO,1,"Depression affects approximately 30% of SCI patients (much higher than the general population). It significantly reduces rehabilitation participation, functional outcomes, and quality of life. Screening should be routine. SSRIs are first-line treatment. Peer support and psychological counseling are also beneficial.")
B.Wj=s(["5","10","15","20"],t.s)
B.a9d=new A.e("The International Standards for Neurological Classification of SCI (ISNCSCI) uses how many key muscles on each side?",B.Wj,1,"ISNCSCI tests 10 key muscles per side (5 upper extremity: C5-T1, and 5 lower extremity: L2-S1). Each is graded 0-5. Upper extremity: C5=elbow flexors, C6=wrist extensors, C7=elbow extensors, C8=finger flexors, T1=finger abductors. Lower: L2=hip flexors, L3=knee extensors, L4=ankle dorsiflexors, L5=great toe extensors, S1=ankle plantar flexors.")
B.Vn=s([B.a5g,B.abh,B.a65,B.a8a,B.a9d],t.b)
B.Jy=new A.a6("Spinal Cord Injury 9",B.Vn)
B.YQ=s(["A screening laboratory test","An extension of the physical examination","A replacement for clinical assessment","A confirmatory imaging study"],t.s)
B.a9D=new A.e("Electrodiagnostic medicine (EMG/NCS) is best described as which of the following?",B.YQ,1,"EDX is fundamentally an extension of the physical exam, not a lab test. The physician goes in with a clinical hypothesis based on history and exam, then uses EMG/NCS to confirm or refute that hypothesis. It answers: location, duration, severity, and prognosis.")
B.UU=s(["Dorsal root ganglion","Posterior horn of the spinal cord","Anterior (ventral) horn of the spinal cord","Lateral horn of the spinal cord"],t.s)
B.a4l=new A.e("Where is the cell body of the lower motor neuron (anterior horn cell) located?",B.UU,2,"The motor neuron cell body is located in the anterior (ventral) horn of the spinal cord gray matter. This is inside the CNS. In contrast, sensory neuron cell bodies are in the dorsal root ganglion, which is outside the CNS.")
B.MW=s(["Why larger nerve fibers conduct faster","Why motor units are recruited from smallest to largest","Why sensory neurons have faster conduction than motor neurons","Why myelination increases conduction velocity"],t.s)
B.a8r=new A.e("The Henneman size principle explains which of the following?",B.MW,1,"The Henneman size principle states that motor units are recruited from smallest to largest. This explains how you can both hold a baby gently (small motor units) and punch a bag forcefully (large motor units added) using the same muscles.")
B.Ya=s(["Incorrect electrode placement","Patient anxiety","Temperature effects","Electrical interference"],t.s)
B.a8T=new A.e("Which factor is described as the single most common cause of misinterpretation in the electrodiagnostic laboratory?",B.Ya,2,"Temperature is the single most common reason for misinterpretation in the EDX lab. Cool limb temperatures slow conduction velocities and can mimic neuropathy. This is an extremely high-yield board topic.")
B.XF=s(["Location of the lesion","Duration of the injury","Etiology of the disease","Prognosis for recovery"],t.s)
B.a6u=new A.e("EDX testing answers four key clinical questions. Which of the following is NOT one of them?",B.XF,2,"The four clinical questions answered by EDX are: (1) Location \u2014 root, plexus, nerve, or muscle; (2) Duration \u2014 acute, subacute, or chronic; (3) Severity \u2014 neuropraxia vs axonotmesis; (4) Prognosis \u2014 will it improve? Etiology/cause is determined by clinical correlation, not EDX alone.")
B.Ze=s([B.a9D,B.a4l,B.a8r,B.a8T,B.a6u],t.b)
B.Jz=new A.a6("Electrodiagnostic 1",B.Ze)
B.X6=s(["C5-C6","C7","C8-T1","C3-C5"],t.s)
B.a8E=new A.e("Klumpke palsy (brachial plexus birth injury) involves which nerve roots?",B.X6,2,"Klumpke palsy: C8-T1 injury affecting hand intrinsics and finger/wrist flexors. Presents with a claw hand deformity. Less common than Erb palsy. If T1 sympathetic fibers are involved, Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis) occurs. Prognosis is generally worse than Erb palsy.")
B.QO=s(["Children never recover from TBI","Younger children (<2 years) with severe TBI have worse outcomes than older children with similar injuries","Children always recover completely","There is no difference"],t.s)
B.a5K=new A.e("In pediatric TBI, children differ from adults in that:",B.QO,1,"Plasticity is a double-edged sword: while mild pediatric TBI may recover well, severe TBI in very young children (<2 years) disrupts fundamental brain development and has worse outcomes than similar injuries in older children/adults. Deficits may not be apparent until developmental demands increase (e.g., executive function deficits emerging in adolescence).")
B.Rv=s(["EMG alone","Genetic testing for dystrophin gene mutations (or muscle biopsy showing absent dystrophin)","Nerve conduction studies","CT scan of muscles"],t.s)
B.a6t=new A.e("Duchenne muscular dystrophy is confirmed by which test?",B.Rv,1,"DMD diagnosis: elevated CPK (screening) \u2192 genetic testing (first-line confirmatory) showing dystrophin gene deletion/duplication/point mutation on Xp21. Muscle biopsy (absent dystrophin on immunostaining) is used when genetic testing is inconclusive. EMG shows myopathic pattern but is not diagnostic.")
B.WI=s(["Cure the disease","Slow disease progression, prolong ambulation, and delay scoliosis","Build muscle mass","Treat infections"],t.s)
B.a95=new A.e("Corticosteroids (prednisone/deflazacort) in DMD are used to:",B.WI,1,"Steroids are the standard of care in DMD: prolong independent ambulation by 2-3 years, delay scoliosis, preserve pulmonary function, and delay cardiomyopathy. Side effects: weight gain, osteoporosis, cataracts, behavioral changes. Deflazacort may have fewer weight gain side effects. Initiate when motor plateau is reached (typically 4-6 years).")
B.ZV=s(["Muscular dystrophy","Spastic diplegic CP","Autism spectrum disorder","Down syndrome"],t.s)
B.a9Q=new A.e("A child who walks on toes bilaterally, has tight heel cords, and was born premature at 28 weeks most likely has:",B.ZV,1,"Bilateral toe-walking + tight heel cords + prematurity = classic spastic diplegic CP from periventricular leukomalacia. The spastic gastrocnemius/soleus causes equinus gait. Treatment: stretching, AFOs, botulinum toxin, and potentially surgical lengthening. Always consider the full clinical picture before diagnosing.")
B.SW=s([B.a8E,B.a5K,B.a6t,B.a95,B.a9Q],t.b)
B.JA=new A.a6("Pediatrics 8",B.SW)
B.ML=s(["Acetaminophen","Morphine 10 mg IV","Fentanyl 100 mcg","Hydrocodone 5 mg"],t.s)
B.a7Q=new A.e("Opioid equianalgesic dosing is based on comparison to which standard?",B.ML,1,"Morphine 10 mg IV (or 30 mg PO) is the standard reference for equianalgesic dosing. Key conversions: hydromorphone ~1.5 mg IV = morphine 10 mg IV, oxycodone 20 mg PO = morphine 30 mg PO, fentanyl 100 mcg IV \u2248 morphine 10 mg IV. Always reduce the calculated equianalgesic dose by 25-50% when rotating opioids (incomplete cross-tolerance).")
B.Si=s(["Nerve roots","Taut bands of skeletal muscle with palpable nodules that produce referred pain patterns","Joint capsules","Bone lesions"],t.s)
B.a8w=new A.e("Trigger point injections target:",B.Si,1,"Myofascial trigger points: hyperirritable spots within taut bands of skeletal muscle that produce local and referred pain when compressed. Treatment: direct needling (dry needling) or injection of local anesthetic \xb1 corticosteroid into the trigger point. Post-injection stretching is essential. Common in tension headache, low back pain, and neck pain.")
B.Z_=s(["Only pain","Pain disproportionate to the inciting event PLUS signs/symptoms in multiple categories (sensory, vasomotor, sudomotor, motor/trophic)","Positive X-ray findings","EMG abnormalities"],t.s)
B.a6p=new A.e("Complex regional pain syndrome (CRPS) Type I diagnostic criteria (Budapest) require:",B.Z_,1,"Budapest criteria for CRPS-I: (1) Continuing pain disproportionate to any inciting event, (2) At least one symptom in 3 of 4 categories (sensory, vasomotor, sudomotor/edema, motor/trophic), (3) At least one sign in 2 or more categories, (4) No other diagnosis better explains the findings. CRPS-II requires a definable nerve injury.")
B.Me=s(["Nausea","Sedation","Constipation","Respiratory depression"],t.s)
B.a6E=new A.e("The most common side effect of chronic opioid use that does NOT develop tolerance is:",B.Me,2,"Constipation: the ONE opioid side effect that does NOT develop tolerance. Patients on chronic opioids need a bowel regimen for the duration of therapy. Tolerance develops to nausea, sedation, and respiratory depression. Constipation management: stimulant laxative (senna) + stool softener (docusate), or methylnaltrexone for refractory opioid-induced constipation.")
B.Op=s(["Disc herniation","Facet joint-mediated pain","Sacroiliac joint pain","Myofascial pain"],t.s)
B.a9P=new A.e("A medial branch block is a diagnostic tool for:",B.Op,1,"Medial branch blocks: local anesthetic injected onto the medial branch nerves that innervate the facet (zygapophyseal) joints. Each facet is innervated by 2 medial branches (one from the level above and one at the same level). If \u226580% pain relief occurs on two separate occasions, the patient is a candidate for radiofrequency ablation of those medial branches.")
B.T7=s([B.a7Q,B.a8w,B.a6p,B.a6E,B.a9P],t.b)
B.JB=new A.a6("Pain 2",B.T7)
B.QR=s(["Rotator cuff insertion","Superior labrum from anterior to posterior","Inferior glenohumeral ligament","Coracoacromial ligament"],t.s)
B.a7U=new A.e("A SLAP lesion involves a tear of the:",B.QR,1,"SLAP = Superior Labrum Anterior to Posterior. It involves the superior labrum at the biceps anchor. Common in overhead athletes and after falls on an outstretched hand. O'Brien's test is used for diagnosis.")
B.Wu=s(["Supraspinatus tendon","Biceps tendon (long head)","Subscapularis tendon","Acromioclavicular joint"],t.s)
B.a5B=new A.e("The Speed's test evaluates pathology of which structure?",B.Wu,1,"Speed's test: resist forward flexion with the elbow extended and forearm supinated. Pain at the anterior shoulder/bicipital groove = biceps tendinopathy or SLAP lesion.")
B.Qn=s(["Neer's test","Cross-body adduction test","Empty can test","Apprehension test"],t.s)
B.a7q=new A.e("AC joint pathology is best assessed with which provocative test?",B.Qn,1,"The cross-body adduction (scarf) test: adduct the arm across the chest. Pain at the AC joint = positive for AC joint pathology (arthritis, separation, or distal clavicle osteolysis).")
B.VU=s(["Rotator cuff tear","AC joint arthritis","Anterior glenohumeral instability","Biceps tendinopathy"],t.s)
B.a3q=new A.e("The apprehension test is positive for:",B.VU,2,"The apprehension test: arm at 90\xb0 abduction and external rotation. The patient feels a sense of impending dislocation (apprehension, not just pain) = positive for anterior glenohumeral instability. The relocation test (posterior pressure on humeral head) should relieve the apprehension.")
B.PP=s(["Deltoid and teres minor","Supraspinatus and infraspinatus","Subscapularis and teres major","Biceps and brachialis"],t.s)
B.a6V=new A.e("Suprascapular neuropathy causes weakness of which two muscles?",B.PP,1,"The suprascapular nerve innervates the supraspinatus (abduction initiation) and infraspinatus (external rotation). Entrapment at the suprascapular notch affects both; entrapment at the spinoglenoid notch affects only infraspinatus.")
B.Ms=s([B.a7U,B.a5B,B.a7q,B.a3q,B.a6V],t.b)
B.JC=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Shoulder 3",B.Ms)
B.ZQ=s(["Burn tissue","Stimulate cellular processes (ATP production, collagen synthesis) without thermal effects","Replace surgical procedures","Diagnose cancer"],t.s)
B.a6B=new A.e("Low-level laser therapy (LLLT/photobiomodulation) is proposed to:",B.ZQ,1,"LLLT/photobiomodulation: low-power laser or LED light stimulates cellular mitochondria (cytochrome c oxidase), increasing ATP production and promoting tissue repair. Non-thermal mechanism. Used for pain, inflammation, wound healing, and musculoskeletal conditions. Wavelengths: 600-1000 nm (red and near-infrared). Evidence is growing but mixed for many conditions.")
B.Mg=s(["Hot and cold water immersion to create a vascular pumping effect","Different ultrasound frequencies","Deep and superficial heating","Electrical and manual stimulation"],t.s)
B.abF=new A.e("Contrast baths alternate between:",B.Mg,0,'Contrast baths: alternating immersion in hot water (38-40\xb0C) and cold water (10-18\xb0C), typically 3-4 minutes hot then 1 minute cold, repeated 4-6 cycles. The alternating vasodilation and vasoconstriction creates a "pumping effect" that may reduce edema. Used for distal extremity conditions (hands, feet) with subacute swelling or CRPS.')
B.Wc=s(["Spinal surgery","Total knee arthroplasty to maintain ROM and prevent adhesions","Hip fracture ORIF","Rotator cuff repair only"],t.s)
B.a41=new A.e("Continuous passive motion (CPM) machines are most commonly used after:",B.Wc,1,"CPM: mechanically moves the joint through a preset ROM continuously. Most studied after total knee arthroplasty. Benefits: maintains ROM, prevents adhesions, promotes articular cartilage nutrition, reduces pain and swelling. Current evidence is mixed \u2014 many surgeons have moved away from routine CPM use, preferring aggressive early physical therapy instead.")
B.Uv=s(["Uses a lower frequency","Uses two medium-frequency currents that intersect in deep tissue to create a low-frequency therapeutic current","Is applied to the skin surface only","Cannot be used for pain"],t.s)
B.a9O=new A.e("Electrical stimulation using interferential current (IFC) differs from TENS in that IFC:",B.Uv,1,"IFC: two medium-frequency currents (e.g., 4000 Hz and 4100 Hz) are applied through 4 electrodes. Where they intersect in tissue, they create a beat frequency (100 Hz in this example) at depth. The medium frequency penetrates tissue better than the low-frequency TENS signal. Advantage: deeper penetration, more comfortable, less skin resistance.")
B.VR=s(["Large body areas like the back","The hands and feet (arthritis, scleroderma, joint stiffness)","The lungs","Post-surgical wounds"],t.s)
B.a8O=new A.e("Paraffin wax baths are most commonly used for:",B.VR,1,"Paraffin wax (mixture of paraffin + mineral oil at 52-54\xb0C): superficial moist heat that conforms to irregular joint surfaces. Best for hands and feet. Methods: dip-wrap (most common \u2014 7-12 dips then wrap), dip-immerse, paint method. Benefits in RA/OA: reduces pain and stiffness, improves ROM before exercise. Contraindicated over open wounds, impaired sensation, and peripheral vascular disease.")
B.Rd=s([B.a6B,B.abF,B.a41,B.a9O,B.a8O],t.b)
B.JD=new A.a6("Physical Modalities 3",B.Rd)
B.W8=s(["Flexor carpi radialis","Extensor carpi radialis brevis","Pronator teres","Biceps brachii"],t.s)
B.a4J=new A.e("Lateral epicondylitis (tennis elbow) involves which muscle/tendon?",B.W8,1,'Lateral epicondylitis primarily involves the extensor carpi radialis brevis (ECRB) tendon at the lateral epicondyle. Despite being called "tennis elbow," it is more common in non-tennis activities involving repetitive wrist extension and gripping.')
B.Ye=s(["Wrist extensors","Wrist flexors and pronators","Supinators","Triceps"],t.s)
B.a48=new A.e("Medial epicondylitis (golfer's elbow) involves the origin of which muscle group?",B.Ye,1,"Medial epicondylitis affects the common flexor-pronator tendon origin at the medial epicondyle. The flexor carpi radialis and pronator teres are most commonly involved. It is important to assess for ulnar nerve irritation, as the nerve runs in the cubital tunnel just posterior to the medial epicondyle.")
B.Qy=s(["Radial tunnel","Cubital tunnel","Carpal tunnel","Guyon's canal"],t.s)
B.aaQ=new A.e("The ulnar nerve passes through which anatomic space at the elbow?",B.Qy,1,"The ulnar nerve passes through the cubital tunnel (between the medial epicondyle and olecranon). It is the second most common upper extremity nerve entrapment (after carpal tunnel syndrome). Prolonged elbow flexion, direct pressure, and subluxation are risk factors.")
B.Zz=s(["0-5 degrees valgus","5-15 degrees valgus","15-25 degrees valgus","25-35 degrees valgus"],t.s)
B.abl=new A.e("The carrying angle of the elbow is normally:",B.Zz,1,'The normal carrying angle is approximately 5-15 degrees of valgus (larger in women). An increased carrying angle (cubitus valgus) can put the ulnar nerve at risk. A decreased angle (cubitus varus, or "gunstock deformity") can result from a malunited supracondylar fracture.')
B.Qm=s(["Lateral collateral ligament","Annular ligament","Ulnar (medial) collateral ligament","Interosseous membrane"],t.s)
B.abT=new A.e("The primary stabilizer of the elbow against valgus stress is:",B.Qm,2,"The ulnar collateral ligament (UCL/MCL), specifically its anterior band, is the primary stabilizer against valgus stress. UCL injuries are common in overhead throwing athletes. Tommy John surgery involves UCL reconstruction.")
B.Yp=s([B.a4J,B.a48,B.aaQ,B.abl,B.abT],t.b)
B.JE=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Elbow 1",B.Yp)
B.QN=s(["Lunate","Triquetrum","Scaphoid","Hamate"],t.s)
B.a68=new A.e("The most commonly fractured carpal bone is:",B.QN,2,"The scaphoid is the most commonly fractured carpal bone, typically from a fall on an outstretched hand (FOOSH). The proximal pole has a retrograde blood supply, putting it at risk for AVN. Initial X-rays may be negative \u2014 suspect with anatomic snuffbox tenderness.")
B.Sm=s(["Scaphoid","Lunate","Capitate","Trapezium"],t.s)
B.a9t=new A.e("Kienbock's disease is avascular necrosis of which carpal bone?",B.Sm,1,"Kienbock's disease is AVN of the lunate, associated with ulnar negative variance (short ulna). The lunate has a tenuous blood supply. It presents with dorsal wrist pain, decreased grip strength, and limited wrist motion. Staging guides treatment (immobilization to salvage procedures).")
B.Yv=s(["Thumb pad","Index finger","Middle finger","Thenar eminence (base of thumb)"],t.s)
B.a3p=new A.e("Carpal tunnel syndrome spares which area of the hand?",B.Yv,3,"The palmar cutaneous branch exits the median nerve 5 cm proximal to the wrist and travels OVER (not through) the carpal tunnel. Therefore, thenar eminence sensation is preserved in CTS. Numbness at the thumb base suggests a more proximal median nerve lesion.")
B.ON=s(["Carpal tunnel syndrome","Scapholunate ligament instability","De Quervain's tenosynovitis","Ulnar collateral ligament injury"],t.s)
B.aaz=new A.e("The Watson (scaphoid shift) test evaluates for:",B.ON,1,"The Watson test: the examiner applies pressure on the scaphoid tubercle while moving the wrist from ulnar to radial deviation. A painful clunk as the scaphoid subluxes dorsally = positive for scapholunate instability. This can lead to DISI (dorsal intercalated segment instability).")
B.UC=s(["Typing","Gripping sports (golf, baseball, tennis)","Wrist hyperextension","Repetitive pronation/supination"],t.s)
B.a8V=new A.e("Hook of hamate fractures are associated with which activity?",B.UC,1,"Hook of hamate fractures occur from direct impact of a club, bat, or racquet handle against the hypothenar eminence. They are easily missed on standard X-rays \u2014 CT scan or carpal tunnel view is needed. The ulnar nerve and artery run adjacent and can be injured.")
B.Of=s([B.a68,B.a9t,B.a3p,B.aaz,B.a8V],t.b)
B.JF=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Wrist",B.Of)
B.Up=s(["Mean arterial pressure + intracranial pressure","Systolic BP - diastolic BP","Mean arterial pressure - intracranial pressure","Intracranial pressure / mean arterial pressure"],t.s)
B.a3x=new A.e("Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) is calculated as:",B.Up,2,"CPP = MAP - ICP. Target CPP in severe TBI is 60-70 mmHg. ICP >20 mmHg is treated aggressively. Methods to reduce ICP include head elevation (30\xb0), osmotic therapy (mannitol, hypertonic saline), sedation, CSF drainage, and decompressive craniectomy.")
B.Rg=s(["Brain volume decreases with age","The cranial vault is a fixed volume containing brain, blood, and CSF \u2014 an increase in one must be offset by a decrease in another","Intracranial pressure is always constant","CSF production stops after brain injury"],t.s)
B.a5e=new A.e("The Monro-Kellie doctrine states that:",B.Rg,1,"The Monro-Kellie doctrine: the rigid skull contains brain (~80%), blood (~10%), and CSF (~10%). An increase in any one component (edema, hemorrhage, hydrocephalus) must be compensated by a decrease in another, or ICP rises. Once compensatory mechanisms are exhausted, small volume changes cause rapid ICP spikes.")
B.Xi=s(["Physical restraints","Environmental modification, redirection, and low-dose medications if needed","Discharge from rehabilitation","Immediate neurosurgical consultation"],t.s)
B.a6M=new A.e("Agitation during TBI recovery (Rancho IV) is best managed with:",B.Xi,1,"Rancho IV agitation management: minimize stimulation (quiet room, limited visitors), consistent routine, redirect rather than restrain, ensure no underlying medical cause (pain, infection, medication effect). Medications if needed: low-dose beta-blockers, amantadine, or atypical antipsychotics.")
B.RB=s(["Reduce spasticity","Accelerate cognitive recovery and improve arousal","Prevent seizures","Treat depression"],t.s)
B.ab4=new A.e("Amantadine is used in TBI rehabilitation primarily to:",B.RB,1,"Amantadine (dopaminergic agent) has been shown to accelerate functional recovery in patients with disorders of consciousness after TBI. It improves arousal, attention, and processing speed. It should be avoided in patients with seizure history as it may lower seizure threshold.")
B.Mb=s(["Complete absence of brainstem function","Wakefulness without awareness \u2014 sleep-wake cycles present but no purposeful behavior","Brief periods of consciousness with prolonged unconsciousness","Normal arousal with severe amnesia"],t.s)
B.ace=new A.e("The vegetative state is defined as:",B.Mb,1,"Vegetative state: eyes open, sleep-wake cycles present (wakefulness) but NO evidence of awareness or purposeful behavior. Persistent vegetative state: >1 month after TBI. Permanent: >12 months (TBI) or >3 months (non-traumatic). Distinguished from coma (no wakefulness) and minimally conscious state (inconsistent but reproducible purposeful behavior).")
B.R9=s([B.a3x,B.a5e,B.a6M,B.ab4,B.ace],t.b)
B.JG=new A.a6("Traumatic Brain Injury 4",B.R9)
B.XD=s(["Exercise tolerance","Quality of life and dyspnea","FEV1 (airway obstruction)","Depression and anxiety"],t.s)
B.a6r=new A.e("Pulmonary rehabilitation has been shown to improve all EXCEPT:",B.XD,2,"Pulmonary rehab improves exercise capacity, dyspnea, quality of life, reduces hospitalizations, and improves anxiety/depression. However, it does NOT improve FEV1 or reverse airway obstruction. The benefits come from improved muscle conditioning, breathing techniques, and self-management education \u2014 not from reversing the underlying disease.")
B.YC=s(["Strengthen expiratory muscles","Encourage deep sustained inspiration to prevent atelectasis","Measure peak expiratory flow","Diagnose COPD"],t.s)
B.acA=new A.e("Incentive spirometry primarily helps:",B.YC,1,"Incentive spirometry: visual feedback device that encourages sustained maximal inspiration (slow deep breaths). This expands alveoli, prevents/treats atelectasis (collapsed lung segments), and improves mucociliary clearance. Used post-operatively and in patients with restricted breathing patterns. Goal: 10 repetitions every 1-2 hours while awake.")
B.TQ=s(["Breathe using accessory muscles","Maximize diaphragmatic excursion while minimizing chest wall and accessory muscle use","Hold their breath for extended periods","Breathe as rapidly as possible"],t.s)
B.acn=new A.e("Diaphragmatic breathing exercises train the patient to:",B.TQ,1,"Diaphragmatic breathing: patient places hand on abdomen, inhales through nose letting abdomen rise (diaphragm descending), then exhales slowly through pursed lips as abdomen falls. This retrains the breathing pattern from inefficient upper chest breathing to efficient diaphragmatic breathing. Reduces work of breathing and dyspnea.")
B.P2=s(["Strengthen the diaphragm","Mobilize and clear retained pulmonary secretions from specific lung segments","Improve vocal cord function","Treat pneumothorax"],t.s)
B.a6R=new A.e("Chest percussion and postural drainage are used to:",B.P2,1,"Chest physiotherapy (percussion + postural drainage): positions the patient so gravity assists drainage from specific lung segments while rhythmic percussion loosens adherent secretions. Used in cystic fibrosis, bronchiectasis, and patients with thick secretions. Each lung segment has a specific drainage position based on bronchial anatomy.")
B.Pj=s(["0 to 5","6 to 20","1 to 100","0 to 4"],t.s)
B.a9o=new A.e("The Borg RPE (Rating of Perceived Exertion) scale used in pulmonary rehab ranges from:",B.Pj,1,"Borg RPE scale: 6 (no exertion) to 20 (maximal exertion). Target for pulmonary rehab exercise: 12-14 (somewhat hard). The modified Borg dyspnea scale (0-10) is also commonly used specifically for breathlessness. RPE is especially useful when heart rate response is unreliable (beta-blockers, deconditioning, pacemaker).")
B.ZK=s([B.a6r,B.acA,B.acn,B.a6R,B.a9o],t.b)
B.JH=new A.a6("Pulmonary Rehab 2",B.ZK)
B.RM=s(["Accidental scalding","Electrical injury","Child abuse","Chemical exposure"],t.s)
B.aaA=new A.e("In children under 2 years old, what is the most common cause of burn injuries?",B.RM,2,"In children under 2 years old, the majority of burns are the result of abuse. This is a critical clinical pearl \u2014 burn patterns such as immersion lines, stocking-glove patterns, or cigarette burns should raise immediate suspicion.")
B.ZH=s(["Zone of coagulation","Zone of stasis","Zone of hyperemia","Zone of necrosis"],t.s)
B.a3A=new A.e("Which zone in Jackson's burn wound model represents the primary target for acute burn management and can potentially be salvaged with proper resuscitation?",B.ZH,1,'The zone of stasis is the critical "battleground" \u2014 tissue is ischemic but cells are still alive. With proper fluid resuscitation, oxygenation, and infection control, this zone can recover. If management fails, it converts to the zone of coagulation (necrosis).')
B.MU=s(["The underestimation effect","The iceberg effect","Jackson's zone theory","The biphasic response"],t.s)
B.ab1=new A.e("A patient sustains a high-voltage electrical injury. Despite only a small entry wound on the hand, extensive deep tissue damage is found. Which concept best explains this finding?",B.MU,1,'The "iceberg effect" describes electrical burns: surface damage appears minimal but internal devastation (muscle, bone, nerves) can be massive. High-voltage current takes the most direct path and generates heat proportional to tissue resistance, with bone having the highest resistance.')
B.Mn=s(["Curling's ulcer","Early-onset cataracts","Marjolin's ulcer","Hypertrophic scarring"],t.s)
B.abu=new A.e("Which of the following is a recognized late complication specific to electrical burn injury?",B.Mn,1,'The "electrical buzzwords" for board exams include: fractures (from violent tetanic muscle contractions), peripheral neuropathy, early-onset cataracts (from lens protein disruption), hearing loss, and heterotopic ossification (HO).')
B.Wl=s(["Thromboxane","Serotonin","Histamine","Epinephrine"],t.s)
B.abW=new A.e("In the acute phase of a major burn injury, which inflammatory mediator is primarily responsible for the massive capillary leak and fluid shift into the extravascular space?",B.Wl,2,"After the initial vasoconstrictive phase (driven by epinephrine, serotonin, thromboxane), histamine causes massive vasodilation and extreme capillary permeability. This leads to protein and fluid shifting out of vessels, causing edema while intravascular volume crashes \u2014 resulting in hypovolemic shock.")
B.R6=s([B.aaA,B.a3A,B.ab1,B.abu,B.abW],t.b)
B.JI=new A.a6("Burns 1",B.R6)
B.OV=s(["Progressive neurological disorder","Non-progressive disorder of movement and posture due to a lesion in the developing brain","Genetic muscular disorder","Peripheral nerve disease"],t.s)
B.a9Y=new A.e("Cerebral palsy is defined as a:",B.OV,1,"CP is a non-progressive disorder of movement and posture resulting from injury to the developing brain (prenatal, perinatal, or early postnatal). While the brain lesion is static, the musculoskeletal manifestations can change over time as the child grows.")
B.Yl=s(["3","4","5","7"],t.s)
B.a4e=new A.e("The Gross Motor Function Classification System (GMFCS) has how many levels?",B.Yl,2,"GMFCS has 5 levels: Level I = walks without limitations. Level II = walks with limitations. Level III = walks with assistive device. Level IV = self-mobility with limitations (power wheelchair). Level V = transported in manual wheelchair. The level is relatively stable over time and predicts adult mobility.")
B.O_=s(["Dyskinetic","Ataxic","Spastic","Hypotonic"],t.s)
B.a5p=new A.e("The most common type of cerebral palsy is:",B.O_,2,"Spastic CP accounts for ~80% of cases. It can be classified by distribution: diplegia (legs > arms), hemiplegia (one side), quadriplegia (all four limbs). Dyskinetic CP (~10-15%) includes dystonic and choreoathetoid types. Ataxic CP (~5%) involves cerebellar dysfunction.")
B.Q8=s(["Full-term infants","Premature infants","Post-term infants","Adolescents"],t.s)
B.aa2=new A.e("Periventricular leukomalacia (PVL) is the most common cause of CP in:",B.Q8,1,"PVL: ischemic injury to the periventricular white matter in premature infants. The periventricular region is a watershed zone vulnerable to hypoperfusion. Because motor fibers to the legs run closest to the ventricles, PVL classically causes spastic diplegia (legs > arms).")
B.YO=s(["Upper extremity involvement greater than lower","Lower extremity involvement greater than upper, scissoring gait","Unilateral involvement only","Primarily truncal hypotonia"],t.s)
B.a7a=new A.e("A child with spastic diplegic CP typically has which motor pattern?",B.YO,1,"Spastic diplegia: bilateral lower extremity involvement greater than upper. Classic pattern: scissoring gait (hip adductor spasticity), toe-walking (ankle plantarflexion spasticity), crouch gait (hip/knee flexion). Associated with prematurity and PVL. Upper extremities may have subtle fine motor deficits.")
B.XQ=s([B.a9Y,B.a4e,B.a5p,B.aa2,B.a7a],t.b)
B.JJ=new A.a6("Pediatrics 1",B.XQ)
B.QE=s(["L3","L4","L5","S1"],t.s)
B.a61=new A.e("A posterolateral disc herniation at L4-L5 will most commonly compress which nerve root?",B.QE,2,"In the lumbar spine, posterolateral disc herniations compress the traversing (lower) nerve root. An L4-L5 disc herniates into the L5 nerve root. A far lateral (foraminal) herniation would compress the exiting (upper) root \u2014 L4 in this case.")
B.RV=s(["0-20\xb0","30-70\xb0","80-90\xb0","Any angle"],t.s)
B.a4Q=new A.e("The straight leg raise (SLR) test is considered positive when it reproduces radicular symptoms at what angle range?",B.RV,1,"A positive SLR reproduces radicular pain (not just hamstring tightness) between 30-70\xb0. Below 30\xb0, the nerve roots are not yet under tension. Above 70\xb0, pelvic rotation contributes more than nerve root stretch. A positive crossed SLR (contralateral leg raise) is more specific for disc herniation.")
B.Mu=s(["Knee extension and hip flexion","Ankle dorsiflexion and great toe extension (EHL)","Ankle plantarflexion and knee flexion","Hip abduction and knee extension"],t.s)
B.a7n=new A.e("An L5 radiculopathy presents with weakness of:",B.Mu,1,"L5 radiculopathy: weakness of ankle dorsiflexion (tibialis anterior), great toe extension (EHL), hip abduction, and foot inversion. Sensory loss is on the dorsum of the foot. The L5 reflex is difficult to test (medial hamstring, often unreliable).")
B.O5=s(["Unilateral radiculopathy","Bilateral leg pain, saddle anesthesia, and bowel/bladder dysfunction","Isolated back pain without neurological deficits","Cervical myelopathy"],t.s)
B.aa6=new A.e("Cauda equina syndrome is a surgical emergency presenting with:",B.O5,1,"Cauda equina syndrome: bilateral leg pain/weakness, saddle anesthesia (perineal numbness), and bowel/bladder dysfunction (typically urinary retention). It requires emergent MRI and surgical decompression within 24-48 hours to prevent permanent deficits.")
B.Q5=s(["Patellar (knee jerk)","Achilles (ankle jerk)","Biceps","Brachioradialis"],t.s)
B.aa1=new A.e("The S1 nerve root is tested by which deep tendon reflex?",B.Q5,1,"S1 = Achilles reflex, ankle plantarflexion, eversion. L4 = patellar reflex, knee extension. S1 radiculopathy is the most common lumbar radiculopathy (L5-S1 disc is the most commonly herniated lumbar disc).")
B.Pm=s([B.a61,B.a4Q,B.a7n,B.aa6,B.aa1],t.b)
B.JK=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Spine 1",B.Pm)
B.Q0=s(["Strengthening neck muscles","Distracting vertebral segments, widening foramina, and reducing intradiscal pressure","Fusing the vertebrae","Increasing disc herniation"],t.s)
B.a5o=new A.e("Traction for cervical radiculopathy works by:",B.Q0,1,"Cervical traction: distraction opens the intervertebral foramina (reducing nerve root compression), reduces intradiscal pressure, stretches paraspinal muscles, and may help retract disc material. Parameters: intermittent, 20-30 lbs, 15-20 minutes, neck in slight flexion (opens posterior foramina). Contraindicated: RA with C-spine instability, myelopathy, vertebral artery insufficiency.")
B.QP=s(["Spinal stenosis","Chronic plantar fasciitis and lateral epicondylitis refractory to conservative treatment","Acute muscle strains","Osteoporosis"],t.s)
B.abI=new A.e("Extracorporeal shock wave therapy (ESWT) is FDA-approved for:",B.QP,1,"ESWT: focused acoustic pressure waves create microtrauma at the tissue interface, stimulating neovascularization and healing. FDA-approved for chronic plantar fasciitis and chronic lateral epicondylitis failing 6+ months of conservative treatment. Also studied for calcific tendinitis, non-union fractures, and Achilles tendinopathy. Mechanism: stimulates biologic healing response.")
B.S7=s(["Fractures","Myofascial trigger points \u2014 spray desensitizes the area, allowing deeper stretching","Ligament tears","Bone spurs"],t.s)
B.a9_=new A.e("The cold spray and stretch technique is used to treat:",B.S7,1,'Spray and stretch: vapocoolant spray (ethyl chloride or fluoromethane) applied in parallel sweeps over the trigger point and referred pain zone, creating superficial anesthesia. The muscle is simultaneously stretched to full length. The cold sensation activates large sensory fibers that "gate" pain, allowing the muscle to release the trigger point. Follow with moist heat.')
B.T6=s(["Muscle soreness","Active bleeding, unstable blood pressure, or incontinence (for full-body immersion)","Mild joint stiffness","Subacute ankle sprain"],t.s)
B.a53=new A.e("Whirlpool therapy is contraindicated in patients with:",B.T6,1,"Whirlpool contraindications: active hemorrhage/bleeding, unstable cardiovascular conditions, incontinence (infection risk in shared equipment), impaired sensation, severe peripheral vascular disease, and acute inflammation (warm water increases edema). Cold whirlpool may be appropriate for acute injuries. Water temperature: warm = 36-38\xb0C, neutral = 33-35\xb0C, cold = 10-18\xb0C.")
B.ZC=s(["Medications to control pain","Electronic monitoring to provide real-time information about physiological processes for self-regulation","Surgical implants","Manual therapy only"],t.s)
B.a5c=new A.e("Biofeedback uses:",B.ZC,1,"Biofeedback: electronic devices measure physiological signals (EMG, skin temperature, heart rate, EEG) and display them in real-time, allowing patients to learn voluntary control. Uses in rehab: muscle re-education (EMG biofeedback for quad activation post-surgery), pelvic floor training (incontinence), pain management, and relaxation training. Evidence-based for many applications.")
B.S8=s([B.a5o,B.abI,B.a9_,B.a53,B.a5c],t.b)
B.JL=new A.a6("Physical Modalities 4",B.S8)
B.QT=s(["No foot structure","The calcaneus and talus (hindfoot)","The metatarsals","Only the great toe"],t.s)
B.a5b=new A.e("Chopart (midtarsal) amputation preserves:",B.QT,1,"Chopart amputation: disarticulation through the talonavicular and calcaneocuboid joints, preserving the hindfoot (talus and calcaneus). Risk: equinus/varus deformity from unopposed tibialis posterior and Achilles. Requires Achilles tendon lengthening and custom shoe filler/AFO. Can be end-bearing.")
B.WJ=s(["Normal ankle ROM is preserved","The heel pad is preserved for end-bearing, allowing limited ambulation without a prosthesis","It is purely cosmetic","No prosthetic is ever needed"],t.s)
B.a6G=new A.e("A Syme amputation (ankle disarticulation) has which advantage?",B.WJ,1,"Syme amputation: ankle disarticulation with preserved heel pad secured over the distal tibia. Advantages: end-bearing capability (can ambulate short distances without prosthesis), durable weight-bearing surface, longer lever arm than transtibial. Disadvantage: bulbous distal end is cosmetically challenging for prosthetic design.")
B.VK=s(["Transfemoral \u2192 Transtibial \u2192 Syme","Syme \u2192 Transtibial \u2192 Transfemoral","Transtibial \u2192 Syme \u2192 Transfemoral","All levels have equal energy cost"],t.s)
B.abg=new A.e("The energy expenditure hierarchy for amputation level from LOWEST to HIGHEST is:",B.VK,1,"Energy cost increases with higher amputation level: Syme (lowest increase) \u2192 Transtibial (10-25%) \u2192 Knee disarticulation \u2192 Transfemoral (40-65%) \u2192 Hip disarticulation (highest). Preserving every joint, especially the knee, dramatically reduces energy expenditure. This is why limb salvage and distal amputation are preferred.")
B.MA=s(["Loads weight on the ischial tuberosity on a shelf","Contains the ischium WITHIN the socket for better medial-lateral control","Is only for pediatric patients","Does not provide suction"],t.s)
B.a5k=new A.e("An ischial containment socket (narrow ML design) for transfemoral prostheses:",B.MA,1,"Ischial containment (IC/IRC) socket: the ischial tuberosity and ramus are contained WITHIN the socket (vs. older quadrilateral socket where ischium sits ON a posterior shelf). Benefits: better mediolateral control, improved femoral alignment, better muscle function, more comfortable. It is the current standard for transfemoral prosthetics.")
B.Sh=s(["Edema control (elastic wrapping, shrinker sock)","Desensitization of the residual limb","Contracture prevention (ROM exercises, positioning)","Immediate return to unrestricted activity"],t.s)
B.a7C=new A.e("Preprosthetic management after amputation includes all EXCEPT:",B.Sh,3,"Preprosthetic management: edema control (figure-8 wrapping, shrinker socks), desensitization (tapping, massage), contracture prevention (ROM, positioning \u2014 avoid pillow under knee), cardiovascular conditioning, upper body strengthening, and emotional support. Activity is progressed gradually with prosthetic fitting when the limb is mature and edema-free.")
B.Yz=s([B.a5b,B.a6G,B.abg,B.a5k,B.a7C],t.b)
B.JM=new A.a6("Prosthetics & Orthotics 8",B.Yz)
B.TF=s(["Elderly women with osteoporosis","Adolescent athletes","Children ages 4-10 (peak 5-7)","Young adults on corticosteroids"],t.s)
B.a5z=new A.e("Legg-Calve-Perthes disease is avascular necrosis of the femoral head in which population?",B.TF,2,"Legg-Calve-Perthes is idiopathic AVN of the femoral head in children ages 4-10, with peak at 5-7 years. It is 5x more common in boys. The child presents with a painless limp and limited hip abduction/internal rotation.")
B.P0=s(["Thin, athletic adolescent girls","Overweight adolescent boys (ages 10-16)","Toddlers learning to walk","Elderly patients after falls"],t.s)
B.a8f=new A.e("Slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE) most commonly presents in which demographic?",B.P0,1,'SCFE occurs in overweight adolescents (10-16 years), more common in boys. The femoral head "slips" posteriorly and inferiorly relative to the femoral neck. Classic presentation: knee or groin pain with a limp. Obligate external rotation with flexion is pathognomonic.')
B.UH=s(["Greater trochanter","Lateral knee at the femoral epicondyle","Medial knee","Anterior hip"],t.s)
B.a8U=new A.e("Iliotibial band syndrome causes pain at which location?",B.UH,1,"IT band syndrome causes lateral knee pain at the femoral epicondyle, where the IT band snaps over the bony prominence during flexion-extension. It is common in runners and cyclists. Ober's test assesses IT band tightness.")
B.Yt=s(["Hamstring tightness","Hip flexion contracture","Sacroiliac joint dysfunction","Quadriceps weakness"],t.s)
B.acg=new A.e("The Thomas test assesses for:",B.Yt,1,"The Thomas test: supine, flex the contralateral hip to flatten the lumbar lordosis. If the tested leg rises off the table, a hip flexion contracture is present (tight iliopsoas). This is common in wheelchair users and patients with prolonged bed rest.")
B.RD=s(["The acetabular labrum","The greater trochanter (external type)","The iliopsoas tendon","The femoral head"],t.s)
B.a9L=new A.e("Snapping hip syndrome (coxa saltans) from the iliotibial band involves snapping over which structure?",B.RD,1,"External snapping hip: the IT band or gluteus maximus snaps over the greater trochanter. Internal snapping hip: the iliopsoas tendon snaps over the iliopectineal eminence or femoral head. Intra-articular: labral tears or loose bodies.")
B.P1=s([B.a5z,B.a8f,B.a8U,B.acg,B.a9L],t.b)
B.JN=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Hip 3",B.P1)
B.MD=s(["Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive","X-linked recessive","Mitochondrial"],t.s)
B.a38=new A.e("Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD) is inherited in which pattern?",B.MD,2,"DMD is X-linked recessive (dystrophin gene on Xp21). Affects almost exclusively males. 1/3 are new mutations. Onset by age 3-5 with progressive proximal weakness, wheelchair by 12, death by 20s-30s from respiratory/cardiac failure. CPK is massively elevated (10-100x normal).")
B.Xn=s(["Calf pseudohypertrophy","The child walking hands up thighs to rise from the floor due to proximal weakness","Winging of the scapula","Toe-walking"],t.s)
B.a4g=new A.e("Gower sign in DMD demonstrates:",B.Xn,1,'Gower sign: the child uses arms to "climb up" their own legs to stand from the floor, compensating for severe hip and knee extensor weakness. It is characteristic (though not pathognomonic) of DMD and reflects proximal muscle weakness that progresses proximally to distally.')
B.SC=s(["After age 5 with mild weakness","Before 6 months with severe hypotonia and inability to sit","At birth with contractures","In adolescence with scoliosis"],t.s)
B.aa3=new A.e("Spinal muscular atrophy (SMA) Type I (Werdnig-Hoffmann) presents:",B.SC,1,"SMA Type I (Werdnig-Hoffmann): onset before 6 months, severe generalized hypotonia (floppy baby), absent reflexes, tongue fasciculations, never achieve sitting. Autosomal recessive, SMN1 gene deletion. Death typically by age 2 from respiratory failure without treatment. Nusinersen (Spinraza) has changed prognosis.")
B.OF=s(["Klumpke palsy","Erb-Duchenne palsy","Total plexus palsy","Horner syndrome"],t.s)
B.aaP=new A.e("Brachial plexus birth palsy involving C5-C6 is called:",B.OF,1,'Erb-Duchenne palsy (C5-C6): the "waiter\'s tip" position \u2014 shoulder adducted and internally rotated, elbow extended, forearm pronated, wrist flexed. Most common brachial plexus birth injury (~75%). Risk factors: large birth weight, shoulder dystocia. Most recover spontaneously by 3-6 months.')
B.Xo=s(["Cervical","Thoracic","Lumbar/lumbosacral","Sacral only"],t.s)
B.a49=new A.e("Myelomeningocele most commonly occurs at which spinal level?",B.Xo,2,"Myelomeningocele (open spina bifida) most commonly occurs at the lumbar/lumbosacral level. The functional level determines ambulatory potential: thoracic = wheelchair, high lumbar (L1-L2) = household ambulation with RGO/KAFO, low lumbar (L3-L4) = community ambulation with AFOs, sacral = near-normal ambulation.")
B.Tr=s([B.a38,B.a4g,B.aa3,B.aaP,B.a49],t.b)
B.JO=new A.a6("Pediatrics 2",B.Tr)
B.SP=s(["Maximum oxygen consumption of the heart muscle","Maximum total body oxygen consumption during exercise","Resting oxygen consumption","Oxygen saturation at peak exercise"],t.s)
B.a44=new A.e("VO2 max represents which of the following?",B.SP,1,"VO2 max is the maximum total body oxygen consumption. It represents the body's aerobic capacity and is determined primarily by cardiac output and peripheral oxygen extraction. It is distinct from MVO2, which is myocardial oxygen consumption.")
B.Q9=s(["1.5 mL/kg/min","3.5 mL/kg/min","5.0 mL/kg/min","7.0 mL/kg/min"],t.s)
B.a6b=new A.e("One metabolic equivalent (MET) is defined as oxygen consumption of:",B.Q9,1,"1 MET = 3.5 mL O2/kg/min, which represents resting metabolic rate. Activity intensity is expressed in METs: light activity = 2-3 METs, moderate = 3-6 METs, vigorous = >6 METs. This is a fundamental board concept.")
B.Ty=s(["Heart rate \xd7 Stroke volume","Systolic BP \xd7 Heart rate","Diastolic BP \xd7 Heart rate","Cardiac output \xd7 Total peripheral resistance"],t.s)
B.aaR=new A.e("The Rate Pressure Product (RPP) is calculated as:",B.Ty,1,"RPP = Systolic BP \xd7 Heart rate. It is the best non-invasive estimate of myocardial oxygen demand (MVO2). An RPP >25,000 generally indicates significant myocardial workload and is used to set exercise limits.")
B.Mt=s(["Systolic BP reaching 200 mmHg","Drop in systolic BP >10 mmHg despite increasing workload","Rating of perceived exertion reaching 15","Heart rate reaching 85% of predicted maximum"],t.s)
B.aco=new A.e("During a graded exercise test, which of the following is an absolute indication to stop the test?",B.Mt,1,"A drop in systolic BP >10 mmHg from baseline despite increasing workload is an absolute indication to stop exercise testing, as it may indicate pump failure, severe ischemia, or outflow obstruction.")
B.XL=s(["A percentage of maximum heart rate","Heart rate reserve (HRmax - HRrest) plus resting heart rate","Maximum heart rate minus age","Resting heart rate plus 20 bpm"],t.s)
B.a50=new A.e("Using the Karvonen formula, target heart rate during exercise is calculated using:",B.XL,1,"The Karvonen formula: Target HR = [(HRmax - HRrest) \xd7 %intensity] + HRrest. It uses heart rate reserve, which accounts for resting heart rate and is more accurate than simply using a percentage of max HR.")
B.Rr=s([B.a44,B.a6b,B.aaR,B.aco,B.a50],t.b)
B.JP=new A.a6("Cardiac Rehab 2",B.Rr)
B.Ui=s(["2 mL \xd7 kg \xd7 %TBSA burned","4 mL \xd7 kg \xd7 %TBSA burned","6 mL \xd7 kg \xd7 %TBSA burned","8 mL \xd7 kg \xd7 %TBSA burned"],t.s)
B.a58=new A.e("The Parkland formula for fluid resuscitation in burn patients calculates the 24-hour crystalloid requirement as:",B.Ui,1,"The Parkland formula is 4 mL \xd7 body weight (kg) \xd7 %TBSA burned using lactated Ringer's solution. Half is given in the first 8 hours, the remaining half over the next 16 hours. This is one of the most commonly tested burn formulas.")
B.WT=s(["9%","14%","18%","36%"],t.s)
B.a8N=new A.e("Using the Rule of Nines in an adult, what percentage of total body surface area (TBSA) does the entire lower extremity represent?",B.WT,2,"In the adult Rule of Nines: each lower extremity = 18% (front 9% + back 9%). Head = 9%, each upper extremity = 9%, anterior trunk = 18%, posterior trunk = 18%, perineum = 1%.")
B.Pg=s(["Dry, leathery, painless surface","Red, painful surface with blisters","Red, dry, painful surface without blisters","Waxy white, painless surface"],t.s)
B.abc=new A.e("A superficial partial-thickness (second-degree) burn is characterized by which of the following?",B.Pg,1,"Superficial partial-thickness burns extend into the papillary dermis and present as red, painful, blistered wounds. First degree burns (superficial) are red and dry without blisters. Deep partial-thickness may appear waxy. Full thickness burns are dry, leathery, and painless.")
B.Ue=s(["0.25 mL/kg/hr","0.5 mL/kg/hr","1.0 mL/kg/hr","2.0 mL/kg/hr"],t.s)
B.acb=new A.e("What is the target urine output used to guide adequacy of fluid resuscitation in adult burn patients?",B.Ue,1,"The target urine output for adult burn resuscitation is 0.5 mL/kg/hr (30-50 mL/hr in adults). For children, the target is higher at 1.0 mL/kg/hr. Urine output is the most reliable indicator of adequate resuscitation.")
B.Us=s(["Thermal burns","Chemical burns","Radiation burns","Electrical burns"],t.s)
B.a42=new A.e("Which type of burn injury is most commonly associated with the need to assess for rhabdomyolysis and monitor creatine kinase levels?",B.Us,3,"Electrical burns cause massive deep muscle destruction due to internal heat generation along current pathways. This muscle breakdown (rhabdomyolysis) releases myoglobin and creatine kinase, risking acute renal failure. CK monitoring is essential.")
B.Nu=s([B.a58,B.a8N,B.abc,B.acb,B.a42],t.b)
B.JQ=new A.a6("Burns 2",B.Nu)
B.TL=s(["1 muscle innervated by the suspected root","2 muscles innervated by different peripheral nerves sharing the same root","3 muscles in the same myotome","All muscles innervated by the root"],t.s)
B.a4o=new A.e("To confirm a radiculopathy on EMG, abnormalities should be found in at least:",B.TL,1,'The "two-muscle, two-nerve, one-root rule": abnormalities must be found in at least 2 muscles innervated by different peripheral nerves but sharing the same nerve root. This pattern excludes a single peripheral nerve lesion.')
B.Ov=s(["Day 1-2","Day 4-5","Week 1 (day 7)","Week 3-4"],t.s)
B.a6J=new A.e("In radiculopathy, fibrillation potentials first appear in the paraspinal muscles at approximately:",B.Ov,2,"Radiculopathy chronology: Day 0 = decreased recruitment only. Day 4-5 = CMAP amplitude drops. Week 1 = fibrillations in paraspinals first (shortest axons from root). Weeks 2-3 = fibrillations appear in limb muscles. Week 5-6 = early reinnervation potentials.")
B.Mf=s(["Absent","Low amplitude","Normal (preserved)","Prolonged in latency"],t.s)
B.aby=new A.e("In radiculopathy, SNAPs are characteristically:",B.Mf,2,"SNAPs are NORMAL in radiculopathy because the lesion is pre-ganglionic (proximal to the DRG). The sensory neuron cell body and its peripheral axon remain intact. This is the key finding that distinguishes radiculopathy from plexopathy or peripheral neuropathy.")
B.ZX=s(["Peripheral nerve","Brachial plexus","Nerve root","Spinal cord"],t.s)
B.a56=new A.e("Abnormal paraspinal muscles on EMG help localize the lesion to the:",B.ZX,2,"Paraspinal muscles are innervated by the posterior rami, which branch off the nerve root before the plexus. Abnormal paraspinals = root-level lesion. Normal paraspinals with limb abnormalities = plexus or peripheral nerve lesion.")
B.Te=s(["Wrist extensors and triceps","Deltoid and biceps (shoulder abduction and elbow flexion)","Hand intrinsics and finger flexors","Forearm pronators and wrist flexors"],t.s)
B.a9Z=new A.e("The C5 nerve root innervates which key muscles?",B.Te,1,"C5 myotome: deltoid (shoulder abduction) and biceps (elbow flexion). C6 = wrist extension, brachioradialis. C7 = triceps, wrist flexion. C8 = finger flexors. T1 = hand intrinsics. Knowing myotomes is essential for localizing radiculopathies.")
B.X9=s([B.a4o,B.a6J,B.aby,B.a56,B.a9Z],t.b)
B.JR=new A.a6("Electrodiagnostic 7",B.X9)
B.M8=s(["L4 vs L5 radiculopathy","Common peroneal neuropathy at the fibular head vs sciatic neuropathy","Tibial vs peroneal nerve injury","Femoral vs obturator neuropathy"],t.s)
B.abf=new A.e('The "short head of biceps femoris" rule helps distinguish between:',B.M8,1,"The short head of biceps femoris is the only peroneal division muscle above the fibular head. If normal on EMG = lesion at the knee (peroneal neuropathy). If abnormal = lesion above the knee (sciatic neuropathy). This is a critical EMG landmark.")
B.T2=s(["Absent","Decreased","Preserved (normal)","Hyperesthetic"],t.s)
B.a9y=new A.e("In tarsal tunnel syndrome, heel sensation is typically:",B.T2,2,"Heel sensation is preserved in tarsal tunnel syndrome because the calcaneal branch exits proximal to the tarsal tunnel. If the heel is numb, consider S1 radiculopathy or a more proximal tibial nerve lesion instead.")
B.Ua=s(["Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve","Saphenous nerve"],t.s)
B.aaG=new A.e("Meralgia paresthetica involves which nerve?",B.Ua,2,"Meralgia paresthetica is entrapment of the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (LFCN) under the inguinal ligament. It is purely sensory \u2014 causing numbness/burning on the anterolateral thigh. There is NEVER motor weakness. Common in obesity, pregnancy, tight waistbands.")
B.OE=s(["Ankle dorsiflexion","Hip abduction","Hip adduction (obturator nerve)","Knee extension only"],t.s)
B.a7E=new A.e("To distinguish femoral neuropathy from an L3/L4 radiculopathy, you should test:",B.OE,2,"Hip adduction is innervated by the obturator nerve (L2-L4). Weak quadriceps + weak adductors = root-level lesion (L3/L4). Weak quadriceps + strong adductors = femoral neuropathy (obturator nerve spared). The adductors are the differentiator.")
B.Sd=s(["The peroneal division carries more fibers","The peroneal division is tethered at two points and has fewer fascicles with less connective tissue","The tibial division is superficial and more exposed","The peroneal division lacks a blood supply"],t.s)
B.a8R=new A.e("The sciatic nerve is more prone to peroneal division injury because:",B.Sd,1,"The peroneal division is tethered at the sciatic notch AND the fibular head (two fixed points). It has fewer fascicles and less surrounding connective tissue than the tibial division. This makes it more vulnerable to stretch and compression injuries.")
B.Ym=s([B.abf,B.a9y,B.aaG,B.a7E,B.a8R],t.b)
B.JS=new A.a6("Electrodiagnostic 10",B.Ym)
B.Rt=s(["85% vs 70%","90% vs 75%","95% vs 64%","98% vs 80%"],t.s)
B.a57=new A.e("What is the 3-year survival rate for post-MI patients who participate in cardiac rehabilitation compared to non-participants?",B.Rt,2,"The 3-year survival rate for cardiac rehab participants is 95%, compared to only 64% for non-participants \u2014 a 31 percentage point difference. This is one of the most effective interventions in all of medicine.")
B.MB=s(["120/80 mmHg","130/80 mmHg","140/90 mmHg","150/90 mmHg"],t.s)
B.a4m=new A.e("According to current guidelines, at what blood pressure threshold should treatment be initiated for cardiac rehab patients?",B.MB,1,"The 2017 guidelines shifted the treatment threshold from 140/90 to the more aggressive target of 130/80 mmHg. This is a commonly tested board question as the guidelines have changed.")
B.Sx=s(["5-10%","11-20%","30-40%","50-60%"],t.s)
B.a5S=new A.e("What percentage of eligible candidates actually participate in cardiac rehabilitation programs?",B.Sx,1,"Despite undeniable survival benefits, only 11-20% of eligible candidates participate in cardiac rehab. This participation gap disproportionately affects older people, females, and non-white populations.")
B.Tw=s(["Male sex","Age (men >45, women >55)","Diabetes mellitus","Family history of premature CAD"],t.s)
B.a7b=new A.e("Which of the following is NOT a non-modifiable risk factor for coronary artery disease?",B.Tw,2,"Diabetes is a modifiable risk factor. Non-modifiable risk factors include: age (men >45, women >55), male sex, family history (male first-degree relative with CAD <55, female <65), and personal history of vascular disease.")
B.Tf=s(["Surgical recovery","Vocational status","Pharmaceutical management","Diagnostic testing"],t.s)
B.aa4=new A.e("In the formal definition of cardiac rehabilitation, which component is often overlooked but specifically included as a goal?",B.Tf,1,"The formal definition includes restoring optimal physiological, psychological, social, vocational, and emotional status. The vocational component is a key board concept \u2014 rehab aims to return patients to a productive role in society, not just survival.")
B.XW=s([B.a57,B.a4m,B.a5S,B.a7b,B.aa4],t.b)
B.JT=new A.a6("Cardiac Rehab 1",B.XW)
B.NO=s(["Etanercept","Adalimumab","Infliximab","Methotrexate"],t.s)
B.abX=new A.e("Biologic DMARDs (TNF inhibitors) for RA include all EXCEPT:",B.NO,3,"Methotrexate is a conventional synthetic DMARD (csDMARD), not a biologic. TNF inhibitors (biologic DMARDs): etanercept (Enbrel), adalimumab (Humira), infliximab (Remicade), certolizumab, golimumab. They target TNF-alpha, a key inflammatory cytokine. Risk: increased infection (screen for TB before starting), reactivation of latent infections.")
B.YU=s(["Osteoarthritis only","Rheumatoid arthritis (marginal erosions at bare areas of bone)","Fibromyalgia","Tendinitis"],t.s)
B.a9B=new A.e("Erosive joint disease on X-ray is a hallmark of:",B.YU,1,'RA: marginal erosions at "bare areas" where bone is exposed without cartilage protection (joint margins). Pannus (inflammatory tissue) invades and destroys cartilage and bone. X-ray findings: periarticular osteopenia, symmetric joint space narrowing, marginal erosions, subluxations. OA shows osteophytes, asymmetric narrowing, sclerosis \u2014 NOT erosions.')
B.Tb=s(["E. coli","Staphylococcus aureus","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Pseudomonas"],t.s)
B.a6q=new A.e("Septic arthritis is a joint emergency. The most common causative organism is:",B.Tb,1,"S. aureus is the most common cause of septic arthritis in adults. Gonococcus (Neisseria gonorrhoeae) is most common in sexually active young adults. Clinical: acute monoarticular hot, swollen joint with severe pain. Joint aspiration is MANDATORY: WBC >50,000, >75% neutrophils, positive culture. Treatment: IV antibiotics \xb1 surgical drainage. Delay = joint destruction.")
B.Pl=s(["PIP hyperextension + DIP flexion","PIP flexion + DIP hyperextension (from central slip rupture)","MCP hyperextension","Wrist subluxation"],t.s)
B.a9m=new A.e("The Boutonni\xe8re deformity in RA consists of:",B.Pl,1,"Boutonni\xe8re: PIP flexion + DIP hyperextension. Mechanism: synovitis stretches/ruptures the central slip of the extensor tendon at the PIP \u2192 PIP drops into flexion \u2192 lateral bands migrate volar to the PIP axis (flex PIP) and pull taut \u2192 DIP hyperextends. Opposite of swan neck deformity.")
B.Wh=s(["Only pain management","Joint protection education, adaptive equipment, splinting, and energy conservation","Strengthening to maximum effort","Surgical planning"],t.s)
B.aaM=new A.e("Occupational therapy in RA focuses on:",B.Wh,1,"OT in RA: joint protection techniques (ergonomic modifications, avoiding deforming forces), adaptive equipment (built-up handles, jar openers, button hooks), resting and functional splints (resting hand splint, ulnar drift splint), energy conservation strategies, and work simplification. The goal is maintaining function and independence while protecting damaged joints.")
B.TO=s([B.abX,B.a9B,B.a6q,B.a9m,B.aaM],t.b)
B.JU=new A.a6("Rheumatology 4",B.TO)
B.MI=s(["Anterior cord syndrome","Central cord syndrome","Brown-S\xe9quard syndrome","Conus medullaris syndrome"],t.s)
B.aaD=new A.e("Incomplete SCI has better prognosis than complete SCI. The most favorable incomplete syndrome is:",B.MI,2,"Brown-S\xe9quard (cord hemisection) has the best prognosis of all incomplete SCI syndromes \u2014 approximately 90% achieve community ambulation. Central cord syndrome also has a reasonable prognosis (legs recover well, hands worst). Anterior cord syndrome has the worst prognosis among incomplete syndromes.")
B.Sy=s(["C7","T6","T12","L3-L4 (functional quadriceps)"],t.s)
B.ab9=new A.e("For community ambulation, an SCI patient typically needs motor function at which level?",B.Sy,3,"Practical community ambulation generally requires at least L3-L4 motor function (quadriceps for knee stability). Higher-level paraplegics may achieve therapeutic/exercise ambulation with KAFOs but the energy cost is prohibitive for community use (up to 6-9x normal energy expenditure for thoracic-level paraplegia).")
B.R3=s(["Power wheelchair with sip-and-puff","Power wheelchair with joystick","Manual wheelchair with independent mobility","No wheelchair needed"],t.s)
B.a74=new A.e("A T1 complete SCI patient would use which type of wheelchair?",B.R3,2,"T1 paraplegia: full upper extremity function (all hand muscles intact). These patients are fully independent with a manual wheelchair, transfers, and all ADLs. They can independently manage bowel/bladder programs. A lightweight manual wheelchair is standard. Power wheelchair is reserved for tetraplegic levels.")
B.Ux=s(["Standard of care for all SCI","Recommended as a treatment option but no longer standard of care due to side effects","Absolutely contraindicated","Only used in pediatric SCI"],t.s)
B.a8Q=new A.e("Methylprednisolone for acute SCI (NASCIS protocol) is currently:",B.Ux,1,"High-dose methylprednisolone (NASCIS II/III) is no longer standard of care. The modest neurological benefits are offset by increased complications (infections, GI bleeding, pneumonia). Current guidelines consider it an option (not a recommendation), and many centers have stopped using it. The decision is left to the treating physician.")
B.OG=s(["Prostate cancer only","Bladder cancer and upper tract deterioration (hydronephrosis)","Kidney stones only","Urethral strictures only"],t.s)
B.a8o=new A.e("SCI patients require lifelong surveillance for which urological complication?",B.OG,1,"SCI patients need annual urological surveillance: renal ultrasound (hydronephrosis), urodynamics (bladder pressures), and monitoring for bladder cancer (risk increases with chronic catheterization, especially indwelling). Renal function (creatinine clearance) should be checked regularly. Upper tract preservation is the primary goal.")
B.Mj=s([B.aaD,B.ab9,B.a74,B.a8Q,B.a8o],t.b)
B.JV=new A.a6("Spinal Cord Injury 10",B.Mj)
B.Oy=s(["Constant rigidity throughout the range","Initial resistance to passive stretch that suddenly gives way","Rhythmic oscillations during movement","Pain-free range of motion"],t.s)
B.a3K=new A.e("The clasp-knife phenomenon in spasticity refers to:",B.Oy,1,'Clasp-knife: initial high resistance to passive stretch that suddenly "gives way" (like opening a clasp knife). This is mediated by the Golgi tendon organ (Ib inhibition) that activates at high tension levels and inhibits the motor neuron. It is a classic UMN spasticity finding.')
B.Wm=s(["Sustained muscle contraction","Rhythmic, involuntary oscillating contractions elicited by quick stretch","Muscle fasciculations","Voluntary rapid alternating movements"],t.s)
B.a7R=new A.e("Clonus is best described as:",B.Wm,1,"Clonus: rhythmic involuntary contractions (typically at the ankle) elicited by quick dorsiflexion stretch. It results from hyperexcitable stretch reflexes in UMN lesions. Sustained clonus (>5 beats) is always pathological and indicates significant UMN dysfunction. It can interfere with standing and transfers.")
B.ZR=s(["Femoral nerve","Sciatic nerve","Obturator nerve","Tibial nerve"],t.s)
B.aau=new A.e("Which nerve is most commonly targeted with phenol for hip adductor spasticity?",B.ZR,2,"The obturator nerve innervates the hip adductors. Phenol neurolysis of the obturator nerve is a classic treatment for scissoring gait pattern (excessive hip adduction) in patients with bilateral UMN spasticity (CP, SCI). It is preferred over botulinum toxin for large muscle groups due to cost-effectiveness.")
B.XK=s(["Contracture is more painful","Spasticity is velocity-dependent and reduces under anesthesia; contracture is fixed and does not","They are clinically identical","Spasticity only occurs in the lower extremities"],t.s)
B.a3r=new A.e("In distinguishing spasticity from contracture, the key difference is:",B.XK,1,"Spasticity: velocity-dependent, reduces with slow passive movement, abolished under anesthesia/nerve block. Contracture: fixed structural shortening of muscle/tendon/capsule \u2014 resistance is constant regardless of speed and persists under anesthesia. Many patients have both, and distinguishing them guides treatment (medications for spasticity, stretching/surgery for contracture).")
B.Mo=s(["Cutting ventral (motor) nerve roots","Selectively cutting dorsal (sensory) rootlets that contribute to the spastic reflex arc","Removing the spinal cord segment","Implanting an intrathecal pump"],t.s)
B.abK=new A.e("Selective dorsal rhizotomy (SDR) for spasticity involves:",B.Mo,1,"SDR selectively divides dorsal (sensory) rootlets at L1-S1 that demonstrate abnormal reflex responses to electrical stimulation. By reducing excessive sensory input to the reflex arc, spasticity is permanently reduced. Primarily used in children with cerebral palsy (spastic diplegia). Requires intensive post-operative rehabilitation.")
B.SU=s([B.a3K,B.a7R,B.aau,B.a3r,B.abK],t.b)
B.JW=new A.a6("Spasticity 3",B.SU)
B.Sj=s(["Continuous propagation of action potentials along unmyelinated fibers","Action potentials jumping from one node of Ranvier to the next","Conduction across the neuromuscular junction","Retrograde conduction in sensory nerves"],t.s)
B.a69=new A.e("Saltatory conduction refers to:",B.Sj,1,'Saltatory conduction is the process by which action potentials "jump" between nodes of Ranvier in myelinated nerves. This dramatically increases conduction velocity (up to 120 m/s) and reduces energy expenditure compared to continuous conduction in unmyelinated fibers.')
B.Un=s(["-40 mV","-55 mV","-70 mV","-90 mV"],t.s)
B.a5W=new A.e("The resting membrane potential of a typical neuron is approximately:",B.Un,2,"The resting membrane potential is approximately -70 mV, maintained primarily by the Na+/K+ ATPase pump (3 Na+ out, 2 K+ in) and potassium leak channels. This negative intracellular charge is essential for action potential generation.")
B.WG=s(["Potassium channels","Calcium channels","Chloride channels","Sodium channels"],t.s)
B.a7N=new A.e("During depolarization of an action potential, which ion channels open first?",B.WG,3,"Voltage-gated sodium channels open first during depolarization, allowing rapid Na+ influx that drives the membrane potential from -70 mV toward +30 mV. Potassium channels open later for repolarization. This sequence is the basis of the action potential.")
B.TJ=s(["Norepinephrine","GABA","Glutamate","Acetylcholine"],t.s)
B.acw=new A.e("The neuromuscular junction uses which neurotransmitter?",B.TJ,3,"Acetylcholine (ACh) is the neurotransmitter at the neuromuscular junction. It is released from the presynaptic terminal, crosses the synaptic cleft, and binds to nicotinic receptors on the motor end plate, triggering muscle contraction.")
B.YD=s(["Increased conduction velocity and decreased amplitude","Decreased conduction velocity and increased amplitude","No change in conduction parameters","Decreased conduction velocity and decreased amplitude"],t.s)
B.ac8=new A.e("Cooling a nerve will cause which of the following changes on nerve conduction studies?",B.YD,1,"Cool temperatures slow sodium channel kinetics, prolonging the action potential duration. This decreases conduction velocity but paradoxically increases amplitude (the prolonged action potential allows more temporal summation). This can mimic demyelinating neuropathy \u2014 always check limb temperature!")
B.Ts=s([B.a69,B.a5W,B.a7N,B.acw,B.ac8],t.b)
B.JX=new A.a6("Electrodiagnostic 2",B.Ts)
B.VC=s(["Sensory loss in the thumb and index finger",'Pure motor deficit with inability to form an "OK" sign',"Wrist drop with radial deviation","Thenar atrophy and thumb opposition weakness"],t.s)
B.a4w=new A.e("Anterior interosseous nerve (AIN) syndrome is characterized by:",B.VC,1,'AIN syndrome is pure motor \u2014 NO sensory findings. It causes weakness of FPL (thumb IP flexion), FDP to digits 2-3 (index/middle DIP flexion), and pronator quadratus. The positive "OK sign" test (can\'t make a circle) is characteristic. Routine median NCS are NORMAL.')
B.PH=s(["Absent","Decreased","Normal (preserved)","Hyperesthetic"],t.s)
B.a9J=new A.e("In carpal tunnel syndrome, sensation at the base of the thumb is typically:",B.PH,2,"The palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve exits 5 cm proximal to the wrist and travels OVER the transverse carpal ligament, not through the tunnel. Therefore, thenar eminence/thumb base sensation is preserved in CTS. If numb, suspect a more proximal lesion.")
B.VX=s(["5 tendons + median nerve","7 tendons + median nerve","9 tendons + median nerve","11 tendons + median nerve"],t.s)
B.aak=new A.e("The carpal tunnel contains how many tendons plus the median nerve?",B.VX,2,'The "Nine Plus One Rule": 4 FDS tendons + 4 FDP tendons + 1 FPL tendon + 1 median nerve = 10 structures. The FCR tendon runs in its own separate compartment, which is why wrist flexion is preserved in carpal tunnel syndrome.')
B.Zl=s(["Radial to ulnar","Median to ulnar","Ulnar to median","Musculocutaneous to median"],t.s)
B.abA=new A.e("Martin-Gruber anastomosis is a crossover between which two nerves in the forearm?",B.Zl,1,"Martin-Gruber anastomosis (15-20% prevalence) is a median-to-ulnar nerve crossover in the forearm. Motor fibers from the median nerve cross to join the ulnar nerve. This can cause confusing NCS results in carpal tunnel or ulnar neuropathy.")
B.Wf=s(["Prolonged sensory latency with normal amplitude","Normal sensory and motor studies","Absent SNAP, prolonged/low motor response, fibrillations in APB","Prolonged sensory latency only"],t.s)
B.abG=new A.e("Severe carpal tunnel syndrome on NCS is characterized by:",B.Wf,2,"CTS severity grading: Mild = prolonged sensory latency only. Moderate = abnormal sensory + prolonged motor latency. Severe = absent SNAP, low/prolonged motor response, and fibrillation potentials in the abductor pollicis brevis (APB) on EMG.")
B.Vh=s([B.a4w,B.a9J,B.aak,B.abA,B.abG],t.b)
B.JY=new A.a6("Electrodiagnostic 8",B.Vh)
B.Ql=s(["Isolated ACL tear","Severe tricompartmental osteoarthritis failing conservative management","Osgood-Schlatter disease","Acute meniscal tear"],t.s)
B.a6n=new A.e("Total knee arthroplasty is indicated for which of the following?",B.Ql,1,"TKA is indicated for severe symptomatic OA (typically tricompartmental) that has failed conservative treatment including weight loss, PT, injections, and medications. Unicompartmental (partial) knee replacement may be considered for isolated medial or lateral compartment disease.")
B.XV=s(["60\xb0","90\xb0","120\xb0","135\xb0"],t.s)
B.a7_=new A.e("After total knee arthroplasty, achieving which degree of knee flexion is considered a functional goal?",B.XV,2,"A functional goal of 120\xb0 flexion allows patients to perform most daily activities. Minimum needed: 67\xb0 for swing phase of gait, 83\xb0 for stairs, 93\xb0 to rise from a chair, ~105-110\xb0 for bathing. 120\xb0 is an excellent outcome.")
B.Mp=s(["ACL tear","Repeated kneeling on a hard surface","Quadriceps tendon rupture","Meniscal degeneration"],t.s)
B.a4M=new A.e('Prepatellar bursitis ("housemaid\'s knee") is caused by:',B.Mp,1,"Prepatellar bursitis results from repetitive friction/pressure over the anterior patella, classically from kneeling (housemaid, roofer, plumber). Septic bursitis must be excluded by aspiration if warmth, erythema, or fever are present.")
B.RQ=s(["ACL","PCL","MCL (medial collateral ligament)","LCL (lateral collateral ligament)"],t.s)
B.ac7=new A.e("The valgus stress test at the knee evaluates which structure?",B.RQ,2,"Valgus stress test: apply a lateral-to-medial force at the knee (pushing the knee medially). Laxity at 30\xb0 flexion = isolated MCL injury. Laxity at full extension = MCL + possible cruciate/capsular involvement (more severe).")
B.PW=s(["ACL tear","MCL sprain","Bucket-handle meniscal tear","Patellofemoral syndrome"],t.s)
B.a7W=new A.e("A locked knee that cannot fully extend is most likely caused by:",B.PW,2,'A bucket-handle meniscal tear is a displaced vertical longitudinal tear that flips into the intercondylar notch, mechanically blocking full extension ("locked knee"). This is a surgical urgency requiring arthroscopic reduction and repair/resection.')
B.Sn=s([B.a6n,B.a7_,B.a4M,B.ac7,B.a7W],t.b)
B.JZ=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Knee 3",B.Sn)
B.Zc=s(["Destroying the muscle fibers","Blocking acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction","Inhibiting spinal cord reflexes","Enhancing GABA activity"],t.s)
B.aai=new A.e("Botulinum toxin reduces spasticity by:",B.Zc,1,"Botulinum toxin blocks presynaptic ACh release at the NMJ, causing chemical denervation and muscle relaxation. Effects onset in 24-72 hours, peak at 2-6 weeks, and last 3-6 months before nerve terminal regeneration occurs. It is best for focal/regional spasticity. Avoid in patients with NMJ disorders.")
B.OI=s(["Mild focal spasticity","Severe generalized spasticity unresponsive to oral medications","All spasticity patients","As first-line treatment"],t.s)
B.abv=new A.e("Intrathecal baclofen pump (ITB) is indicated for:",B.OI,1,"ITB delivers baclofen directly to the intrathecal space, achieving 100x higher CSF concentrations with 1/100th the oral dose. This minimizes systemic side effects (drowsiness, confusion). Indicated for severe generalized spasticity (SCI, MS, CP, TBI) failing oral medications. Requires trial dose before pump implantation.")
B.ZF=s(["Mild muscle stiffness","Life-threatening rebound spasticity, hyperthermia, rhabdomyolysis, and multi-organ failure","Headache only","Improved motor function"],t.s)
B.a47=new A.e("Sudden withdrawal from intrathecal baclofen can cause:",B.ZF,1,"ITB withdrawal is a medical emergency: severe rebound spasticity, hyperthermia (>40\xb0C), rhabdomyolysis, DIC, and multi-organ failure \u2014 can be fatal. Causes: pump malfunction, catheter kink/disconnect, empty reservoir. Treatment: high-dose oral/IV baclofen or IV benzodiazepines, and urgent pump evaluation.")
B.XY=s(["Enhancing nerve conduction","Chemical destruction of nerve fibers","Blocking calcium channels","Stimulating GABA receptors"],t.s)
B.a8Z=new A.e("Phenol neurolysis for spasticity works by:",B.XY,1,"Phenol (5-7%) injected near a nerve causes protein denaturation and chemical neurolysis, reducing spasticity. Effects last 3-6 months (longer than botulinum toxin). Best for mixed nerves with both motor and sensory components (e.g., obturator nerve for hip adductor spasticity). Complications: pain, dysesthesia (if sensory nerve affected).")
B.YJ=s(["Strengthen weakened muscles","Progressively increase range of motion by sustained low-load stretch","Replace surgical intervention permanently","Eliminate the need for medications"],t.s)
B.a5U=new A.e("Serial casting for spasticity aims to:",B.YJ,1,"Serial casting applies prolonged low-load stretch to gradually lengthen spastic muscles and increase ROM. Casts are changed every 5-7 days with progressive improvement in joint position. Most commonly used for ankle equinus (plantarflexion contracture). Often combined with botulinum toxin for optimal results.")
B.Qu=s([B.aai,B.abv,B.a47,B.a8Z,B.a5U],t.b)
B.K_=new A.a6("Spasticity 2",B.Qu)
B.UA=s(["Lumbar disc herniation","Trauma (fall onto buttocks) or repetitive microtrauma","Sacroiliac joint dysfunction","Piriformis syndrome"],t.s)
B.aaZ=new A.e("Coccydynia (tailbone pain) is most commonly caused by:",B.UA,1,"Coccydynia is most commonly caused by direct trauma (falls onto buttocks) or repetitive microtrauma (prolonged sitting on hard surfaces). Treatment includes cushion modifications (donut/wedge cushion), NSAIDs, and local corticosteroid injection. Coccygectomy is a last resort.")
B.Xj=s(["Any complication during spine surgery","Persistent or recurrent pain after spinal surgery","Spinal cord injury during surgery","Post-surgical infection"],t.s)
B.abU=new A.e("Failed back surgery syndrome (FBSS) is defined as:",B.Xj,1,"FBSS is persistent or recurrent lumbar/leg pain after anatomically successful spine surgery. Causes include wrong level surgery, recurrent disc herniation, epidural fibrosis, foraminal stenosis, and adjacent segment disease. Spinal cord stimulation may benefit selected patients.")
B.Y3=s(["High-velocity trauma","Osteoporosis","Metastatic disease","Infection"],t.s)
B.acv=new A.e("Vertebral compression fractures are most commonly caused by:",B.Y3,1,"Osteoporosis is the most common cause of vertebral compression fractures, especially in postmenopausal women. They most commonly occur at the thoracolumbar junction (T11-L1). Kyphoplasty or vertebroplasty may be considered for pain refractory to conservative management.")
B.WL=s(["It has the largest vertebral bodies","It is the transition zone between the rigid thoracic spine and mobile lumbar spine","It has the weakest posterior ligament complex","It lacks facet joint support"],t.s)
B.aas=new A.e("The thoracolumbar junction (T11-L1) is particularly vulnerable to fractures because:",B.WL,1,"The thoracolumbar junction is the transition between the relatively rigid thoracic spine (stabilized by rib cage) and the mobile lumbar spine. This creates a stress concentration point, making it the most common location for traumatic spinal fractures.")
B.US=s(["Sacroiliac joint fusion","Flowing ossification along at least 4 contiguous vertebral body anterior margins","Bamboo spine on X-ray","Facet joint ankylosis"],t.s)
B.a81=new A.e("Diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis (DISH) is characterized by:",B.US,1,"DISH requires flowing ossification along \u22654 contiguous vertebral bodies anterolaterally, with preserved disc height and no SI joint or facet joint ankylosis. It is distinguished from ankylosing spondylitis (which involves SI joints and has bamboo spine).")
B.VL=s([B.aaZ,B.abU,B.acv,B.aas,B.a81],t.b)
B.K0=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Spine 6",B.VL)
B.Mw=s(["Anterior spinal artery","Posterior inferior cerebellar artery (PICA)","Anterior inferior cerebellar artery (AICA)","Superior cerebellar artery"],t.s)
B.a71=new A.e("Wallenberg syndrome (lateral medullary syndrome) results from occlusion of which artery?",B.Mw,1,"Wallenberg syndrome is caused by PICA occlusion affecting the lateral medulla. Classic findings include ipsilateral facial pain/temperature loss, contralateral body pain/temperature loss, dysphagia, hoarseness, vertigo, and ipsilateral Horner syndrome.")
B.QY=s(["Weber syndrome","Wallenberg syndrome","Locked-in syndrome","Benedikt syndrome"],t.s)
B.a3V=new A.e("A patient presents with ipsilateral facial numbness, contralateral body numbness, dysphagia, hoarseness, and Horner syndrome. What is the most likely diagnosis?",B.QY,1,"This is the classic presentation of Wallenberg (lateral medullary) syndrome. The crossed sensory findings (ipsilateral face, contralateral body) are pathognomonic and a favorite board question.")
B.Or=s(["Lateral medulla","Ventral pons","Midbrain","Thalamus"],t.s)
B.acc=new A.e("Locked-in syndrome is caused by a lesion in which structure?",B.Or,1,"Locked-in syndrome results from a ventral pontine lesion (typically basilar artery occlusion). The patient is fully conscious but quadriplegic and mute, retaining only vertical eye movements and blinking. Cognition is completely intact.")
B.RC=s(["Pons","Medulla","Midbrain","Cerebellum"],t.s)
B.a7S=new A.e("Weber syndrome is characterized by ipsilateral CN III palsy and contralateral hemiparesis. The lesion is located in which structure?",B.RC,2,'Weber syndrome is a midbrain syndrome caused by damage to the cerebral peduncle and CN III fascicle. It produces ipsilateral oculomotor palsy (ptosis, dilated pupil, "down and out" eye) and contralateral hemiparesis.')
B.Yn=s(["Thalamus","Posterior limb of the internal capsule","Caudate nucleus","Cerebral cortex"],t.s)
B.a7p=new A.e("A lacunar infarct causing pure motor hemiparesis is most commonly located in which structure?",B.Yn,1,"Pure motor hemiparesis is the most common lacunar syndrome. The lesion is typically in the posterior limb of the internal capsule or basis pontis. It produces contralateral face, arm, and leg weakness without sensory, visual, or cognitive deficits.")
B.ZB=s([B.a71,B.a3V,B.acc,B.a7S,B.a7p],t.b)
B.K1=new A.a6("Cerebrovascular Accident 3",B.ZB)
B.Vm=s(["The hip joint","The tibial tuberosity apophysis in adolescents","The lumbar spine","The shoulder joint"],t.s)
B.ab7=new A.e("Osgood-Schlatter disease affects:",B.Vm,1,"Osgood-Schlatter: traction apophysitis of the tibial tubercle from repetitive quadriceps contraction through the patellar tendon. Most common in active adolescents during growth spurts (boys 12-14, girls 10-12). Treatment: activity modification, quad stretching, ice. Self-limiting \u2014 resolves when growth plate closes.")
B.XT=s(["The tibial tuberosity","The calcaneal apophysis (heel)","The medial epicondyle","The iliac crest"],t.s)
B.a4Z=new A.e("Sever disease is a traction apophysitis affecting:",B.XT,1,"Sever disease (calcaneal apophysitis): traction injury at the Achilles tendon insertion on the calcaneal growth plate. Most common cause of heel pain in active children ages 8-14. Bilateral in 60%. Treatment: heel cups, Achilles stretching, activity modification. Self-limiting with skeletal maturity.")
B.Vr=s(["Femoral head avascular necrosis","Pathologic tibia vara (bowleg) from growth plate disturbance of the proximal medial tibia","Scoliosis","Wrist fracture"],t.s)
B.aav=new A.e("The Blount disease involves:",B.Vr,1,"Blount disease: abnormal endochondral bone formation of the medial proximal tibial physis causing progressive tibia vara (bowleg). Distinguished from physiologic bowing (improves by age 3). Infantile type (1-3 years, often bilateral, associated with obesity/early walking) and adolescent type (unilateral). Bracing for infantile; surgery for severe/adolescent.")
B.Uh=s(["Neonates","Overweight adolescents with hip or knee pain and limited internal rotation","Toddlers learning to walk","Elderly patients"],t.s)
B.ac3=new A.e("Slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE) typically presents in:",B.Uh,1,"SCFE: the femoral epiphysis slips posteriorly and medially relative to the metaphysis through the weakened growth plate. Classic patient: overweight adolescent (10-16 years), boys > girls. Presents with hip, groin, or referred knee pain and obligatory external rotation with hip flexion. Emergency: NO weight-bearing; surgical pinning in situ.")
B.Vs=s(["Intelligence quotient only","Four domains: gross motor, fine motor/adaptive, language, personal-social","Academic readiness","Behavioral problems only"],t.s)
B.a9U=new A.e("In pediatric rehabilitation, the Denver Developmental Screening Test II assesses:",B.Vs,1,"Denver II: screening tool for developmental delays in children 0-6 years across 4 domains: gross motor, fine motor-adaptive, language, and personal-social. Results are categorized as normal, suspect, or untestable. It is a SCREENING tool (not diagnostic) \u2014 abnormal results require comprehensive evaluation with tools like the Bayley Scales.")
B.WF=s([B.ab7,B.a4Z,B.aav,B.ac3,B.a9U],t.b)
B.K2=new A.a6("Pediatrics 6",B.WF)
B.Wg=s(["Day 1","Day 3","Day 7","Day 14"],t.s)
B.a5r=new A.e("After a facial nerve injury, prognostic NCS testing should be delayed until at least:",B.Wg,2,'The "seven-day rule": the distal nerve stump remains excitable for 3-7 days after injury due to Wallerian degeneration timeline. Testing before day 7 may show falsely normal responses, giving inaccurate prognosis.')
B.Sk=s(["Excellent prognosis, recovery in weeks","Good prognosis, recovery in 2 months","Fair prognosis, recovery in 2-8 months","Poor prognosis, recovery may take over 1 year"],t.s)
B.a39=new A.e("A facial nerve CMAP amplitude less than 10% of the normal side indicates:",B.Sk,3,"Facial nerve prognosis by CMAP amplitude: >30% = good (recovery <2 months), 10-30% = fair (2-8 months), <10% = poor (>1 year recovery). This guides counseling and surgical decision-making.")
B.U2=s(["Anterior triangle of the neck at the thyroid cartilage","Posterior border of the SCM, 3 cm above the clavicle","Suprasternal notch","Axilla at the level of the second rib"],t.s)
B.a6y=new A.e("The phrenic nerve is stimulated at which anatomical landmark for NCS?",B.U2,1,"The phrenic nerve is stimulated at the posterior border of the sternocleidomastoid (SCM) muscle, approximately 3 cm above the clavicle. Recording is at the xiphoid process with a 16 cm electrode distance.")
B.Ry=s(["Brachial plexus","Cervical spinal cord","Thalamus","Primary somatosensory cortex"],t.s)
B.a9l=new A.e("In somatosensory evoked potentials (SSEPs) for the median nerve, the N20 waveform represents arrival at which structure?",B.Ry,3,"Median nerve SSEP checkpoints: N9 = brachial plexus (Erb's point), N11 = root entry zone, N13 = cervical cord/medulla, P14 = brainstem, N18 = thalamus, N20 = primary somatosensory cortex. The number roughly corresponds to milliseconds.")
B.ZP=s(["Propofol and narcotics","Halogenated anesthetics (sevoflurane, isoflurane)","Ketamine","Dexmedetomidine"],t.s)
B.a87=new A.e("During intraoperative SSEP monitoring, which anesthetic agents should be avoided because they suppress cortical signals?",B.ZP,1,"Halogenated anesthetics (sevoflurane, isoflurane, desflurane) suppress cortical SSEP signals and can mimic CNS injury. Total intravenous anesthesia (TIVA) with propofol and narcotics is preferred for intraoperative neuromonitoring.")
B.U8=s([B.a5r,B.a39,B.a6y,B.a9l,B.a87],t.b)
B.K3=new A.a6("Electrodiagnostic 5",B.U8)
B.SI=s(["Completed stroke","Reversible ischemic neurologic deficit (RIND)","Transient ischemic attack (TIA)","Minor stroke"],t.s)
B.abD=new A.e("A patient presents with neurological deficits that completely resolve within 18 hours. How should this event be classified?",B.SI,2,"Neurological deficits lasting less than 24 hours are classified as a TIA. The term RIND is obsolete and should never be selected on board exams. The modern binary classification is TIA (<24 hours) or stroke (\u226524 hours).")
B.YA=s(["10.5%","21.2%","40%","58.4%"],t.s)
B.a5H=new A.e("According to the ARIC study data, what is the approximate mortality rate at 1 year post-stroke?",B.YA,1,"Per the ARIC (Atherosclerosis Risk in Communities) study: 30-day mortality is ~10.5%, 1-year is ~21.2%, 5-year is ~40%, and 24-year is ~58.4%. Board examiners frequently test this timeline.")
B.Qa=s(["Increased hypertension prevalence","Decreased use of antihypertensives","Widespread adoption of CT scanning","Rising obesity rates"],t.s)
B.a4b=new A.e("The apparent rise in stroke incidence in the early 1980s is best explained by which of the following?",B.Qa,2,'The ~17% spike in stroke incidence in the early 1980s is a data artifact caused by the widespread availability of CT scanners. Previously, many strokes were misdiagnosed as "sudden spells" or "senility." Better detection increased apparent incidence without a true increase in events.')
B.SE=s(["Third","Fourth","Fifth","Sixth"],t.s)
B.a3k=new A.e("Stroke is currently ranked as which leading cause of death in the United States?",B.SE,2,"Stroke is the 5th leading cause of death in the US, behind: (1) heart disease, (2) cancer, (3) chronic lower respiratory disease (COPD), and (4) accidents/unintentional injuries. It previously held the 3rd position.")
B.Zj=s(["Introduction of tPA therapy","Advent of effective antihypertensive medications","Development of CT scanning","Improved surgical thrombectomy techniques"],t.s)
B.a63=new A.e("The dramatic 46% decline in stroke mortality from the 1950s to the 1970s is most strongly correlated with which development?",B.Zj,1,"The massive decline in stroke mortality from the 1950s-1970s correlates directly with the introduction of effective antihypertensive medications. Controlling blood pressure was the single greatest factor in reducing stroke deaths during this period.")
B.Zx=s([B.abD,B.a5H,B.a4b,B.a3k,B.a63],t.b)
B.K4=new A.a6("Cerebrovascular Accident 1",B.Zx)
B.X3=s(["Large weight-bearing joints only","Small joints of the hands and feet symmetrically (MCPs, PIPs, wrists)","The spine exclusively","DIP joints only"],t.s)
B.a9i=new A.e("Rheumatoid arthritis primarily affects which joints?",B.X3,1,"RA: symmetric inflammatory polyarthritis primarily affecting MCPs, PIPs, and wrists. DIPs are typically SPARED (DIP involvement suggests osteoarthritis or psoriatic arthritis). Morning stiffness >1 hour is characteristic. RF and anti-CCP antibodies are diagnostic markers. Treatment: early DMARDs (methotrexate first-line).")
B.Rz=s(["DIP hyperextension and PIP flexion","PIP hyperextension and DIP flexion","MCP ulnar deviation only","Thumb Z-deformity"],t.s)
B.abr=new A.e("Swan neck deformity in RA consists of:",B.Rz,1,"Swan neck: PIP hyperextension + DIP flexion. Caused by intrinsic muscle tightness, volar plate laxity at PIP, and lateral band dorsal subluxation. Boutonni\xe8re deformity is the opposite: PIP flexion + DIP hyperextension (central slip rupture). Both result from chronic synovitis destroying periarticular structures.")
B.SL=s(["Increase its efficacy","Reduce side effects (stomatitis, GI upset, cytopenias) without reducing efficacy","Treat anemia of chronic disease","Improve joint lubrication"],t.s)
B.a4z=new A.e("Methotrexate in RA is supplemented with folic acid to:",B.SL,1,"Folic acid (1 mg daily or 5 mg weekly, taken on non-MTX days) reduces methotrexate side effects: oral ulcers, nausea, hepatotoxicity, and cytopenias. It does NOT reduce MTX efficacy. Folinic acid (leucovorin) can be used for rescue if MTX toxicity is severe. Regular monitoring: CBC, LFTs, creatinine.")
B.Tp=s(["It causes carpal tunnel syndrome","Pannus erosion of the transverse ligament allows C1 to sublux on C2, risking spinal cord compression","It only causes neck pain","It is always asymptomatic"],t.s)
B.a6e=new A.e("Atlantoaxial (C1-C2) instability in RA is dangerous because:",B.Tp,1,"RA-related C1-C2 instability: inflammatory pannus erodes the transverse ligament and dens \u2192 anterior atlantoaxial subluxation. ADI >3mm (adults) is abnormal. Can progress to myelopathy (cord compression) or sudden death. Flexion-extension cervical X-rays are essential before intubation/surgery in RA patients. Surgical fusion if symptomatic or ADI >9mm.")
B.MG=s(["Is an autoimmune disease","Is a degenerative joint disease with asymmetric involvement, DIP/PIP nodes, and minimal morning stiffness (<30 min)","Always requires biological therapy","Causes positive RF and anti-CCP"],t.s)
B.a6x=new A.e("Osteoarthritis (OA) differs from RA in that OA:",B.MG,1,'OA: degenerative "wear and tear" of articular cartilage. Key differences from RA: asymmetric involvement, DIP joints involved (Heberden nodes), PIP nodes (Bouchard), minimal morning stiffness (<30 minutes vs. >1 hour in RA), normal inflammatory markers, and no systemic features. First CMC joint involvement is classic.')
B.PV=s([B.a9i,B.abr,B.a4z,B.a6e,B.a6x],t.b)
B.K5=new A.a6("Rheumatology 1",B.PV)
B.PQ=s(["Unilateral arm weakness only","Upper motor neuron signs in the lower extremities (spasticity, hyperreflexia, Babinski)","Isolated neck pain without neurological deficits","Normal gait with isolated hand numbness"],t.s)
B.a4d=new A.e("Cervical myelopathy is characterized by which finding?",B.PQ,1,"Cervical myelopathy compresses the spinal cord, producing UMN signs below the level of compression: spastic gait, hyperreflexia, Babinski sign, Hoffman sign, and clumsiness. It is a surgical condition \u2014 progressive myelopathy requires decompression.")
B.YZ=s(["Constant pain unrelated to activity","Neurogenic claudication that improves with flexion (shopping cart sign)","Acute onset foot drop","Pain that worsens with sitting"],t.s)
B.a83=new A.e("Lumbar spinal stenosis classically presents with:",B.YZ,1,"Neurogenic claudication: bilateral leg pain/heaviness with walking that improves with flexion (leaning on a shopping cart, sitting). Flexion opens the spinal canal. Extension narrows it. This distinguishes it from vascular claudication (which improves with standing still, not flexion).")
B.O1=s(["Flexing the neck while applying axial compression","Extending and laterally flexing the neck toward the affected side with axial compression","Rotating the neck away from the affected side","Applying traction to the neck"],t.s)
B.a4t=new A.e("The Spurling test for cervical radiculopathy involves:",B.O1,1,"Spurling's test: extend, laterally flex toward the symptomatic side, and apply axial compression. This narrows the neural foramen and reproduces radicular symptoms. It has high specificity (~93%) but moderate sensitivity (~40-60%) for cervical radiculopathy.")
B.TX=s(["A fracture of the vertebral body","Forward slippage of one vertebra on another","Disc herniation into the spinal canal","Lateral curvature of the spine"],t.s)
B.a8j=new A.e("Spondylolisthesis refers to:",B.TX,1,"Spondylolisthesis is anterior slippage of a vertebra relative to the one below. It is most common at L5-S1. Graded I-V by percentage of slip (Meyerding classification). Isthmic type (pars defect/spondylolysis) is most common in young athletes. Degenerative type is most common in older adults.")
B.PT=s(["Vertebral body","Spinous process","Pars interarticularis","Transverse process"],t.s)
B.aaX=new A.e("Spondylolysis is a defect in which part of the vertebra?",B.PT,2,'Spondylolysis is a stress fracture/defect of the pars interarticularis, most common at L5. Bilateral pars defects can lead to spondylolisthesis. The "Scotty dog" sign on oblique X-ray shows the pars defect as a collar on the dog. Common in gymnasts, football linemen, and dancers.')
B.QA=s([B.a4d,B.a83,B.a4t,B.a8j,B.aaX],t.b)
B.K6=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Spine 2",B.QA)
B.UL=s(["0 to 10","1 to 20","6 to 20","1 to 100"],t.s)
B.a35=new A.e("The Borg Rating of Perceived Exertion (RPE) scale ranges from:",B.UL,2,'The classic Borg RPE scale ranges from 6 to 20, designed to roughly correlate with heart rate when multiplied by 10 (e.g., RPE 12 \u2248 HR 120). Target exercise intensity in cardiac rehab is typically RPE 11-14 ("fairly light" to "somewhat hard").')
B.Pu=s(["Resting in bed","Walking 3-4 mph on level ground","Running at 6 mph","Competitive swimming"],t.s)
B.a6X=new A.e("Which of the following activities requires approximately 3-5 METs?",B.Pu,1,"Walking at 3-4 mph is approximately 3-5 METs (moderate intensity). Resting = 1 MET, light walking = 2-3 METs, running = 8+ METs. Understanding MET levels for common activities is essential for exercise prescription.")
B.Z2=s(["Controlled hypertension","Unstable angina not stabilized by medical therapy","Mild aortic stenosis","History of prior MI more than 6 months ago"],t.s)
B.a5s=new A.e("Which of the following is an absolute contraindication to exercise testing?",B.Z2,1,"Absolute contraindications to exercise testing include: unstable angina, acute MI (within 2 days), uncontrolled symptomatic heart failure, active endocarditis/myocarditis/pericarditis, severe symptomatic aortic stenosis, and acute aortic dissection.")
B.OK=s(["Exaggerated heart rate response","Normal heart rate response","Blunted heart rate response","Irregular heart rate response"],t.s)
B.a3F=new A.e("During cardiac rehab exercise, patients on beta-blockers will demonstrate which of the following?",B.OK,2,"Beta-blockers blunt the heart rate response to exercise. This means heart rate alone cannot be used reliably to gauge exercise intensity. In these patients, RPE (Borg scale) and the talk test become more important measures of exercise intensity.")
B.TK=s(["Bedpans require more upper extremity effort","The Valsalva maneuver and isometric strain are greater with a bedpan","Bedpans increase heart rate more than walking","Commodes require longer time"],t.s)
B.a4L=new A.e("Why is using a bedpan considered more physiologically stressful than using a bedside commode in cardiac patients?",B.TK,1,"Using a bedpan requires more isometric straining and often triggers a Valsalva maneuver, significantly increasing myocardial oxygen demand. A bedside commode is physiologically safer because it allows more natural positioning and less straining.")
B.Sc=s([B.a35,B.a6X,B.a5s,B.a3F,B.a4L],t.b)
B.K7=new A.a6("Cardiac Rehab 4",B.Sc)
B.Xz=s(["Wrist in maximum flexion","Wrist 20-30\xb0 extension, MCPs 45-70\xb0 flexion, IPs slight flexion, thumb opposed","All joints in full extension","Only the thumb in opposition"],t.s)
B.abb=new A.e("A wrist-hand orthosis (WHO) in the resting hand position maintains:",B.Xz,1,"Resting hand splint (WHO): maintains the functional position \u2014 wrist 20-30\xb0 extension, MCPs 45-70\xb0 flexion, IP joints slightly flexed, thumb in palmar abduction and opposition. This prevents shortening of collateral ligaments and joint contractures. Used for upper motor neuron injuries (stroke, TBI), burns, and inflammatory conditions.")
B.Mh=s(["Complete hand paralysis","C6 tetraplegia \u2014 uses wrist extension to passively flex the fingers for grasp","Carpal tunnel syndrome","Trigger finger"],t.s)
B.aa_=new A.e("A tenodesis splint (wrist-driven flexor hinge orthosis) is designed for patients with:",B.Mh,1,"Tenodesis splint: mechanical linkage that converts active wrist extension (C6 \u2014 ECRL intact) into passive finger flexion (grasp) and wrist flexion into finger extension (release). This provides functional prehension for C6 tetraplegics who lack hand intrinsics. The mechanical principle mimics the natural tenodesis effect.")
B.Om=s(["Immobilize the wrist","Maintain the thumb in opposition to preserve the first web space","Control finger flexion","Stabilize the elbow"],t.s)
B.a4I=new A.e("An opponent splint (short opponens) is used to:",B.Om,1,"Short opponens splint: maintains thumb CMC joint in palmar abduction and opposition, preserving the first web space. Used for median nerve palsy (loss of thenar muscles), thumb CMC arthritis, and after tendon transfer. It positions the thumb for functional pinch without immobilizing the wrist or fingers.")
B.PX=s(["Dynamic is always more comfortable","Dynamic applies a low-load prolonged stretch through springs, rubber bands, or elastic components","Static is never used in rehabilitation","Dynamic is only for the lower extremity"],t.s)
B.ac_=new A.e("Dynamic splinting differs from static splinting in that:",B.PX,1,"Dynamic splints: apply continuous low-load force through elastic components (springs, rubber bands, outriggers) to gradually increase ROM. Best for joint stiffness with tissue remodeling potential. Static splints: rigid, maintain a fixed position (protect, immobilize, or prevent contracture). Serial static splinting: progressive position changes as ROM improves.")
B.YL=s(["Walking","Overhead reaching","Table-top self-feeding by supporting the arm against gravity","Trunk balance"],t.s)
B.a7Z=new A.e("In a C5 tetraplegic, a mobile arm support (balanced forearm orthosis) assists with:",B.YL,2,"Mobile arm support (MAS/balanced forearm orthosis): gravity-eliminated arm support mounted to the wheelchair. Allows C5 tetraplegics (intact deltoid and biceps but no triceps/hand function) to perform tabletop activities (feeding with adapted utensils, page turning) by eliminating gravity from the movement. Arm floats on a trough balanced on a pivot.")
B.WZ=s([B.abb,B.aa_,B.a4I,B.ac_,B.a7Z],t.b)
B.K8=new A.a6("Prosthetics & Orthotics 7",B.WZ)
B.Vz=s(["Above knee (transfemoral)","Below knee (transtibial)","Hip disarticulation","Syme amputation"],t.s)
B.a78=new A.e("The most common level of lower extremity amputation is:",B.Vz,1,"Transtibial (below-knee) amputation is the most common level. It preserves the knee joint, which dramatically improves prosthetic ambulation potential and reduces energy expenditure compared to transfemoral. The ideal residual limb length is middle to distal third of the tibia.")
B.Xt=s(["Trauma","Peripheral vascular disease/diabetes","Cancer","Congenital deficiency"],t.s)
B.a96=new A.e("The leading cause of lower extremity amputation in the US is:",B.Xt,1,"Peripheral vascular disease (PVD) and diabetes account for ~80% of all lower extremity amputations. The dysvascular population tends to be older with significant comorbidities (cardiac disease, renal disease), which affects rehabilitation potential. Trauma is the leading cause in younger patients.")
B.Uk=s(["Edema control","Protection from trauma","Immediate prosthetic fitting capability","Increased risk of contracture"],t.s)
B.a6z=new A.e("A rigid post-operative dressing after transtibial amputation provides all EXCEPT:",B.Uk,3,"Rigid dressings (immediate post-op prosthesis): control edema (compression), protect the residual limb from trauma, allow early weight-bearing, reduce phantom pain, and help prevent knee flexion contracture by maintaining extension. They REDUCE (not increase) contracture risk.")
B.UD=s(["10% increase","25% increase","40-65% increase","100% increase"],t.s)
B.act=new A.e("The energy cost of ambulation with a unilateral transfemoral prosthesis compared to normal is approximately:",B.UD,2,"Energy expenditure: unilateral transtibial = 10-25% increase. Unilateral transfemoral = 40-65% increase. Bilateral transtibial = 40-100% increase. Bilateral transfemoral = up to 280% increase. Higher amputation level = greater energy cost. Dysvascular patients often cannot meet the energy demands of transfemoral prosthetic ambulation.")
B.QU=s(["Socket design","Unopposed hip flexor pull (iliopsoas) with loss of hip extensors","Prosthetic malalignment","Phantom limb pain"],t.s)
B.a3a=new A.e("A flexion contracture of the hip after transfemoral amputation is primarily caused by:",B.QU,1,"Hip flexion contracture: the gluteus maximus insertion is partially lost with the amputation, creating an imbalance with the intact iliopsoas. Prolonged sitting (wheelchair) compounds this. Prevention: prone lying 20-30 min 2-3x/day, hip extension stretching, avoiding prolonged hip flexion. The prosthetic socket must accommodate any existing contracture.")
B.YH=s([B.a78,B.a96,B.a6z,B.act,B.a3a],t.b)
B.K9=new A.a6("Prosthetics & Orthotics 1",B.YH)
B.Mq=s(["Preventive","Restorative","Supportive","Palliative"],t.s)
B.aaJ=new A.e("According to Dietz classification, which type of cancer rehabilitation focuses on patients with known cancer and anticipated deficits from treatment?",B.Mq,0,"Dietz classification: Preventive = anticipating and preventing deficits before/during treatment. Restorative = returning to prior function after treatment. Supportive = maximizing function with ongoing disease. Palliative = comfort and function at end of life.")
B.O9=s(["Lung cancer","Colorectal cancer","Prostate cancer","Bladder cancer"],t.s)
B.a3G=new A.e("What is the most common cancer in men in the United States?",B.O9,2,"Prostate cancer is the most commonly diagnosed cancer in men. Lung cancer remains the leading cause of cancer death in both men and women. Breast cancer is the most common cancer in women.")
B.W5=s(["It resolves with adequate sleep","It is proportional to activity level","It is persistent and not relieved by rest","It only occurs during active treatment"],t.s)
B.a3n=new A.e("Cancer-related fatigue is the most common symptom reported by cancer patients. Which of the following best describes its characteristics?",B.W5,2,"Cancer-related fatigue is persistent, distressing, and not proportional to activity level or relieved by rest. It is the most common symptom in cancer patients and can persist long after treatment ends. Exercise is the best-studied intervention.")
B.Y0=s(["Alkylating agents","Platinum-based agents and taxanes","Antimetabolites","Hormonal agents"],t.s)
B.a6W=new A.e("Which chemotherapy class is most commonly associated with peripheral neuropathy?",B.Y0,1,"Platinum compounds (cisplatin, oxaliplatin) and taxanes (paclitaxel, docetaxel) are the most common causes of chemotherapy-induced peripheral neuropathy (CIPN). It typically presents as a distal, symmetric, sensory-predominant neuropathy in a stocking-glove distribution.")
B.NL=s(["The ipsilateral lower extremity","The contralateral upper extremity","The ipsilateral upper extremity","Both upper extremities"],t.s)
B.a3S=new A.e("Lymphedema following axillary lymph node dissection for breast cancer most commonly affects:",B.NL,2,"Lymphedema after axillary lymph node dissection affects the ipsilateral upper extremity. Risk increases with the number of nodes removed, radiation therapy, obesity, and infection. Complete decongestive therapy (CDT) is the gold standard treatment.")
B.W6=s([B.aaJ,B.a3G,B.a3n,B.a6W,B.a3S],t.b)
B.Ka=new A.a6("Cancer Rehab 1",B.W6)
B.Pa=s(["PLP is always worse","PLP is perceived in the missing limb; residual limb pain is at the amputation site","They are the same thing","PLP only occurs in upper extremity amputees"],t.s)
B.a5E=new A.e("Phantom limb pain (PLP) differs from residual limb pain in that:",B.Pa,1,"Phantom limb PAIN: painful sensations (burning, cramping, shooting) perceived in the MISSING portion of the limb. Phantom SENSATION (non-painful awareness of missing limb) is nearly universal. Residual limb pain: pain AT the stump (neuroma, bone spur, skin breakdown, infection). Treatment differs: PLP responds to mirror therapy, gabapentin, TCAs; residual limb pain needs local treatment.")
B.SV=s(["Distracting the patient",'Providing visual feedback of the intact limb that "tricks" the brain into perceiving normal movement of the missing limb',"Strengthening the residual limb","Reducing edema"],t.s)
B.a5O=new A.e("Mirror therapy for phantom limb pain works by:",B.SV,1,"Mirror therapy: the patient views the reflection of the intact limb moving, creating a visual illusion that the amputated limb is present and moving normally. This is thought to resolve the conflict between motor commands and absent proprioceptive feedback (cortical remapping), reducing phantom pain. Strong evidence supports its efficacy.")
B.X_=s(["It looks better cosmetically","It distributes forces evenly, prevents edema, and improves proprioception","It is cheaper to manufacture","It allows for growth in pediatric patients"],t.s)
B.a3v=new A.e("Total contact socket design is important in transtibial prosthetics because:",B.X_,1,"Total surface bearing (TSB) sockets distribute pressure evenly across the entire residual limb surface, preventing pressure concentration that causes skin breakdown. Benefits: edema control, improved proprioceptive feedback, better suspension, and more comfortable weight-bearing. Pressure-tolerant areas bear more load; pressure-sensitive areas are relieved.")
B.QC=s(["The distal tibia","The patellar tendon, medial tibial flare, and anterior compartment musculature","The fibular head","The hamstring tendons"],t.s)
B.a3N=new A.e("The patellar tendon-bearing (PTB) socket loads weight primarily on:",B.QC,1,"PTB socket: primary weight-bearing on the patellar tendon (pressure-tolerant), with additional load on the medial tibial flare and anteromedial tibial surface. Pressure-sensitive areas (fibular head, tibial crest, distal tibia) are relieved. The PTB design has been largely replaced by TSB sockets but remains a board exam staple.")
B.Sb=s(["The cosmetic cover","Alignment \u2014 positioning the socket anterior to the foot creates knee extension moment","A mechanical knee lock","Hip flexor strength only"],t.s)
B.abL=new A.e("Knee flexion during stance phase in a transtibial prosthesis is controlled by:",B.Sb,1,"Prosthetic alignment controls knee stability. The ground reaction force (GRF) vector relative to the knee axis determines flexion/extension moment. Anterior socket placement (or posterior foot placement) keeps GRF anterior to knee = extension moment (stable). Conversely, posterior GRF creates flexion moment (buckling risk).")
B.Tn=s([B.a5E,B.a5O,B.a3v,B.a3N,B.abL],t.b)
B.Kb=new A.a6("Prosthetics & Orthotics 2",B.Tn)
B.QS=s(["It causes joint destruction","Untreated, it can cause permanent blindness from ischemic optic neuropathy","It only causes headaches","It resolves spontaneously"],t.s)
B.a9c=new A.e("Giant cell arteritis (temporal arteritis) requires urgent treatment because:",B.QS,1,"GCA: vasculitis of medium/large arteries in patients >50. Symptoms: new-onset temporal headache, scalp tenderness, jaw claudication, elevated ESR (often >100). Vision loss from anterior ischemic optic neuropathy is the most feared complication \u2014 can be sudden and permanent. HIGH-DOSE prednisone (40-60 mg/day) must be started IMMEDIATELY if suspected, before biopsy results.")
B.TR=s(["Immediate total knee replacement","Weight loss, exercise (quadriceps strengthening), acetaminophen/topical NSAIDs, and bracing","Chronic opioid therapy","Bed rest"],t.s)
B.a5Y=new A.e("Osteoarthritis of the knee is best initially managed with:",B.TR,1,"OA knee first-line: non-pharmacologic (weight loss \u2014 every 1 lb lost = 4 lbs less knee load, exercise \u2014 especially quadriceps strengthening, activity modification) + pharmacologic (acetaminophen, topical NSAIDs, oral NSAIDs if needed). Intra-articular corticosteroid for flares. Total knee replacement reserved for refractory cases with significant functional limitation.")
B.SF=s(["Bone fractures","Synovitis weakening the MCP radial collateral ligaments and extensor tendons, allowing ulnar deviation of the fingers","Osteoarthritis","Trigger finger"],t.s)
B.a93=new A.e("Ulnar drift deformity in RA is caused by:",B.SF,1,'Ulnar drift: MCP joints deviate ulnarly due to chronic synovitis destroying radial collateral ligaments and allowing extensor tendons to sublux ulnarly. Wrist radial deviation (compensatory) is often seen with MCP ulnar drift ("zigzag" deformity). Joint protection: avoid activities that push fingers ulnarly (opening jars, turning doorknobs). Ulnar drift splints may help.')
B.Td=s(["Cures osteoarthritis","Provides modest pain relief for 6 months by supplementing synovial fluid lubrication and shock absorption","Is the first-line treatment","Works better than total knee replacement"],t.s)
B.a4C=new A.e("Viscosupplementation (hyaluronic acid injections) for knee OA:",B.Td,1,"Viscosupplementation: intra-articular hyaluronic acid injection series. Modest evidence for pain reduction, may last 3-6 months. Mechanism: supplements degraded synovial fluid, improves lubrication and shock absorption, may have anti-inflammatory effects. Not universally recommended (AAOS guidelines are inconclusive). Typically tried after NSAIDs/corticosteroid injections fail.")
B.QJ=s(["Pain reduction only","Achieving and maintaining remission or low disease activity, with treatment adjustments every 3-6 months","Complete immobilization","Avoiding all medications"],t.s)
B.a5N=new A.e('In RA, the "treat to target" strategy aims for:',B.QJ,1,'Treat to target: measure disease activity objectively (DAS28, CDAI, SDAI) every 1-3 months, adjusting therapy until target is reached (remission or low disease activity). If target not reached at 3-6 months, escalate treatment. This aggressive approach leads to better outcomes than traditional "step-up" therapy. Methotrexate first, then add biologic DMARDs if needed.')
B.Vl=s([B.a9c,B.a5Y,B.a93,B.a4C,B.a5N],t.b)
B.Kc=new A.a6("Rheumatology 6",B.Vl)
B.YE=s(["Constant muscle rigidity regardless of movement speed","Velocity-dependent increase in muscle tone with exaggerated stretch reflexes","Muscle weakness from nerve damage","Involuntary rhythmic muscle contractions"],t.s)
B.aag=new A.e("Spasticity is defined as:",B.YE,1,"Spasticity = velocity-dependent increase in tonic stretch reflexes (Lance definition). Faster passive movement produces greater resistance. This distinguishes it from rigidity (velocity-independent, as in Parkinson disease) and from contracture (fixed soft tissue shortening). It is an UMN phenomenon.")
B.ZG=s(["0 to 4","0 to 5","1 to 10","0 to 6"],t.s)
B.aaC=new A.e("The Modified Ashworth Scale (MAS) grades spasticity from:",B.ZG,0,"MAS: 0 = no increase in tone. 1 = slight catch and release at end range. 1+ = slight catch followed by minimal resistance through <50% of ROM. 2 = more marked increase through most ROM. 3 = considerable increase, passive movement difficult. 4 = rigid in flexion or extension.")
B.OU=s(["Only cortical pathways","Muscle spindle \u2192 Ia afferent \u2192 spinal cord \u2192 alpha motor neuron \u2192 muscle contraction","Golgi tendon organ \u2192 Ib afferent \u2192 cortex","Only peripheral nerve pathways"],t.s)
B.a5f=new A.e("The stretch reflex arc involved in spasticity includes:",B.OU,1,"The monosynaptic stretch reflex: muscle spindle detects stretch \u2192 Ia afferent fires \u2192 enters dorsal horn \u2192 synapses with alpha motor neuron \u2192 muscle contracts. In spasticity, this reflex is disinhibited due to loss of descending UMN inhibition (corticospinal, reticulospinal tracts). The result is exaggerated reflex responses.")
B.Wb=s(["Tizanidine","Dantrolene","Baclofen","Diazepam"],t.s)
B.ab2=new A.e("Which oral medication for spasticity acts at the GABA-B receptor in the spinal cord?",B.Wb,2,"Baclofen is a GABA-B receptor agonist that acts in the spinal cord to reduce spasticity. Tizanidine = alpha-2 adrenergic agonist (central). Dantrolene = direct muscle relaxant (peripheral, inhibits calcium release from sarcoplasmic reticulum). Diazepam = GABA-A agonist (central/sedating).")
B.MR=s(["In the brain","At the spinal cord","Directly on the muscle (peripheral mechanism)","At the neuromuscular junction"],t.s)
B.ab0=new A.e("Dantrolene sodium is unique among antispasticity medications because it acts:",B.MR,2,"Dantrolene acts directly on skeletal muscle by inhibiting calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, reducing the force of muscle contraction. It is the only antispasticity drug with a peripheral mechanism. Main risk: hepatotoxicity (monitor LFTs). Useful in patients who cannot tolerate central sedating effects.")
B.QX=s([B.aag,B.aaC,B.a5f,B.ab2,B.ab0],t.b)
B.Kd=new A.a6("Spasticity 1",B.QX)
